var e={level:`C1`,type:`true-false`,questions:[{id:`c1-tf-001`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says growth 'tells us little about well-being' and rich countries 'report high levels of stress'.`,passage:`The economist argued that economic growth, long considered the main measure of progress, tells us little about well-being. Countries with high incomes, she noted, often report high levels of stress, while some poorer nations show greater life satisfaction.`,statements:[{text:`The economist thinks growth is a perfect measure of progress.`,answer:!1},{text:`Rich countries can still have high stress levels.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-002`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says 'closed for renovation for three years' and 'Critics blamed the high ticket prices'.`,passage:`Although the museum had been closed for renovation for three years, its reopening drew far fewer visitors than expected. Critics blamed the high ticket prices, whereas the director insisted that the exhibition itself was the real attraction.`,statements:[{text:`The museum was closed for three years.`,answer:!0},{text:`Critics said the tickets were cheap.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-003`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says their metabolism 'all but stops' when dry and mentions 'several days in the vacuum of space'; one protein 'appears to shield their genetic material'; when active they are 'as vulnerable as many other small animals'.`,passage:`Tardigrades, microscopic animals rarely longer than a millimetre, have earned a reputation as the toughest creatures on Earth. Found in moss, ocean sediment and even on mountain tops, they can survive conditions that would kill almost any other living thing. When their surroundings dry out, they pull in their legs, lose nearly all the water in their bodies and enter a state in which their metabolism all but stops. In this form, they have withstood temperatures close to absolute zero, intense radiation and, in one famous experiment, several days in the vacuum of space. Only when water returns do they slowly revive, sometimes after many years. What makes this possible has long puzzled biologists. Recent research suggests that tardigrades produce unusual proteins which form a glass-like substance inside their cells, holding delicate structures in place until conditions improve. Another protein appears to shield their genetic material from damage. Scientists are now investigating whether such mechanisms could be put to practical use, for instance in storing vaccines without refrigeration. It should be stressed, however, that tardigrades are not immortal: in their active state, they are as vulnerable as many other small animals.`,statements:[{text:`Tardigrades can survive drying out by almost stopping their metabolism.`,answer:!0},{text:`Tardigrades have never been exposed to space.`,answer:!1},{text:`One of their proteins seems to protect their genetic material.`,answer:!0},{text:`Tardigrades are equally resistant whether they are active or dried out.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-004`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`About ten thousand died in the eruption but 'many more died in the famine'; 'almost nobody connected these disasters' with the eruption; snow fell 'in June'; scientists studied 'ice cores and historical weather records'.`,passage:`In April 1815, Mount Tambora, a volcano on an Indonesian island, erupted with a force that dwarfed anything recorded in modern history. Around ten thousand people in the surrounding region were killed by the eruption itself, and many more died in the famine that followed. Yet the effects were felt far beyond Indonesia. The volcano had thrown enormous quantities of ash and sulphur gas high into the atmosphere, where they spread across the globe and reflected part of the sun's light back into space. The following year, 1816, became known in Europe and North America as the 'year without a summer'. Snow fell in New England in June, crops failed across large parts of the continent, and food prices soared. In some regions, hunger drove thousands of people to leave their homes in search of work. At the time, almost nobody connected these disasters with a distant eruption; many blamed sunspots or divine punishment. It was not until the twentieth century that scientists, studying ice cores and historical weather records, were able to demonstrate the link convincingly. The episode is now often cited as evidence of how vulnerable food supplies are to sudden changes in climate.`,statements:[{text:`More people in the region died in the eruption itself than in the famine that followed.`,answer:!1},{text:`People at the time widely understood that the volcano had caused the bad weather.`,answer:!1},{text:`Snow fell in New England in the summer of 1816.`,answer:!0},{text:`Ice cores helped scientists prove the connection.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-005`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The rumbles are 'too low for humans to hear'; results with recordings 'have been mixed'; vibrations are detected 'through sensitive cells in their feet and trunks'; they recognise relatives 'even after years of separation'.`,passage:`Elephants are known for their trumpeting calls, but much of their communication takes place at frequencies too low for humans to hear. These rumbles, known as infrasound, can travel several kilometres through the air and, remarkably, through the ground as well. Researchers studying elephants in southern Africa have found that the animals appear to detect ground vibrations through sensitive cells in their feet and trunks. When an elephant suspects that a distant call is reaching it in this way, it may lean forward, lift one foot or press its trunk against the soil, as if listening more carefully. Such signals seem to serve many purposes: keeping scattered family groups in contact, warning others of danger and helping males and females find each other during the brief period when a female is ready to mate. Interestingly, elephants seem able to recognise the voices of individual relatives, even after years of separation. Conservationists hope that a better understanding of this hidden language will help them reduce conflict between elephants and farmers. Recordings of alarm calls, for example, are being tested as a humane way of keeping herds away from crops, although results so far have been mixed.`,statements:[{text:`Humans cannot hear some elephant calls.`,answer:!0},{text:`Playing alarm calls has proved completely successful at protecting crops.`,answer:!1},{text:`Elephants detect ground vibrations only with their ears.`,answer:!1},{text:`Elephants may recognise relatives they have not seen for a long time.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-006`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`Lind 'gave each pair a different remedy'; it was 'decades before' the navy acted; boiling and copper pipes 'destroyed much of its vitamin content'; vitamin C was 'only identified in the twentieth century'.`,passage:`Long before Europeans understood the cause of scurvy, the disease was killing sailors in enormous numbers. On long voyages, men developed bleeding gums, aching joints and wounds that refused to heal, and many died before reaching land. In 1747, a Scottish naval surgeon named James Lind carried out what is often described as one of the first controlled clinical trials. He divided twelve sick sailors into pairs and gave each pair a different remedy, including vinegar, seawater and cider. The two men who received oranges and lemons recovered so quickly that one of them was soon fit enough to help care for the others. Surprisingly, Lind himself did not fully grasp the importance of his result, and it was decades before the British navy made citrus juice a regular part of sailors' diet. Even then, confusion persisted: when limes replaced lemons, the juice proved less effective, partly because it was often boiled or stored in copper pipes, which destroyed much of its vitamin content. The true cause of scurvy, a lack of vitamin C, was only identified in the twentieth century, long after the disease had largely disappeared from the seas.`,statements:[{text:`Lind tested several remedies on different pairs of sailors.`,answer:!0},{text:`The navy introduced citrus juice immediately after Lind's experiment.`,answer:!1},{text:`Lime juice was sometimes less effective because of the way it was prepared or stored.`,answer:!0},{text:`Lind discovered that scurvy is caused by a lack of vitamin C.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-007`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`MPs 'hung curtains soaked in chemicals'; the sewers were 'much wider than the population at the time required'; action came from 'the sheer unpleasantness' for politicians, 'not concern for public health as such'; it was built on 'the mistaken belief' about smells.`,passage:`In the summer of 1858, London endured what became known as the Great Stink. For years, the city's waste had flowed largely untreated into the River Thames, and an unusually hot season turned the river into a vast, foul-smelling sewer. Members of Parliament, whose building stood on the riverbank, hung curtains soaked in chemicals over the windows in a desperate attempt to block out the smell. What finally pushed the government into action was not concern for public health as such but the sheer unpleasantness of the situation for the politicians themselves. Within weeks, legislation was passed, and the engineer Joseph Bazalgette was put in charge of a huge new sewer system. Over the following years, his workers built hundreds of kilometres of tunnels that carried waste away from the centre and released it further downstream. Crucially, Bazalgette made the main sewers much wider than the population at the time required, reasoning that the work would only ever be done once. This foresight meant that the system coped with the city's growth for well over a century. Ironically, it was built partly on the mistaken belief that disease was spread by bad smells rather than by contaminated water.`,statements:[{text:`Politicians tried to block the smell with treated curtains.`,answer:!0},{text:`Bazalgette built the sewers larger than were needed at the time.`,answer:!0},{text:`The government acted mainly out of concern for public health.`,answer:!1},{text:`The system was designed on the correct understanding of how disease spreads.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-008`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The artists were 'rejected by the official exhibition' and 'organised their own show'; 'Financial success came slowly'; a reviewer used the word 'as an insult' before they adopted it; paint in 'portable metal tubes' made outdoor work easier.`,passage:`Few artistic movements have been mocked as cruelly at birth as Impressionism. When a group of young painters, rejected by the official exhibition in Paris, organised their own show in 1874, many critics reacted with open contempt. One reviewer seized on the title of a painting of a harbour at sunrise and used the word 'impression' as an insult, suggesting that the artists had merely sketched their ideas rather than finished them. The label stuck, and the painters eventually adopted it themselves. What disturbed the critics was not only the loose, visible brushstrokes but also the subject matter. Instead of historical scenes and noble figures, these artists painted railway stations, crowded boulevards and ordinary people relaxing by the river. Many of them worked outdoors, trying to capture the changing effects of light at particular moments of the day, a practice made easier by the recent invention of paint sold in portable metal tubes. Financial success came slowly; several of the group struggled for years to sell their work. Today, of course, their paintings attract enormous crowds and sell for staggering sums, a reversal that has made critics wary of dismissing new art too quickly.`,statements:[{text:`The painters held their own exhibition after being rejected by the official one.`,answer:!0},{text:`The members of the group quickly became wealthy.`,answer:!1},{text:`The word 'impression' was first used by the artists to describe their own style.`,answer:!1},{text:`Portable paint tubes made it easier to paint outdoors.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-009`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`Microplastics have been detected 'even in human blood'; 'Not all of them come from discarded bottles and bags' (clothes and tyres too); the health effect 'remains far less clear'; filters 'could capture a large proportion of fibres'.`,passage:`Every year, millions of tonnes of plastic find their way into rivers and oceans, where sunlight, waves and friction gradually break them down into ever smaller fragments. Particles less than five millimetres across, known as microplastics, have now been detected almost everywhere scientists have looked: in deep-sea sediment, in Arctic ice, in table salt and drinking water, and even in human blood. Not all of them come from discarded bottles and bags. A considerable share is released when synthetic clothes are washed, and car tyres shed vast quantities of tiny particles as they wear down on the road. What remains far less clear is the effect of all this on human health. Laboratory studies have shown that very high doses can damage cells, but such doses are far greater than anything people are likely to encounter. Researchers stress that the absence of clear evidence of harm is not the same as evidence of safety, and many argue that it would be unwise to wait for certainty before acting. Some countries have already banned microbeads in cosmetics, while engineers are developing filters for washing machines that could capture a large proportion of fibres before they reach the sewers.`,statements:[{text:`Microplastics have been found in human blood.`,answer:!0},{text:`All microplastics come from bottles and bags thrown away by consumers.`,answer:!1},{text:`Scientists have proved that microplastics seriously harm human health.`,answer:!1},{text:`Filters for washing machines could reduce the number of fibres entering sewers.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-010`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The fold was devised by 'a Japanese astrophysicist'; it opens 'with a single pull on opposite corners'; folded tubes are inserted 'into blood vessels'; origami 'relies on geometry rather than on complicated mechanical parts'.`,passage:`When engineers began designing large solar panels for satellites, they faced an awkward problem: the panels had to be enormous once in orbit, yet small enough to fit inside a rocket. Surprisingly, part of the solution came from origami, the traditional Japanese art of paper folding. In the 1970s, a Japanese astrophysicist devised a fold that allows a flat sheet to be collapsed into a compact block and then opened again with a single pull on opposite corners. The pattern was later used to deploy solar arrays in space, and it has since been adapted for everything from maps that are easy to refold to medical devices. Researchers have, for instance, developed tiny folded tubes that can be inserted into blood vessels and then expanded to hold them open. Other teams are experimenting with folding techniques to build robots that assemble themselves when heated. What makes origami so attractive to engineers is that it relies on geometry rather than on complicated mechanical parts, which tend to fail. Had mathematicians not begun to study the rules of folding seriously in the late twentieth century, it is unlikely that the art would have found so many practical applications.`,statements:[{text:`The fold described in the text was invented by a Japanese astrophysicist.`,answer:!0},{text:`The fold can only be opened with several separate movements.`,answer:!1},{text:`Origami techniques have been used in medicine.`,answer:!0},{text:`Engineers like origami because it requires complex mechanical parts.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-011`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The settlement had bakeries and cattle bones, so labourers were 'relatively well fed'; healed fractures show 'injured workers received medical care'; workers were 'buried with bread and beer'; the text says 'This does not mean that the work was voluntary or easy'.`,passage:`For a long time, popular culture portrayed the builders of the Egyptian pyramids as slaves, driven by whips under the burning sun. Archaeological discoveries over the past few decades have painted a rather different picture. Near the pyramids at Giza, excavators uncovered the remains of a settlement where workers lived, complete with bakeries, breweries and large quantities of cattle bones, suggesting that the labourers were relatively well fed. Nearby cemeteries contained the tombs of workers buried with bread and beer for the afterlife, an honour that would hardly have been granted to slaves. Some skeletons even show signs of healed fractures and, in a few cases, amputations, indicating that injured workers received medical care. Most researchers now believe that the pyramids were built by a permanent core of skilled craftsmen, supported by rotating teams of farmers who worked for a few months at a time, possibly while the annual flooding of the Nile made their fields impossible to farm. This does not mean that the work was voluntary or easy; the labour was hard and the state demanded it. But the image of a vast army of chained slaves appears to owe more to films than to evidence.`,statements:[{text:`Archaeologists found evidence that the workers ate quite well.`,answer:!0},{text:`Some injured workers appear to have received medical treatment.`,answer:!0},{text:`Workers who died were buried without any goods.`,answer:!1},{text:`The text claims that the work was voluntary and easy.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-012`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The repair 'draws attention to' the damage rather than disguising it; the text says 'Whether or not the tale is true'; 'a single repair can take weeks'; some craftsmen 'are uneasy' that it is becoming 'a fashionable slogan'.`,passage:`In Japan, a broken bowl is not always thrown away. Through a technique known as kintsugi, craftsmen join the pieces with lacquer mixed with powdered gold, so that the cracks become shining lines running across the surface. Rather than disguising the damage, the repair draws attention to it, turning the history of the object into part of its beauty. According to one popular story, the practice began in the fifteenth century, when a powerful ruler sent a cracked tea bowl abroad to be mended and was disappointed when it came back held together with ugly metal staples. Whether or not the tale is true, kintsugi became closely linked with the tea ceremony, in which simple, imperfect objects were highly valued. The process is slow: the lacquer, made from the sap of a tree, must dry in a humid environment, and a single repair can take weeks to complete. In recent years, the idea has spread far beyond Japan. It is frequently used as a metaphor in self-help books, which encourage readers to accept their own flaws. Some traditional craftsmen, however, are uneasy about this trend, feeling that the technique is being reduced to a fashionable slogan.`,statements:[{text:`Kintsugi hides the cracks so that the repair cannot be seen.`,answer:!1},{text:`The story of its origin is definitely known to be true.`,answer:!1},{text:`A kintsugi repair can take several weeks.`,answer:!0},{text:`Some craftsmen dislike the way kintsugi is used in self-help books.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-013`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`Hunting 'for their fur' made them extinct in many countries; farmers 'have complained' about flooded fields; dams 'can reduce flooding in towns downstream'; during droughts the ponds 'store water'.`,passage:`Beavers were once common across much of Europe, but centuries of hunting for their fur and for a substance used in perfume left them extinct in many countries. Over the last few decades, however, they have been reintroduced to areas from which they had long vanished, and their return has proved both beneficial and controversial. Beavers are remarkable engineers. By felling trees and building dams across streams, they create ponds and wetlands that provide habitats for fish, insects, birds and amphibians. Their dams also slow the flow of water after heavy rain, which can reduce flooding in towns downstream, and they help to filter out soil and pollutants. During droughts, the ponds they create store water that would otherwise have drained away. Not everyone welcomes them, though. Farmers have complained that beaver dams flood valuable fields, and the animals sometimes damage trees that landowners would prefer to keep. In response, some regions have introduced management schemes that allow dams to be lowered or animals to be moved, rather than simply culled. Supporters argue that, given the costs of conventional flood defences, beavers provide their services remarkably cheaply.`,statements:[{text:`Beavers disappeared from some countries partly because they were hunted for their fur.`,answer:!0},{text:`Farmers have unanimously welcomed the return of beavers.`,answer:!1},{text:`Beaver dams make flooding worse in towns downstream.`,answer:!1},{text:`Ponds created by beavers can store water during dry periods.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-014`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The rock and permafrost 'keep the seeds cold even if the electricity fails'; each country 'retains ownership' and 'only it can request their return'; 'The first withdrawal came in 2015'; 'the seeds themselves were not affected'.`,passage:`Deep inside a mountain on a remote Arctic island lies a building that its designers hope will never be needed. The global seed vault, opened in 2008, stores duplicate samples of crop seeds from gene banks around the world, providing a backup in case the originals are lost through war, natural disaster or simple mismanagement. The location was chosen with care. The permafrost and thick rock keep the seeds cold even if the electricity fails, and the site is high enough above sea level to remain dry even if the polar ice melts. More than a million samples are now held there, including varieties of rice, wheat and beans that are no longer grown commercially. Each country that deposits seeds retains ownership of them, and only it can request their return. The first withdrawal came in 2015, when researchers whose gene bank had been damaged by conflict in the Middle East asked for their samples back in order to rebuild their collection. Despite its reputation, the vault is not invulnerable: in 2017, unusually warm weather caused meltwater to flood the entrance tunnel, although the seeds themselves were not affected. The incident led to costly improvements to the building.`,statements:[{text:`The vault is designed to keep seeds cold even without electricity.`,answer:!0},{text:`Any country can request seeds deposited by another country.`,answer:!1},{text:`The first withdrawal of seeds took place in 2015.`,answer:!0},{text:`The seeds were damaged when water flooded the entrance in 2017.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-015`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`Snow 'was not convinced' by the miasma theory; brewery workers 'had largely escaped the disease'; the outbreak 'was already declining'; officials 'remained reluctant to accept his explanation'.`,passage:`In 1854, a severe outbreak of cholera struck the Soho district of London, killing hundreds of people in little more than a week. At the time, most doctors believed that the disease spread through 'miasma', a poisonous vapour rising from rotting matter. A physician named John Snow was not convinced. Having studied earlier outbreaks, he suspected that cholera was carried in contaminated water. To test his theory, he marked each death on a street map and noticed that the cases were clustered around a single public water pump on Broad Street. He also discovered telling exceptions: workers at a nearby brewery, who drank mainly beer, had largely escaped the disease, while a woman who lived some distance away but had water from the pump delivered to her home had died. Snow persuaded the local authorities to remove the pump's handle, and the outbreak, which was already declining, came to an end. Even so, officials remained reluctant to accept his explanation, partly because it implied that the city's water supply was dangerous. Only years after his death was Snow widely recognised as one of the founders of modern epidemiology, and his map is still reproduced in textbooks today.`,statements:[{text:`Snow agreed with most doctors that cholera spread through bad air.`,answer:!1},{text:`Brewery workers were mostly unaffected by the outbreak.`,answer:!0},{text:`The outbreak had already begun to decline when the pump handle was removed.`,answer:!0},{text:`Officials immediately accepted Snow's explanation.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-016`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`Energy is released 'in the form of light rather than heat'; 'blue light travels furthest'; animals glowing on their undersides become 'almost invisible to hunters looking up'; red light lets predators illuminate prey 'without being noticed'.`,passage:`Thousands of metres below the surface of the ocean, where sunlight never reaches, many creatures produce their own light. This phenomenon, known as bioluminescence, results from a chemical reaction in which a molecule releases energy in the form of light rather than heat. Scientists estimate that the great majority of animals living in the open ocean at these depths are capable of glowing in some way. The light serves a remarkable variety of purposes. Some fish dangle a glowing lure in front of their mouths to attract prey, while certain shrimp release a cloud of luminous liquid to confuse predators, much as an octopus might squirt ink in shallower water. Other animals use light for camouflage: by glowing faintly on their undersides, they match the dim light filtering down from above and become almost invisible to hunters looking up at them. Most deep-sea light is blue, since blue light travels furthest through seawater, and many animals cannot see red at all. A few predators have taken advantage of this by producing red light, which allows them to illuminate their prey without being noticed. Researchers are still discovering new forms of bioluminescence, and many of its chemical secrets remain unexplained.`,statements:[{text:`In bioluminescence, energy is released mainly as heat.`,answer:!1},{text:`Red light travels further through seawater than blue light.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some animals glow on their undersides to avoid being seen from below.`,answer:!0},{text:`Some predators use red light because their prey cannot see it.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-017`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`They were found by 'farmers digging a well'; soil tests 'detected unusually high levels' of mercury; bodies came from 'standard moulds' with details 'added by hand'; the paint 'flaked off within minutes'.`,passage:`The famous army of terracotta warriors was discovered in 1974, when farmers digging a well in northern China struck fragments of a clay figure. Excavations soon revealed thousands of life-size soldiers, along with horses, chariots and weapons, buried in pits near the tomb of the first emperor to unify China, who died in 210 BC. What astonishes visitors most is that no two faces appear to be exactly alike. Researchers believe that the figures were produced in workshops using a system of standard moulds for the bodies, to which individual heads, hands and details were then added by hand. Names stamped on some pieces suggest that workshops were held responsible for the quality of their output. Originally, the warriors were brightly painted, but much of the paint flaked off within minutes of being exposed to air, a loss that has made archaeologists cautious. Indeed, the emperor's burial mound itself has never been opened. Ancient historians claimed that it contained rivers of mercury, and soil tests have since detected unusually high levels of the metal. Rather than risk damaging whatever lies inside, the authorities have decided to wait until better techniques are available.`,statements:[{text:`The warriors were found by archaeologists searching for the tomb.`,answer:!1},{text:`Tests have found high levels of mercury in the soil around the mound.`,answer:!0},{text:`The figures were made using a combination of moulds and handwork.`,answer:!0},{text:`The original paint has survived well since the excavation.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-018`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`'numerous theories have been put forward'; players wore goggles 'that prevented them from seeing which was which'; most 'were unable to tell the difference reliably'; findings 'have been fiercely disputed by some dealers'.`,passage:`Violins made by Antonio Stradivari in the late seventeenth and early eighteenth centuries are among the most valuable musical instruments in the world, and many players insist that their sound is unequalled. Over the years, numerous theories have been put forward to explain their supposed superiority: the special varnish, chemical treatments of the wood, or the unusually dense timber produced during a period of cold weather in Europe. Yet the question of whether these instruments actually sound better has proved surprisingly hard to answer. In a series of carefully controlled experiments, professional violinists were asked to play both old Italian instruments and high-quality modern ones while wearing goggles that prevented them from seeing which was which. The results were striking: most players were unable to tell the difference reliably, and many actually preferred the new violins. Audiences in concert halls showed similar preferences. Not surprisingly, these findings have been fiercely disputed by some dealers and musicians, who argue that the qualities of a great instrument only reveal themselves over long periods of playing. Whatever the truth, it seems that the reputation of a violin, as well as its price, can strongly influence how its sound is perceived.`,statements:[{text:`Only one theory has been proposed to explain the quality of these violins.`,answer:!1},{text:`In the experiments, the players could not see which violin they were playing.`,answer:!0},{text:`Most players reliably identified the old Italian violins.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some dealers rejected the results of the experiments.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-019`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`'more than a third of humanity can no longer see the Milky Way'; astronomers 'were among the first to complain'; light is 'one of the factors' in insect decline; it 'could be reduced almost instantly' and 'at little cost'.`,passage:`For most of human history, night was genuinely dark. Today, however, artificial lighting has transformed the night sky over much of the planet, and more than a third of humanity can no longer see the Milky Way from where they live. Astronomers were among the first to complain, but biologists are increasingly concerned about the effects of light pollution on wildlife. Insects are particularly vulnerable. Many species navigate by keeping natural light sources at a constant angle, and when confronted with a street lamp, they circle it endlessly until they die of exhaustion or are eaten. Some researchers suspect that artificial light is one of the factors contributing to the alarming decline in insect populations reported in several countries. Other creatures are affected too. Newly hatched sea turtles, which instinctively head towards the brightest horizon, can be lured inland by coastal lights instead of making for the sea. Unlike many forms of pollution, though, this one could be reduced almost instantly. Simple measures such as shielding lamps so that they point downwards, switching them off when they are not needed, or using warmer colours that attract fewer insects can make a considerable difference at little cost.`,statements:[{text:`More than a third of people cannot see the Milky Way from their homes.`,answer:!0},{text:`Astronomers were the last group to complain about light pollution.`,answer:!1},{text:`Artificial light is described as the only cause of insect decline.`,answer:!1},{text:`Light pollution could be reduced quickly and cheaply.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-020`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`'French troops rebuilding a fort' found it; it recorded 'the same official decree in three scripts'; Champollion showed the system was 'a mixture' with some signs standing for sounds; 'Egypt has repeatedly called for its return'.`,passage:`When Napoleon's army invaded Egypt in 1798, it brought along not only soldiers but also a team of scholars. A year later, French troops rebuilding a fort near the town of Rosetta came across a slab of dark stone covered in writing. The inscription, it turned out, recorded the same official decree in three scripts: Egyptian hieroglyphs, a later Egyptian script, and ancient Greek. Since Greek could still be read, scholars realised that the stone might provide the key to hieroglyphs, which nobody had understood for well over a thousand years. Deciphering them nonetheless took more than two decades. Many scholars had assumed that hieroglyphs were purely symbolic, each picture representing an idea. The French linguist Jean-François Champollion, who had studied numerous ancient languages, showed that the system was in fact a mixture: some signs stood for sounds, while others represented whole words or meanings. His knowledge of Coptic, a later form of the Egyptian language, proved crucial. After the French were defeated, the stone was handed over to the British and has been displayed in London ever since, although Egypt has repeatedly called for its return.`,statements:[{text:`The stone was discovered by French soldiers.`,answer:!0},{text:`The three inscriptions on the stone recorded three different decrees.`,answer:!1},{text:`Champollion proved that all hieroglyphs represented ideas rather than sounds.`,answer:!1},{text:`Egypt has asked for the stone to be returned.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-021`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`Models let surgeons 'rehearse difficult procedures'; implants 'can be shaped to fit an individual perfectly, rather than adjusted from standard sizes'; a printed kidney 'remains a distant prospect'; 'it is not always clear who should be held responsible'.`,passage:`Three-dimensional printing, once associated mainly with plastic prototypes and novelty gadgets, is increasingly finding its way into hospitals. Surgeons now routinely use printed models of a patient's organs, based on medical scans, to plan complex operations. Being able to hold an exact replica of a damaged heart or a tumour in their hands allows them to rehearse difficult procedures and, in some cases, to shorten the time a patient spends under anaesthetic. Printed implants are also becoming more common. Titanium replacements for sections of the skull or jaw can be shaped to fit an individual perfectly, rather than adjusted from standard sizes during surgery. Perhaps the most ambitious goal is the printing of living tissue. Using 'inks' made of cells, researchers have produced small pieces of skin, cartilage and blood vessels. Printing an entire functioning organ such as a kidney, however, remains a distant prospect, because such organs contain an intricate network of tiny vessels that scientists cannot yet reproduce. There are also unresolved questions about regulation: when a hospital prints a device for a single patient, it is not always clear who should be held responsible if something goes wrong.`,statements:[{text:`Printed models can help surgeons prepare for operations.`,answer:!0},{text:`Printed skull implants must always be adjusted from standard sizes.`,answer:!1},{text:`Researchers have already printed complete working kidneys.`,answer:!1},{text:`Responsibility for faults in printed devices is not always clear.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-022`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`Some species filter salt 'through their roots, while others' use 'their leaves'; they store 'far more per hectare than many inland forests'; roots break 'the force of waves and storm surges'; experts say restoring water flow is 'usually more effective'.`,passage:`Along tropical coastlines, mangrove forests occupy the narrow zone where land meets sea. Their trees have adapted to conditions that would kill most plants: salty water, soft mud that contains little oxygen, and tides that flood their roots twice a day. Some species filter salt out through their roots, while others get rid of it through their leaves. Many have roots that grow upwards out of the mud, allowing them to breathe. For coastal communities, mangroves are extraordinarily valuable. Their dense tangle of roots breaks the force of waves and storm surges, protecting villages that lie behind them, and they serve as nurseries for many of the fish that local people depend on. They also store huge amounts of carbon in their soils, far more per hectare than many inland forests. Despite this, mangroves have been cleared at an alarming rate, often to make way for shrimp farms, hotels and housing. In recent years, restoration projects have attempted to replant them, with mixed results. Where seedlings have simply been planted in unsuitable places, many have died. Experts now argue that it is usually more effective to restore the natural flow of water and allow mangroves to return on their own.`,statements:[{text:`All mangrove species remove salt in exactly the same way.`,answer:!1},{text:`Mangrove soils store less carbon per hectare than most inland forests.`,answer:!1},{text:`Mangroves can reduce the damage caused by storm surges.`,answer:!0},{text:`Experts think restoring natural water flow often works better than planting seedlings.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-023`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`Messages travelled 'no faster than the horse, ship or train'; 'Not everyone was impressed'; the 1866 cable came 'after several earlier attempts had failed'; companies 'charged by the word'.`,passage:`Long before smartphones, a single invention shrank the world more dramatically than any other. Until the 1840s, a message could travel no faster than the horse, ship or train that carried it. The electric telegraph changed that almost overnight. By sending pulses of current along a wire, operators could transmit messages across great distances in a matter of minutes, using a code of short and long signals. Within a few decades, telegraph lines stretched across continents, and in 1866 a cable laid across the floor of the Atlantic finally linked Europe and North America after several earlier attempts had failed. The consequences were profound. Newspapers could report events from distant countries on the day they happened, governments could communicate instantly with their representatives abroad, and traders could compare prices in different markets. Not everyone was impressed. Some commentators complained that the flood of brief, unconfirmed reports encouraged superficial thinking, while others worried that private messages could be read by operators. Telegraph companies also charged by the word, which led customers to develop a famously abbreviated style of writing. In many respects, these debates closely resemble those surrounding the digital technologies of our own time.`,statements:[{text:`Before the telegraph, messages could not travel faster than physical transport.`,answer:!0},{text:`Everyone welcomed the effects of the telegraph.`,answer:!1},{text:`The first attempt to lay a cable across the Atlantic was successful.`,answer:!1},{text:`Customers wrote short messages partly because they paid for each word.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-024`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`Thousands of snails were needed, so the dye was 'a symbol of wealth'; Perkin was trying to produce 'an artificial version of quinine'; he 'left his studies' and 'opened a factory'; the industry produced 'medicines, perfumes and explosives'.`,passage:`Dyes were among the most precious commodities of the ancient world. The deep purple prized by Roman emperors, for instance, was extracted from sea snails, and thousands of them were needed to colour a single garment, making it a symbol of wealth and power. For centuries, all dyes came from plants, animals or minerals, and many colours remained expensive or faded quickly. This changed in 1856, when an eighteen-year-old English chemistry student named William Perkin was attempting to produce an artificial version of quinine, a medicine used against malaria. His experiment failed, but while cleaning his equipment he noticed that the dark residue produced a brilliant purple colour when dissolved in alcohol. Realising its commercial potential, Perkin left his studies, patented the substance and opened a factory to manufacture it. The new colour, known as mauve, became extremely fashionable, and its success inspired chemists across Europe to search for other synthetic dyes. The industry that grew from these efforts went on to produce not only colours but also medicines, perfumes and explosives. Perkin's accidental discovery is therefore often regarded as a turning point in the history of the chemical industry.`,statements:[{text:`Purple dye from sea snails was cheap to produce.`,answer:!1},{text:`Perkin was trying to make an artificial medicine when he discovered mauve.`,answer:!0},{text:`Perkin continued his studies and did not pursue the commercial side of his discovery.`,answer:!1},{text:`The synthetic dye industry eventually produced medicines as well.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-025`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`It was found by 'four teenagers'; some signs have a meaning that 'remains unknown'; visitors brought carbon dioxide and humidity that encouraged fungi; since 1963 'only a handful of scientists have been allowed inside'.`,passage:`In 1940, four teenagers exploring the countryside of south-west France followed their dog into a narrow opening in the ground and found themselves in a cave whose walls were covered with paintings of animals. The images at Lascaux, created around seventeen thousand years ago, include horses, deer, bulls and a few mysterious signs whose meaning remains unknown. What struck the first visitors was the skill of the artists, who used the natural curves of the rock to give their animals a sense of movement and depth. Pigments were made from minerals such as iron oxide and charcoal, and some were probably blown onto the walls through hollow bones. The cave was opened to the public after the war, and soon more than a thousand people were visiting it every day. Unfortunately, the carbon dioxide and humidity they brought with them encouraged the growth of fungi and algae on the paintings, and in 1963 the cave was closed to protect them. Since then, only a handful of scientists have been allowed inside. Visitors now see detailed replicas instead, and while some regret the loss of the original experience, most accept that preservation must come first.`,statements:[{text:`The cave was discovered by a group of archaeologists.`,answer:!1},{text:`The meaning of some signs in the cave is still a mystery.`,answer:!0},{text:`The paintings were damaged partly because of the large number of visitors.`,answer:!0},{text:`Ordinary tourists can still visit the original cave today.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-026`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`Residents begin to sing, 'often remembering every word'; music can 'lessen the need for sedative drugs'; musical memory areas are damaged 'later in the disease'; there is 'no convincing evidence that it slows the progress of the disease'.`,passage:`For people living with dementia, music can sometimes achieve what conversation cannot. Care workers frequently report that residents who rarely speak, or who seem unaware of their surroundings, will suddenly begin to sing along when they hear a song from their youth, often remembering every word. Researchers believe this happens partly because musical memories are stored in areas of the brain that tend to be damaged later in the disease than those responsible for other kinds of memory. In recent years, programmes offering personalised playlists to people with dementia have spread across care homes in several countries. Studies suggest that listening to familiar music can reduce agitation and anxiety, and in some cases lessen the need for sedative drugs. Group singing sessions appear to bring additional benefits, encouraging social contact and giving carers a different way of connecting with the people they look after. Scientists are careful, though, not to exaggerate what music can do. There is no convincing evidence that it slows the progress of the disease itself, and its effects often fade soon after the music stops. Nevertheless, given its low cost and lack of side effects, many experts believe it deserves a far more central place in care.`,statements:[{text:`Some people with dementia can remember the words of old songs.`,answer:!0},{text:`Familiar music may reduce the need for some drugs.`,answer:!0},{text:`Musical memories are usually the first to be lost in dementia.`,answer:!1},{text:`Research shows that music slows the progress of dementia.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-027`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`In variolation 'healthy people were deliberately infected'; Jenner was intrigued by 'a widespread belief among country people'; 'the child remained healthy'; smallpox was declared eradicated 'in 1980'.`,passage:`Towards the end of the eighteenth century, smallpox was one of the most feared diseases in the world, killing a large proportion of those it infected and leaving many survivors scarred or blind. People had long noticed that those who recovered never caught it again, and in parts of Asia and Africa a risky practice called variolation was used: healthy people were deliberately infected with material from a mild case, in the hope of developing a less dangerous form of the illness. The English doctor Edward Jenner was intrigued by a widespread belief among country people that milkmaids who had caught cowpox, a mild disease of cattle, were protected against smallpox. In 1796, he took material from a cowpox sore on a milkmaid's hand and introduced it into a small cut on the arm of a young boy. Weeks later, he exposed the boy to smallpox, and the child remained healthy. Such an experiment would be considered completely unethical today. Jenner's method, which he called vaccination after the Latin word for cow, spread rapidly despite fierce opposition from some critics. Nearly two centuries later, in 1980, smallpox became the first human disease to be officially declared eradicated.`,statements:[{text:`Variolation involved deliberately infecting healthy people.`,answer:!0},{text:`Jenner's idea was partly inspired by a belief held by country people.`,answer:!0},{text:`The boy in Jenner's experiment caught smallpox.`,answer:!1},{text:`Smallpox was declared eradicated in 1980.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-028`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`Plants are 'grown without soil'; staple crops 'would be far too expensive to produce this way'; they use 'a fraction of the water'; companies went bankrupt because of 'the enormous cost of the electricity'.`,passage:`Vertical farms, in which crops are grown indoors on shelves stacked from floor to ceiling, have been promoted as a solution to many of the problems facing modern agriculture. Plants are grown without soil, their roots fed by water containing carefully measured nutrients, while coloured lights replace the sun. Because conditions are completely controlled, there is no need for pesticides, and crops can be harvested all year round regardless of the weather. Supporters point out that such farms use a fraction of the water required by conventional agriculture and can be built in the middle of cities, reducing the distance food has to travel. Yet the industry has had a turbulent few years. Several well-funded companies have gone bankrupt, largely because of the enormous cost of the electricity needed to power the lights and the climate control systems. When energy prices rose sharply, many found that their business model simply did not work. Critics also note that vertical farms are suited mainly to leafy greens and herbs, which grow quickly and have a high value; staple crops such as wheat or rice would be far too expensive to produce this way. Most analysts now see vertical farming as a useful supplement to traditional agriculture rather than a replacement.`,statements:[{text:`Vertical farms grow plants without soil.`,answer:!0},{text:`Vertical farming is well suited to producing wheat and rice cheaply.`,answer:!1},{text:`Vertical farms need more water than conventional farms.`,answer:!1},{text:`High electricity costs have caused some companies to fail.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-029`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`It had been open 'for only four months'; workers 'had noticed that the deck moved'; 'the only life lost was that of a dog'; the deck was thin 'in order to save money'.`,passage:`On a windy morning in November 1940, a newly opened suspension bridge in the north-western United States began to twist violently and then collapsed into the water below. The Tacoma Narrows Bridge had been open for only four months. Even during construction, workers had noticed that the deck moved up and down in moderate winds, earning the bridge the nickname 'Galloping Gertie', and some drivers had crossed it just for the thrill. On the day of the collapse, the movement changed from a gentle wave into a dramatic twisting motion. Fortunately, the only life lost was that of a dog that had been left in an abandoned car. The disaster was filmed, and the footage has been shown to engineering students ever since. Its cause is often described as resonance, but engineers today generally explain it differently: the wind interacted with the shape of the deck in a way that made the twisting grow larger and larger, a process known as aeroelastic flutter. The bridge's designer had made the deck unusually thin and narrow in order to save money and achieve an elegant appearance. The lessons learned led to a new emphasis on testing bridge designs with scale models in wind tunnels.`,statements:[{text:`The bridge collapsed less than a year after it opened.`,answer:!0},{text:`Nobody had noticed any movement of the bridge before the collapse.`,answer:!1},{text:`Several people were killed in the collapse.`,answer:!1},{text:`The deck was made thin partly to reduce costs.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-030`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`It is 'impossible to represent its surface on paper without distorting something'; the projection 'was intended for sailors'; Greenland appears 'roughly as large as Africa', i.e. too large; accurate-area projections 'inevitably distort shapes instead'.`,passage:`Every flat map of the world is, in some sense, a compromise. Because the Earth is roughly a sphere, it is impossible to represent its surface on paper without distorting something: shapes, areas, distances or directions. The projection designed by the Flemish mapmaker Gerardus Mercator in 1569 was intended for sailors. Its great advantage was that a line of constant compass direction appeared on the map as a straight line, making it far easier to plot a course across the ocean. The price of this convenience was a dramatic distortion of size towards the poles. On a Mercator map, Greenland appears roughly as large as Africa, although Africa is in reality about fourteen times bigger. For centuries, this did not matter greatly, since the map was used mainly for navigation. However, as it became the standard image of the world in schools and offices, some critics argued that it gave a misleading impression, making Europe and North America look larger and more important than they really are. Several alternative projections have been proposed that show areas more accurately, although they inevitably distort shapes instead. Many online map services still use a version of Mercator's projection, because it preserves the shapes of streets and buildings when users zoom in.`,statements:[{text:`It is possible to make a flat map with no distortion at all.`,answer:!1},{text:`Mercator's map was originally designed to help sailors.`,answer:!0},{text:`On a Mercator map, Greenland looks much smaller than it really is.`,answer:!1},{text:`Alternative projections that show areas accurately also have weaknesses.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-031`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The rise is 'too rapid to be explained by changes in our genes'; farm children have 'notably low rates of allergy'; the researcher studied 'children from large families'; 'good hygiene remains essential'.`,passage:`Allergies and asthma have become far more common in wealthy countries over the past few decades, a rise too rapid to be explained by changes in our genes. In the late 1980s, a British researcher noticed that children from large families were less likely to suffer from hay fever, and suggested that early exposure to infections from older siblings might help to train the immune system. This idea, which became known as the hygiene hypothesis, was widely misunderstood as implying that people should stop washing their hands or cleaning their homes. Most scientists now prefer a somewhat different version. According to this view, what matters is not exposure to dangerous germs but contact with the wide variety of harmless microbes that humans encountered throughout their evolution, in soil, animals and other people. Children who grow up on traditional farms, surrounded by livestock, have notably low rates of allergy, which supports this interpretation. Modern urban life, with its limited contact with nature, frequent use of antibiotics and diets low in fibre, may have deprived our immune systems of these 'old friends'. Researchers stress, however, that good hygiene remains essential for preventing serious infectious diseases, and nobody is suggesting that we abandon it.`,statements:[{text:`Changes in human genes fully explain the rise in allergies.`,answer:!1},{text:`Children raised on traditional farms tend to have high rates of allergy.`,answer:!1},{text:`The original observation concerned children from large families.`,answer:!0},{text:`Researchers still consider good hygiene important.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-032`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`It was made 'from lime and volcanic ash'; it 'was considerably weaker than its modern equivalent'; seawater 'strengthened the concrete over time'; the lumps gave it 'the ability to repair itself'.`,passage:`Many Roman buildings and harbours have survived for two thousand years, while modern concrete structures sometimes begin to crumble within decades. This contrast has long fascinated engineers, and recent research has begun to explain it. Roman concrete was made from lime and volcanic ash, rather than the cement used today. In sea walls, seawater seeping through the material reacted with the ash to form rare minerals that actually strengthened the concrete over time, instead of weakening it. Another discovery concerns small white lumps of lime that are found throughout Roman concrete and were previously dismissed as signs of careless mixing. Scientists now believe that these lumps were the result of a hot mixing process and that they gave the material the ability to repair itself. When cracks formed and water entered, the lime dissolved and then hardened again, sealing the gaps. Engineers are now experimenting with similar techniques, hoping to produce longer-lasting concrete. That said, Roman concrete was considerably weaker than its modern equivalent and could not be used for skyscrapers or long bridges. Moreover, the buildings that survive are, by definition, the successful ones; countless others have long since collapsed.`,statements:[{text:`Roman concrete was made using volcanic ash.`,answer:!0},{text:`Roman concrete was stronger than modern concrete.`,answer:!1},{text:`Seawater weakened the concrete in Roman sea walls.`,answer:!1},{text:`The white lumps in Roman concrete are now thought to have been useful.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-033`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`Many objects are 'still there, long after they stopped working'; 'a fragment the size of a coin can seriously damage a spacecraft'; the station 'regularly has to change its position'; 'no piece of large debris has been actively removed'.`,passage:`Since the first satellite was launched in 1957, humans have sent thousands of objects into orbit around the Earth. Many of them are still there, long after they stopped working. Together with spent rocket stages and fragments from collisions and explosions, they form a growing cloud of space debris. Tens of thousands of pieces larger than a tennis ball are currently tracked, and there are believed to be many millions of smaller ones. Because they travel at speeds of several kilometres per second, even a fragment the size of a coin can seriously damage a spacecraft. The International Space Station regularly has to change its position to avoid passing objects. Scientists have warned of a possible chain reaction, in which collisions produce more fragments, which in turn cause further collisions, until certain orbits become too dangerous to use. The rapid increase in the number of satellites launched by private companies has made the issue more urgent. Several solutions are being explored, from nets and harpoons designed to capture old satellites to rules requiring operators to bring their satellites down within a few years of the end of their missions. So far, however, no piece of large debris has been actively removed from orbit.`,statements:[{text:`All satellites are removed from orbit when they stop working.`,answer:!1},{text:`Even a very small fragment can damage a spacecraft.`,answer:!0},{text:`The International Space Station sometimes moves to avoid debris.`,answer:!0},{text:`Large pieces of debris have already been removed using nets and harpoons.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-034`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`'the last book he published was devoted to' earthworms; he 'concluded that they were deaf'; worms 'gradually bury objects', which explains why ruins are below the surface; 'the book sold remarkably well'.`,passage:`Charles Darwin is best known for his theory of evolution, but the last book he published was devoted to a far humbler subject: earthworms. Darwin had been observing them for over forty years, and in his garden he conducted patient experiments to discover how they lived. He played musical instruments to them and concluded that they were deaf, although they responded strongly to vibrations when their pots were placed on a piano. He also offered them leaves of different shapes to see how they dragged them into their burrows, and suggested that they showed a degree of intelligence. His most important conclusion, however, concerned their effect on the landscape. By swallowing soil and depositing it on the surface, worms gradually bury objects lying on the ground, which explains why ancient ruins are often found below the surface. Darwin calculated that worms brought enormous quantities of earth to the surface each year, constantly mixing and enriching the soil. Many readers were amused that a famous scientist should spend his time on such creatures, yet the book sold remarkably well. Modern soil scientists acknowledge that Darwin was among the first to appreciate how vital earthworms are to fertile land.`,statements:[{text:`Darwin's last book was about earthworms.`,answer:!0},{text:`Darwin concluded that worms had excellent hearing.`,answer:!1},{text:`According to Darwin, worms help to explain why ancient ruins are often underground.`,answer:!0},{text:`The book sold very poorly.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-035`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`Devices measure heart rate 'accurately when the wearer is at rest'; calorie estimates 'can be wildly inaccurate'; most infer sleep from movement 'rather than measuring brain activity directly'; long-term benefits 'remain an open question'.`,passage:`Wearable devices that count our steps, measure our heart rate and analyse our sleep have become hugely popular. Their appeal is easy to understand: they turn health into a set of numbers that can be tracked, compared and improved. But how reliable are these numbers? Independent studies suggest that most devices count steps reasonably well and measure heart rate accurately when the wearer is at rest. Their estimates of calories burned, however, can be wildly inaccurate, sometimes differing from laboratory measurements by a large margin. Sleep tracking is also less precise than many users assume, since most devices infer sleep from movement and heart rate rather than measuring brain activity directly. Accuracy is not the only concern. Some psychologists have observed that a minority of users become anxious about their data, worrying excessively when their sleep score is low, for example. Others question what happens to the vast amounts of personal information collected by the companies that make the devices. On the positive side, there is evidence that tracking activity can encourage some people to exercise more, at least in the short term. Whether these benefits last once the novelty has worn off remains an open question.`,statements:[{text:`Most devices measure resting heart rate accurately.`,answer:!0},{text:`Calorie estimates are the most accurate feature of these devices.`,answer:!1},{text:`Most sleep trackers measure brain activity directly.`,answer:!1},{text:`It is unclear whether the benefits of tracking last in the long term.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-036`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`Its origins 'lie in the courts of Renaissance Italy'; performers 'were mostly courtiers'; 'he founded a royal academy of dance'; dancing on the toes became central 'only in the nineteenth century'.`,passage:`Although ballet is often associated with Russia and France, its origins lie in the courts of Renaissance Italy, where elaborate entertainments combining music, poetry and dance were staged to celebrate weddings and other important occasions. When an Italian noblewoman married into the French royal family in the sixteenth century, she brought these traditions with her, and they flourished at the French court. Early ballets were very different from those we see today. The performers were mostly courtiers rather than professional dancers, the costumes were heavy, and the steps were relatively simple. King Louis XIV, who was himself a passionate dancer, played a decisive role in the art's development. In 1661, he founded a royal academy of dance, and many of the basic positions and terms still used by dancers around the world date from this period, which is why ballet vocabulary is largely French. Women were initially excluded from the professional stage, and male dancers performed female roles. It was only in the nineteenth century that dancing on the tips of the toes became a central feature of female technique, helping to create the image of the weightless ballerina that dominates the popular imagination today.`,statements:[{text:`Ballet originated in Russia.`,answer:!1},{text:`Early ballet performers were mainly members of the court.`,answer:!0},{text:`Louis XIV founded an academy of dance.`,answer:!0},{text:`Dancing on the tips of the toes was common from the very beginning of ballet.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-037`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`Pandas 'have the digestive system of a carnivore'; numbers have 'risen to over eighteen hundred'; bamboo 'provides so little energy'; 'Not all experts welcomed the change'.`,passage:`The giant panda has long served as a symbol of wildlife conservation, and for good reason. By the 1980s, its numbers in the wild had fallen to around a thousand, largely because the bamboo forests on which it depends were being cleared for farming and logging. Pandas are poorly suited to their diet. Although they belong to the order of meat-eating mammals and have the digestive system of a carnivore, they live almost entirely on bamboo, which provides so little energy that they must eat for up to fourteen hours a day. Breeding them in captivity also proved extremely difficult for many years, as females are able to conceive for only a few days each year. Since then, the Chinese government has created a network of protected reserves and banned logging in many areas, and wild numbers have risen to over eighteen hundred. In 2016, the species was reclassified from 'endangered' to 'vulnerable'. Not all experts welcomed the change, warning that it might encourage complacency. Many reserves are small and isolated from one another, which prevents populations from mixing, and climate change could eventually destroy much of the remaining bamboo.`,statements:[{text:`Pandas have the digestive system of a meat-eating animal.`,answer:!0},{text:`Wild panda numbers have increased since the 1980s.`,answer:!0},{text:`Bamboo provides pandas with a large amount of energy.`,answer:!1},{text:`All experts agreed with the decision to reclassify the species.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-038`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`'the pictures it sent back were fuzzy'; Hubble helped 'estimate the age of the universe'; the error was 'around one-fiftieth of the thickness of a human hair'; 'the mirror could not be replaced', so corrective optics were installed.`,passage:`In 1990, the Hubble Space Telescope was launched with enormous expectations. Orbiting above the Earth's atmosphere, which blurs the view of ground-based telescopes, it was expected to produce the sharpest images of the universe ever seen. Within weeks, however, it became clear that something was badly wrong: the pictures it sent back were fuzzy. Investigators discovered that the telescope's main mirror had been ground to slightly the wrong shape. The error was tiny, around one-fiftieth of the thickness of a human hair, but it was enough to ruin the images. It had gone unnoticed because a testing instrument had been assembled incorrectly, and warning signs from other tests had been ignored. The telescope became the subject of jokes, and the space agency faced fierce criticism. Since the mirror could not be replaced, engineers designed corrective optics that would work rather like glasses. In 1993, astronauts carried out a complex series of spacewalks to install them. The repair was a complete success, and Hubble went on to produce some of the most celebrated images in the history of science, helping astronomers to estimate the age of the universe and to discover that its expansion is accelerating.`,statements:[{text:`Hubble's first images were extremely sharp.`,answer:!1},{text:`Hubble later helped scientists estimate the age of the universe.`,answer:!0},{text:`The mirror's error was very small.`,answer:!0},{text:`The faulty mirror was replaced with a new one in 1993.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-039`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`'The city is slowly sinking, while sea levels are rising'; the project 'was plagued by delays, rising costs' and corruption; the barriers 'are filled with air' and rise; when raised, 'ships cannot enter or leave the port'.`,passage:`For centuries, Venice has lived in an uneasy relationship with the sea. The city is slowly sinking, while sea levels are rising, and exceptionally high tides have become increasingly frequent. In 2019, one of the worst floods in living memory submerged much of the historic centre, damaging churches, shops and homes. A system of mobile barriers designed to protect the lagoon had been planned for decades, but the project was plagued by delays, rising costs and a corruption scandal that led to numerous arrests. The barriers, which lie flat on the seabed at the entrances to the lagoon, are filled with air when a high tide is forecast, causing them to rise and temporarily seal the lagoon off from the sea. They were finally used for the first time in 2020 and have since prevented a number of serious floods. Nevertheless, the system is not a permanent solution. Each time the barriers are raised, ships cannot enter or leave the port, and the exchange of water between the lagoon and the sea is interrupted, which may harm its delicate ecosystem. If sea levels continue to rise as predicted, the barriers may eventually have to be raised so often that these problems become serious.`,statements:[{text:`Venice is sinking at the same time as sea levels are rising.`,answer:!0},{text:`The barrier project was completed quickly and within budget.`,answer:!1},{text:`The barriers rise when they are filled with air.`,answer:!0},{text:`Raising the barriers has no effect on shipping.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-040`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The ants 'do not eat the leaves' but grow fungus on them; the queen 'carries a small piece of the fungus with her'; bacteria 'produce chemicals that kill the mould'; colonies are divided 'into different castes according to size'.`,passage:`Leafcutter ants, found in the forests and grasslands of Central and South America, were farming long before humans were. Columns of workers can be seen carrying pieces of leaf many times their own weight back to their underground nests. Contrary to what one might expect, the ants do not eat the leaves. Instead, they chew them into a paste on which they grow a particular kind of fungus, and it is this fungus that feeds the colony. The relationship is so close that neither partner can survive without the other. When a young queen leaves to start a new colony, she carries a small piece of the fungus with her in a pocket inside her mouth. The ants also tend their gardens with great care, removing waste and fighting off a harmful mould that attacks the fungus. To do so, they rely on bacteria living on their bodies, which produce chemicals that kill the mould, in effect a natural form of pesticide. A mature colony may contain several million ants, divided into different castes according to size, from tiny gardeners to large soldiers that defend the nest. Their appetite for leaves can make them a serious pest for farmers in the regions where they live.`,statements:[{text:`Leafcutter ants feed on the leaves they collect.`,answer:!1},{text:`A new queen takes some fungus with her when she starts a colony.`,answer:!0},{text:`The ants use bacteria to protect their fungus from mould.`,answer:!0},{text:`All the ants in a colony are the same size.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-041`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`They change 'not only the colour of their skin but also its pattern and texture'; smaller males imitate 'the appearance of females'; the skin theory 'has not been proven'; some 'were willing to wait for a preferred food'.`,passage:`Cuttlefish, relatives of squid and octopuses, are among the most accomplished masters of disguise in the animal kingdom. Within a fraction of a second, they can change not only the colour of their skin but also its pattern and texture, blending into sand, rocks or seaweed. This is made possible by millions of tiny sacs of pigment in the skin, each surrounded by muscles controlled directly by the nervous system. By expanding or shrinking these sacs, the animal can produce stripes, spots or waves of colour that ripple across its body. Remarkably, cuttlefish appear to be colour-blind, which has left scientists wondering how they manage to match their surroundings so precisely. One theory suggests that their skin itself may be able to detect light, although this has not been proven. Their displays are not used only for hiding. Males put on dazzling shows to attract females, and some smaller males have been observed imitating the appearance of females in order to approach a mate without being challenged by larger rivals. Cuttlefish have also performed well in tests of self-control: in one study, some individuals were willing to wait for a preferred food rather than accept a less attractive one straight away.`,statements:[{text:`Cuttlefish can change the texture of their skin as well as its colour.`,answer:!0},{text:`Some small males pretend to be females.`,answer:!0},{text:`Scientists have proved that cuttlefish skin can detect light.`,answer:!1},{text:`In one study, cuttlefish always chose to eat the first food offered.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-042`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`Texts had been printed in East Asia 'for centuries'; the technology 'spread with astonishing speed'; the press was 'adapted from those used to make wine'; 'Gutenberg himself gained little from his invention'.`,passage:`Around the middle of the fifteenth century, a goldsmith in the German city of Mainz developed a method of printing that would transform European society. Printing itself was not new; in East Asia, texts had been printed from carved wooden blocks for centuries, and movable type had also been invented there. Johannes Gutenberg's achievement lay in combining several technologies into an efficient system: individual metal letters that could be cast quickly and in large numbers, an oil-based ink that stuck to metal, and a press adapted from those used to make wine. His most famous product, a Bible printed in the mid-1450s, is admired to this day for its beauty. Ironically, Gutenberg himself gained little from his invention. He had borrowed heavily to finance his work, and after a legal dispute his business partner took over much of his equipment. The new technology, however, spread with astonishing speed. Within fifty years, printing presses were operating in hundreds of European towns, and millions of books had been produced. Ideas could now circulate far more rapidly and widely than ever before, and historians have linked printing to developments as varied as the Reformation, the rise of modern science and the spread of literacy.`,statements:[{text:`Gutenberg was the first person in the world to print texts.`,answer:!1},{text:`Printing spread quickly across Europe.`,answer:!0},{text:`Gutenberg's press was based on a type of press used for making wine.`,answer:!0},{text:`Gutenberg became very rich as a result of his invention.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-043`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The practice 'killed a considerable number of bell ringers'; Franklin 'became convinced that lightning was a form of electricity'; historians 'still debate exactly what happened'; 'its basic design has hardly changed since'.`,passage:`In the middle of the eighteenth century, lightning was still widely regarded as a mysterious and even supernatural force. Church bells were rung during storms in the belief that they would drive the lightning away, a practice that killed a considerable number of bell ringers, since church towers were often the tallest buildings in town. Benjamin Franklin, the American writer, politician and scientist, became convinced that lightning was a form of electricity. He proposed an experiment in which a metal rod placed on a high building would draw electricity from a storm cloud, and he is famously said to have flown a kite during a thunderstorm to prove his point. Historians still debate exactly what happened during the kite experiment, and it is worth noting that a scientist in Russia was killed while attempting a similar test shortly afterwards. Franklin's most practical contribution was the lightning rod: a pointed metal bar fixed to the top of a building and connected by a wire to the ground, which safely carries the electrical current away. Although some religious figures objected that it interfered with the will of God, the device spread rapidly, and its basic design has hardly changed since.`,statements:[{text:`Ringing church bells during storms was a safe practice.`,answer:!1},{text:`Franklin believed that lightning was electrical.`,answer:!0},{text:`Historians agree on every detail of the kite experiment.`,answer:!1},{text:`The design of the lightning rod has changed very little since Franklin's time.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-044`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`Some countries 'rely on it for most of their supply'; reverse osmosis forces water 'through membranes', while boiling was the early method; desalinated water 'is still more expensive'; brine 'can harm marine life near the outlet'.`,passage:`As populations grow and droughts become more frequent, a growing number of countries are turning to the sea for their drinking water. Desalination, the removal of salt from seawater, is now carried out in thousands of plants around the world, particularly in the Middle East, where some countries rely on it for most of their supply. Early plants worked by boiling seawater and collecting the steam, a method that required huge amounts of energy. Most modern plants use a process called reverse osmosis, in which water is forced at high pressure through membranes with pores so small that salt cannot pass through. This has considerably reduced the cost, although desalinated water is still more expensive than water from rivers or underground sources. Environmental concerns also remain. For every litre of fresh water produced, plants generate a similar or greater amount of extremely salty liquid, known as brine, which is usually pumped back into the sea, where it can harm marine life near the outlet. In addition, many plants are powered by fossil fuels. Engineers are working on ways to reduce these impacts, for example by running plants on solar energy and finding commercial uses for the minerals contained in the brine.`,statements:[{text:`Some countries in the Middle East obtain most of their water from desalination.`,answer:!0},{text:`Reverse osmosis works by boiling seawater.`,answer:!1},{text:`Desalinated water is now cheaper than water from rivers.`,answer:!1},{text:`The brine produced by desalination can harm marine life.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-045`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`Galvani 'concluded that the electricity came from the animal itself'; the pile 'produced a steady electric current'; Volta 'was not persuaded'; 'both men were partly right', since nerves do use electrical signals.`,passage:`In the 1780s, the Italian physician Luigi Galvani noticed that the legs of a dead frog twitched when touched with two different metals. He concluded that the electricity came from the animal itself, and called it 'animal electricity'. His fellow countryman Alessandro Volta, a professor of physics, was not persuaded. He suspected that the electricity was produced by the contact between the two metals, with the frog merely acting as a conductor. The disagreement between the two scientists became one of the most famous scientific debates of the age. To prove his theory, Volta built a tall stack of alternating discs of zinc and copper, separated by pieces of cardboard soaked in salt water. When the top and bottom of the pile were connected by a wire, it produced a steady electric current. Announced in 1800, this 'voltaic pile' was the first battery, and it allowed scientists to study electricity in a way that had previously been impossible. Within weeks, researchers had used it to split water into hydrogen and oxygen. As it turned out, both men were partly right: Volta's explanation of the battery was correct, but nerves and muscles do indeed use electrical signals. The unit of electrical potential, the volt, is named in his honour.`,statements:[{text:`Galvani believed that the electricity came from the frog.`,answer:!0},{text:`The voltaic pile produced a steady electric current.`,answer:!0},{text:`Volta agreed with Galvani's explanation.`,answer:!1},{text:`According to the text, Galvani was completely wrong.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-046`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`Honey 'contains very little water'; crystallising 'does not mean that it has gone off'; some hospitals use 'sterilised honey in dressings'; it 'should not' be given 'to babies under one year old'.`,passage:`Honey is one of the very few foods that, if stored properly, can last almost indefinitely. Archaeologists excavating ancient Egyptian tombs are said to have found pots of honey that were thousands of years old and still edible, although such stories are difficult to verify. The secret lies in its chemistry. Honey contains very little water and a great deal of sugar, which means that bacteria and other microorganisms that land on it are quickly dried out and die. It is also slightly acidic, and an enzyme added by bees produces small quantities of hydrogen peroxide, a substance with antiseptic properties. For these reasons, honey was used to treat wounds in many ancient cultures, and some hospitals today use specially sterilised honey in dressings for certain types of injury. Ordinary honey should not, however, be given to babies under one year old, because it can occasionally contain spores of a bacterium that their immature digestive systems are unable to cope with. Over time, honey may crystallise and become solid, but this is a natural process and does not mean that it has gone off. Gently warming the jar will usually restore its original texture. Problems arise only if water gets in, which can allow it to ferment.`,statements:[{text:`Honey contains a large amount of water.`,answer:!1},{text:`Crystallised honey has not necessarily gone bad.`,answer:!0},{text:`Some hospitals use honey to treat certain wounds.`,answer:!0},{text:`Honey is suitable for babies of any age.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-047`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`'their hearts can be seen beating' from below; they store red blood cells 'in their liver'; they 'appear able to avoid this problem entirely'; a new technique let scientists observe them 'without waking them'.`,passage:`In the rainforests of Central and South America live small frogs whose skin is so transparent that, from below, their hearts can be seen beating. For a long time, scientists were puzzled by how these glass frogs managed to remain almost invisible, since blood is red and would normally make an animal easy to spot. A study published in 2022 provided a surprising answer. When the frogs sleep during the day, clinging to the underside of leaves, they remove almost all the red blood cells from circulation and store them in their liver, which is covered by a reflective layer. As a result, their bodies become two or three times more transparent. When they wake up and become active, the cells return to the bloodstream. What makes this particularly remarkable is that, in humans and most other animals, such a concentration of blood cells would cause dangerous clots. The frogs appear able to avoid this problem entirely, and researchers hope that understanding how they do so might one day help in developing new treatments for blood disorders. The findings were made possible by a new imaging technique that allowed scientists to observe the frogs without waking them, since stress immediately caused their blood to return to circulation.`,statements:[{text:`From below, the heart of a glass frog can be seen.`,answer:!0},{text:`The frogs store red blood cells in their liver while sleeping.`,answer:!0},{text:`The frogs suffer from dangerous blood clots.`,answer:!1},{text:`Scientists had to wake the frogs to study their blood.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-048`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says 'a significant proportion of it is caused by noise'; human hair cells 'do not grow back'; damage 'builds up gradually and painlessly'; 'it is not yet clear whether one directly causes the other'.`,passage:`Hearing loss is often thought of as an inevitable part of growing old, but a significant proportion of it is caused by noise and could be prevented. Inside the inner ear are thousands of tiny hair cells that convert sound vibrations into signals sent to the brain. Loud sounds can damage or destroy these cells, and, unlike those of birds and some other animals, human hair cells do not grow back. The damage often builds up gradually and painlessly, so that many people are unaware of it until it has become serious. The World Health Organization has estimated that more than a billion young people are at risk of hearing loss because of unsafe listening habits, particularly the use of headphones at high volume for long periods. A common rule of thumb is that if someone standing nearby can hear the music from your headphones, it is probably too loud. Ringing in the ears after a concert is a warning sign that the ears have been overexposed. Researchers have also found links between untreated hearing loss in later life and an increased risk of dementia, although it is not yet clear whether one directly causes the other. Fortunately, using hearing aids appears to reduce some of these risks.`,statements:[{text:`All hearing loss is caused by ageing.`,answer:!1},{text:`Human hair cells in the ear cannot grow back once destroyed.`,answer:!0},{text:`Noise damage to hearing is always painful and noticed immediately.`,answer:!1},{text:`It has been proved that hearing loss directly causes dementia.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-049`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`It lived 'only on the island of Mauritius'; pigs, rats and monkeys 'destroyed the dodo's nests and ate its eggs'; paintings 'were made from overfed birds kept in captivity'; bones were found 'only in the nineteenth century'.`,passage:`The dodo, a large flightless bird that lived only on the island of Mauritius in the Indian Ocean, has become a symbol of extinction. Having evolved without any natural predators, it had no fear of humans when Dutch sailors arrived at the end of the sixteenth century. Within less than a hundred years, the species had disappeared. It is often assumed that sailors simply ate the birds until none were left, but historians now believe that hunting was not the main cause. More damaging were the animals that the newcomers brought with them, such as pigs, rats and monkeys, which destroyed the dodo's nests and ate its eggs, as well as the clearing of the forests where it lived. Our image of the dodo as a fat, clumsy creature may also be misleading. Many of the paintings on which it is based were made from overfed birds kept in captivity, or from badly stuffed specimens. Recent studies of its bones suggest that it was a relatively agile animal, well adapted to its environment. Remarkably little physical evidence of the bird survived, and for a long time some scholars even doubted that it had ever existed. It was only in the nineteenth century that substantial collections of dodo bones were found.`,statements:[{text:`The dodo lived on many islands in the Indian Ocean.`,answer:!1},{text:`Animals brought by the newcomers contributed to the dodo's extinction.`,answer:!0},{text:`Some famous images of the dodo may be based on overfed captive birds.`,answer:!0},{text:`Large collections of dodo bones were found as early as the seventeenth century.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-050`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The actors 'carried its timbers across the river'; 'nobody was seriously hurt'; he 'died a few years before the new theatre opened'; plays are performed 'without microphones'.`,passage:`The original Globe Theatre, where many of Shakespeare's plays were first performed, was built in London in 1599 by a company of actors who had taken apart an earlier theatre and carried its timbers across the river. In 1613, a cannon fired during a performance set fire to the thatched roof, and the building burned to the ground within an hour, although nobody was seriously hurt. It was rebuilt the following year, only to be closed in 1642 when the authorities banned theatrical performances, and it was demolished shortly afterwards. For centuries, its exact location was unknown. The idea of reconstructing it came from an American actor and director, who campaigned for more than twenty years to raise the necessary funds but died a few years before the new theatre opened in 1997. Built close to the original site, the modern Globe uses the same construction methods as the Elizabethan building, including oak beams joined with wooden pegs and a thatched roof, the first allowed in London since the Great Fire of 1666. Plays are performed in daylight, without microphones, and around seven hundred spectators stand in the open yard in front of the stage, just as audiences did four centuries ago.`,statements:[{text:`The first Globe was built partly with timber from an earlier theatre.`,answer:!0},{text:`Many people died in the fire of 1613.`,answer:!1},{text:`The man who campaigned for the reconstruction lived to see it open.`,answer:!1},{text:`Plays at the modern Globe are performed without microphones.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-051`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`Edison imagined it 'would be used mainly for dictating letters'; discs 'could be mass-produced far more cheaply' and won; the few minutes per side 'influenced the length of popular songs'; 'Some composers feared' recorded music.`,passage:`Long before the arrival of streaming services, the invention of sound recording completely changed the way people experienced music. Until the late nineteenth century, music could be heard only when someone performed it live, whether in a concert hall, a church or a family living room. In 1877, Thomas Edison demonstrated a machine that recorded sound as grooves on a cylinder wrapped in tinfoil, although he initially imagined it would be used mainly for dictating letters rather than for entertainment. A decade later, the flat disc was introduced, and because discs could be mass-produced far more cheaply than cylinders, they eventually won the competition between the two formats. Early recordings were made without microphones: musicians crowded around a large horn and played as loudly as possible, and quiet instruments were often replaced by louder ones. Each side of a disc could hold only a few minutes of music, which influenced the length of popular songs for decades. Not everyone welcomed the new technology. Some composers feared that recorded music would discourage people from learning to play instruments themselves, and in many homes the gramophone did indeed gradually replace the piano as the centre of family entertainment.`,statements:[{text:`Edison first thought his machine would be used mainly for dictating letters.`,answer:!0},{text:`Cylinders won the competition because they were cheaper to produce than discs.`,answer:!1},{text:`The short playing time of discs affected the length of popular songs.`,answer:!0},{text:`All composers were enthusiastic about recorded music.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-052`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the 'real value' lies in the data and that shoppers without a card 'often end up paying noticeably more'; regulators are interested because of the schemes' role in pricing.`,passage:`Supermarket loyalty cards are usually presented as a small reward for faithful customers: collect enough points and you will eventually receive a voucher or a discount on your weekly shop. Yet the real value of these schemes lies elsewhere. Every time a card is scanned, the retailer learns what was bought, when, in which store and alongside which other products. Accumulated over months, this information allows companies to predict with remarkable accuracy what a household is likely to need next, and to tailor offers accordingly. A family that suddenly starts buying nappies, for instance, may soon find itself receiving coupons for baby food. Critics argue that customers are effectively paying for their discounts with personal data whose worth they seriously underestimate. Retailers respond that the arrangement is transparent and voluntary, and that nobody is forced to sign up. Consumer groups, however, point out that the prices offered to cardholders are increasingly the only reasonable ones, which means that shoppers who refuse to join often end up paying noticeably more for the same goods. Had the schemes remained a modest bonus, few would object; it is their growing role in setting prices that has drawn the attention of regulators.`,statements:[{text:`According to the text, the main benefit of loyalty schemes for retailers is the information they collect.`,answer:!0},{text:`Retailers admit that customers are forced to join their schemes.`,answer:!1},{text:`Shoppers without a card may pay higher prices for identical products.`,answer:!0},{text:`Regulators became interested because the vouchers offered were too small.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-053`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The studies found the loans 'rarely transformed household earnings'; economists see them as 'one useful instrument among many', not a substitute for public investment.`,passage:`When small loans to people without access to banks first attracted international attention, they were hailed as a near-miraculous tool against poverty. The idea was simple: lend modest sums to would-be entrepreneurs, typically women in rural areas, so that they could buy a sewing machine, a few goats or stock for a market stall. Repayment rates were impressively high, which seemed to prove that the poor were reliable borrowers who had simply been ignored by conventional lenders. In the years that followed, however, a series of careful studies produced a more sober picture. While the loans did help families cope with emergencies and smooth out irregular incomes, they rarely transformed household earnings in the dramatic way that early supporters had promised. In some regions, rapid commercial expansion led to borrowers taking out several loans at once, sometimes from competing lenders, simply to repay earlier debts. Few economists now dismiss the approach altogether. Rather, they see it as one useful instrument among many, most effective when combined with savings schemes, training and access to wider markets. What it is not, they insist, is a substitute for public investment in health, education and infrastructure.`,statements:[{text:`Early supporters believed small loans could dramatically reduce poverty.`,answer:!0},{text:`Later research confirmed that the loans usually multiplied household incomes.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some borrowers took on new loans in order to pay off old ones.`,answer:!0},{text:`Most economists today believe the approach should be abandoned completely.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-054`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`Surveys show people tip mainly to avoid guilt, and tips are only weakly linked to ratings; some staff left after tips were abolished.`,passage:`Few customs confuse international travellers as much as tipping. In some countries, a generous tip is regarded as an essential part of a waiter's income, and leaving nothing would be taken as a serious insult. In others, service is already included in the price, and an extra payment may be met with polite confusion or even refused outright. Economists have long been intrigued by the practice, since it seems to defy the assumption that people only pay what they are obliged to. Surveys suggest that customers tip mainly to avoid feeling guilty or to be seen as fair, rather than to reward exceptional service. Indeed, studies comparing the size of tips with diners' ratings of their meals have found only a weak link between the two. More troubling is evidence that tips may reflect diners' prejudices about a server's age, appearance or background. For this reason, a number of restaurant owners have experimented with abolishing tips altogether and raising menu prices instead, paying staff a higher fixed wage. The results have been mixed: some employees welcomed the stability, while others, particularly those who had previously earned large tips, left for establishments where the old system still applied.`,statements:[{text:`In certain countries, offering a tip may lead to confusion.`,answer:!0},{text:`Research shows that tips closely reflect how much diners enjoyed their meal.`,answer:!1},{text:`The text suggests that tips are not always distributed fairly.`,answer:!0},{text:`All employees were happy when their restaurant replaced tips with higher wages.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-055`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`Resale used to be linked to charity shops; researchers fear it may 'encourage people to buy more'; brands now launch their own platforms after initial suspicion.`,passage:`Only a decade ago, buying second-hand clothes was associated mainly with charity shops and limited budgets. Today, resale has become one of the fastest-growing parts of the fashion industry. Mobile apps allow anyone to photograph an unwanted jacket and sell it to a stranger within hours, and younger shoppers in particular seem untroubled by the idea of wearing garments that have belonged to someone else. For many, the appeal is partly environmental: extending the life of a piece of clothing is widely seen as more responsible than buying something new. Not everyone is convinced that the boom is entirely positive, though. Some researchers warn that the ease of reselling may actually encourage people to buy more, since they can tell themselves that any purchase they regret can quickly be passed on. If that is the case, resale could end up speeding up consumption rather than slowing it down. Major fashion brands, which initially viewed the trend with suspicion, have begun to launch their own resale platforms, hoping both to polish their environmental image and to capture a share of a market that would otherwise bypass them entirely.`,statements:[{text:`Second-hand clothing was once linked mostly to people with little money.`,answer:!0},{text:`Young shoppers tend to be uncomfortable wearing clothes previously owned by others.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some experts fear that resale might increase the amount of clothing people buy.`,answer:!0},{text:`Fashion brands welcomed the resale trend enthusiastically from the start.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-056`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`Small regular payments 'attract far less attention'; some governments require that cancelling be no harder than signing up; forgetfulness may be the main cause.`,passage:`It is now possible to subscribe to almost anything: films and music, of course, but also razors, coffee, children's toys, car features and even socks. For companies, the attraction of the subscription model is obvious. Instead of having to persuade customers to buy again and again, they receive a steady and predictable flow of income, which investors tend to reward handsomely. For consumers, the benefits are less clear-cut. Subscriptions promise convenience, yet many people underestimate how much they spend on them each month, partly because small regular payments attract far less attention than a single large one. A recent survey found that a significant proportion of respondents were still paying for at least one service they had not used for months. Businesses are often accused of exploiting this inertia by making it effortless to sign up but surprisingly difficult to cancel, hiding the relevant button deep in a menu or insisting on a phone call. In response, several governments have introduced rules requiring that cancelling a subscription be no harder than starting one. Whether such measures will significantly change consumer behaviour remains to be seen, since forgetfulness, rather than obstruction, may be the main reason unwanted payments continue.`,statements:[{text:`Investors generally value the regular income that subscriptions provide.`,answer:!0},{text:`People tend to notice small monthly payments more than one large payment.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some governments have made laws about how easy it must be to cancel a subscription.`,answer:!0},{text:`The text claims that new rules have already solved the problem of unwanted payments.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-057`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`Studies show the amounts are 'tiny' and users 'were already committed to shopping locally'; shopkeepers struggle because suppliers are 'based elsewhere'.`,passage:`In a number of towns, residents have experimented with printing their own money. These local currencies, usually valid only within a limited area, are exchanged one-for-one with the national currency and can be spent in participating shops, cafés and markets. Their supporters argue that, because the notes cannot be used in large chains or online, they keep spending within the community and help independent businesses survive. Some schemes have attracted considerable publicity, with well-known local figures appearing on the banknotes and tourists buying them as souvenirs. Economists, however, tend to be sceptical about their real impact. Studies of several schemes suggest that the amounts in circulation are usually tiny compared with the local economy, and that most of the people who use them were already committed to shopping locally. In other words, the currency may reward existing habits rather than create new ones. Shopkeepers, too, have mixed feelings: accepting the notes can create extra paperwork, and businesses often struggle to spend what they receive, since their own suppliers are frequently based elsewhere. Even so, defenders insist that the true value of a local currency is symbolic, sparking conversations about where money goes and who benefits from it.`,statements:[{text:`Local notes are worth the same as the national currency.`,answer:!0},{text:`Studies show local currencies have persuaded many new people to shop locally.`,answer:!1},{text:`Shopkeepers sometimes find it hard to use the local notes they receive.`,answer:!0},{text:`Defenders accept that local currencies have no value at all.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-058`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`Housing costs are 'an obvious factor' but 'not the whole story'; researchers say clear agreements matter 'far more than the size of the house'.`,passage:`For much of the twentieth century, the ideal home in many Western countries was a house occupied by a single nuclear family. Grandparents lived separately, and adult children were expected to move out as soon as they could afford to. In recent years, however, multigenerational households have been making a quiet comeback. Rising housing costs are an obvious factor: young adults who cannot afford to rent on their own are staying with their parents for longer, and older relatives are sometimes invited to move in to share expenses. Yet economics is not the whole story. Many families report genuine advantages, such as grandparents helping with childcare and teenagers learning practical skills from older relatives. Architects have begun to respond, designing homes with separate entrances, small second kitchens and flexible rooms that can be adapted as needs change. Living together is not always harmonious, of course. Disagreements over noise, money and household chores are common, and privacy can become a precious commodity. Researchers who have interviewed such families conclude that clear agreements about responsibilities, made at the outset, matter far more than the size of the house in determining whether the arrangement succeeds.`,statements:[{text:`According to the text, high housing costs are the only reason families live together.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some new homes are being designed to suit several generations.`,answer:!0},{text:`Shared living can lead to conflicts about money and housework.`,answer:!0},{text:`Researchers found that larger houses are the key to successful shared living.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-059`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`Owners treat pets 'as members of the family'; vet costs 'have risen faster than general inflation'; some charities run courses for prospective owners.`,passage:`Pet ownership rose sharply in many countries during the past decade, and the businesses serving animal owners have grown accordingly. Spending now extends far beyond food and basic veterinary care to include insurance, grooming salons, day care centres, specialist diets and even pet-friendly holidays. Sociologists link this trend to broader changes in family life. With people marrying later and having fewer children, many treat their animals less as possessions than as members of the family, and are prepared to spend accordingly. However, the boom has a darker side. Animal welfare organisations report that a significant number of pets acquired on impulse are later abandoned or handed over to shelters when their owners realise how much time and money they require. Veterinary costs, in particular, have risen faster than general inflation, partly because clinics now offer advanced treatments that were once available only to humans. Some charities have responded by running courses for prospective owners, encouraging them to think carefully before committing to an animal that may live for fifteen years or more. Others argue that breeders and sellers should be legally required to check that buyers are prepared for the responsibility.`,statements:[{text:`Spending on pets now includes services such as holidays and insurance.`,answer:!0},{text:`Sociologists connect the trend to people having more children.`,answer:!1},{text:`Veterinary fees have increased more quickly than prices in general.`,answer:!0},{text:`Breeders are already legally required to check buyers carefully.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-060`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`Popular names 'begin to sound ordinary' and are avoided; old names return after about a century; variety 'has increased considerably'.`,passage:`Parents choosing a name for their baby may believe they are making a highly personal decision, but statistics suggest otherwise. Names rise and fall in popularity in remarkably regular patterns, rather like fashions in clothing. A name that sounds fresh and distinctive to one generation of parents often becomes common within a few years, precisely because so many others have had the same idea at the same time. Once a name is widely used, it begins to sound ordinary, and the next generation of parents tends to avoid it. Researchers who study these cycles have found that names associated with older relatives frequently return after an absence of around a century, when they no longer remind people of anyone they know personally. Celebrities and popular television series can also cause sudden spikes, although such names often disappear as quickly as they arrived. Interestingly, the overall variety of names has increased considerably. Whereas a small number of names once accounted for a large share of all babies, parents today choose from a much wider range, and many invent entirely new names or unusual spellings. Some linguists see this as a reflection of a culture that places growing value on individuality.`,statements:[{text:`The popularity of names follows fairly predictable patterns.`,answer:!0},{text:`Once a name becomes very common, parents tend to choose it even more.`,answer:!1},{text:`Names inspired by television shows usually remain popular for decades.`,answer:!1},{text:`Parents today choose from a greater variety of names than in the past.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-061`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The food produced is 'rarely significant'; many gardens exist 'on short-term agreements'; schemes succeed when they involve locals 'from the very beginning'.`,passage:`Across many cities, unused patches of land are being transformed into community gardens where residents grow vegetables, herbs and flowers. Some occupy former car parks; others are squeezed between apartment blocks or laid out on rooftops. Although the amount of food they produce is rarely significant, supporters claim that their real contribution lies in what they do for people rather than for supplies. Gardeners describe the pleasure of working outdoors, the chance to meet neighbours they would otherwise never have spoken to and, for those without a garden of their own, the simple satisfaction of watching something grow. Health researchers have found that regular gardening is associated with lower stress levels and improved mood, particularly among older participants. There are tensions, however. Because community gardens often occupy land awaiting development, many exist on short-term agreements and may be removed when a builder finally moves in. In some neighbourhoods, critics have pointed out that gardens tend to be run by newer, better-off residents, which can leave long-standing communities feeling excluded from projects meant to bring everyone together. Successful schemes, organisers say, are those that involve local people from the very beginning rather than simply inviting them once the plans have been made.`,statements:[{text:`Community gardens generally produce large quantities of food.`,answer:!1},{text:`Gardening has been linked to reduced stress.`,answer:!0},{text:`Many gardens are secure because they have permanent agreements for the land.`,answer:!1},{text:`Organisers believe local people should be involved before plans are made.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-062`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`Members are chosen at random but balanced; some assemblies recommended reforms politicians thought 'too risky'; supporters accept governments may ignore them.`,passage:`When a government needs to settle a divisive question, it usually relies on elected politicians or, occasionally, a national referendum. A growing number of countries and cities have tried a third approach: the citizens' assembly. A group of perhaps a hundred people is selected at random, much like a jury, but carefully balanced so that it reflects the population in terms of age, gender, region and education. Over several weekends, members listen to experts, question campaigners from different sides and discuss the issue in small groups before voting on recommendations. Advocates argue that ordinary people, given time and good information, are capable of thoughtful and nuanced decisions, and that they are free from the pressure of re-election that constrains politicians. Several assemblies have reached conclusions that surprised observers, recommending reforms that professional politicians had considered too risky to propose. Critics question whether a small, unelected group should influence national policy, and note that governments sometimes ignore the recommendations once the assembly has disbanded. Supporters accept that this is a real danger. Unless a government commits in advance to responding formally to the proposals, they warn, the exercise risks becoming little more than an expensive display of public consultation.`,statements:[{text:`Members of citizens' assemblies are chosen through elections.`,answer:!1},{text:`Assembly members hear from experts before making recommendations.`,answer:!0},{text:`Some assemblies have proposed changes that politicians had avoided.`,answer:!0},{text:`Supporters deny that governments might ignore an assembly's proposals.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-063`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The aim was to save fuel; the spring change disrupts the body clock; countries would need to agree, and there is 'no consensus' on which time to keep.`,passage:`Twice a year, millions of people move their clocks forward or back by an hour, a practice that has provoked debate almost since it began. The original justification was economic: by shifting daylight into the evening during summer, governments hoped to reduce the use of artificial lighting and save fuel. Whether it ever achieved substantial savings is disputed, and in an age of efficient lighting and air conditioning, the energy argument looks weaker than ever. Opponents now focus mainly on health. Sleep scientists point out that the spring change, in particular, disrupts the body's internal clock, and some studies have reported a short-term rise in road accidents and heart problems in the days that follow. Yet abolishing the system is far from straightforward. Neighbouring countries would need to agree on which time to keep permanently, since a patchwork of different arrangements could cause chaos for transport and trade. Moreover, there is no consensus on whether permanent summer time or permanent winter time would be preferable. Darker mornings in winter would worry parents of schoolchildren, while darker evenings would disappoint those who enjoy outdoor activities after work. As a result, proposals to end the changes have repeatedly stalled.`,statements:[{text:`Changing the clocks was first introduced to save energy.`,answer:!0},{text:`Scientists believe the autumn change is the most harmful to health.`,answer:!1},{text:`Ending the system would require cooperation between neighbouring countries.`,answer:!0},{text:`Most experts agree that permanent summer time is the best option.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-064`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`Unstructured interviews are 'a surprisingly poor predictor'; structured ones predict performance 'more accurately'; the compromise keeps the open part out of the score.`,passage:`Most managers believe they are good at judging people in interviews, yet decades of research suggest that the traditional unstructured interview, in which the conversation flows freely, is a surprisingly poor predictor of how well a candidate will perform. Interviewers tend to form an impression within the first few minutes and spend the rest of the meeting looking for evidence to confirm it. They are also influenced by factors that have nothing to do with the job, such as whether the candidate shares their hobbies, went to the same university or simply has a firm handshake. Structured interviews, by contrast, ask every candidate the same questions in the same order and score the answers against agreed criteria. Studies consistently show that this approach predicts job performance more accurately and reduces the influence of personal bias. Despite the evidence, many organisations are reluctant to adopt it. Interviewers often complain that structured formats feel rigid and impersonal, and that they prevent them from getting to know the real person. Some experts suggest a compromise: keeping a core of standard questions while allowing a few minutes of open conversation at the end, which should not be counted in the final score.`,statements:[{text:`Interviewers often make up their minds very early in an unstructured interview.`,answer:!0},{text:`Structured interviews let each interviewer choose different questions for each candidate.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some interviewers dislike structured interviews because they seem impersonal.`,answer:!0},{text:`The suggested compromise includes scoring the open conversation at the end.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-065`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`Even banks and law firms relaxed rules partly to attract younger staff; without rules some find it 'harder to know what is appropriate'; experiments show smart clothes affect perceived competence.`,passage:`Not so long ago, a dark suit and tie were the unquestioned uniform of the office worker. Over the past few decades, dress codes in many sectors have relaxed considerably, beginning with the so-called casual Friday and gradually extending to the rest of the week. Technology companies led the way, cultivating an image in which jeans and trainers signalled creativity rather than carelessness. Even banks and law firms, long the strongholds of formal attire, have loosened their rules, partly to attract younger employees who regard strict codes as outdated. Yet the disappearance of explicit rules has not necessarily made life easier. Some employees report that they now find it harder to know what is appropriate, since casual dress comes in endless varieties, some of which are considered acceptable and others not. Without clear guidance, staff may fear being judged for looking either too formal or too relaxed. There is also evidence that clothing still affects how people are perceived: in several experiments, observers rated smartly dressed individuals as more competent, even when they knew nothing else about them. Consequently, many consultants advise dressing slightly more formally than colleagues on important occasions such as presentations.`,statements:[{text:`Technology companies were among the first to adopt relaxed dress codes.`,answer:!0},{text:`Banks and law firms have kept their strict dress codes unchanged.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some workers find relaxed dress codes confusing.`,answer:!0},{text:`Experiments show that clothing no longer influences how people are judged.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-066`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`Sabbaticals originated in universities; covering colleagues gain experience; unpaid breaks may favour better-paid workers.`,passage:`The idea of a sabbatical, an extended break from work after a number of years of service, originated in universities, where academics were traditionally granted time away from teaching to research and write. In recent years, a small but growing number of private companies have begun offering similar arrangements to their employees, sometimes paid and sometimes unpaid. The breaks typically last between one and six months, and staff use them for purposes as varied as travelling, volunteering, caring for relatives or simply resting. Employers who offer sabbaticals argue that they more than pay for themselves. Employees return refreshed, often with new perspectives, and are less likely to leave for a competitor. Furthermore, colleagues who cover for the absent worker gain valuable experience, which can reveal hidden talent within the organisation. However, smaller firms frequently feel unable to cope without a key member of staff for several months. There is also a concern that only those with substantial savings can afford an unpaid break, meaning the benefit may in practice be restricted to better-paid workers. Some companies have tried to address this by offering partial pay or allowing employees to save part of their salary in advance.`,statements:[{text:`Sabbaticals began in the world of business.`,answer:!1},{text:`Employers claim sabbaticals help them keep staff.`,answer:!0},{text:`Covering for an absent colleague can help a company discover talented employees.`,answer:!0},{text:`Unpaid sabbaticals are equally accessible to all workers regardless of income.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-067`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`Titles are 'a cheap way of rewarding staff'; some recruiters 'largely ignore titles'; others think the issue harmless if job descriptions are accurate.`,passage:`Look through the job advertisements of almost any large organisation and you will notice titles that would have puzzled previous generations. Receptionists have become directors of first impressions, cleaners have become hygiene technicians and junior assistants have been promoted, at least on paper, to coordinators or associates. This phenomenon, sometimes called job title inflation, has several causes. For employers, an impressive title is a cheap way of rewarding staff when pay rises are limited. For employees, a grander title may look better on a professional networking profile and help when applying for future jobs. Yet the trend has drawbacks. When titles no longer reflect responsibilities, recruiters find it increasingly difficult to compare candidates, and some admit that they now largely ignore titles when reading applications. Inflated titles can also create resentment within teams, particularly when a newly hired manager turns out to manage nobody at all. Some companies have reacted by simplifying their structures and adopting standard titles across departments. Others argue that the issue is harmless, provided that job descriptions remain accurate. What matters, they say, is not what someone is called, but whether the work they actually do is clearly defined and fairly rewarded.`,statements:[{text:`Employers may give impressive titles instead of larger pay rises.`,answer:!0},{text:`Recruiters say inflated titles make it easier to compare candidates.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some people consider job title inflation harmless as long as job descriptions are accurate.`,answer:!0},{text:`All companies have responded to the trend by introducing standard titles.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-068`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`In reverse mentoring juniors teach executives; executives gain 'an unfiltered view'; juniors may be 'reluctant to speak frankly'.`,passage:`Mentoring has traditionally flowed in one direction: an experienced senior employee advises a younger colleague on how to navigate the organisation and develop a career. Some companies have turned this arrangement on its head. In reverse mentoring, junior staff are paired with senior executives in order to teach them about areas where the younger generation is assumed to have the advantage, such as social media, new technologies or changing attitudes among customers. The practice was popularised by a large industrial company whose chief executive reportedly asked hundreds of managers to find a young mentor. Advocates claim that the benefits extend well beyond technical knowledge. Executives gain an unfiltered view of what is happening at lower levels of the organisation, while junior employees acquire confidence and visibility that might otherwise take years to achieve. Nevertheless, reverse mentoring is not without its pitfalls. Junior staff may be reluctant to speak frankly to someone who could influence their future, and the relationship can easily become a token gesture if senior participants treat it as a box to be ticked. Organisations that have made it work, consultants say, establish clear goals for each pair and ensure that mentors are protected from any negative consequences of honest feedback.`,statements:[{text:`In reverse mentoring, younger employees advise senior colleagues.`,answer:!0},{text:`According to advocates, the only benefit is improved technical knowledge.`,answer:!1},{text:`Junior mentors may hesitate to be honest with powerful colleagues.`,answer:!0},{text:`Consultants recommend avoiding specific goals for mentoring pairs.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-069`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`Even long-term night workers 'rarely adapt completely'; specialists recommend rotating forwards because the body delays sleep more easily.`,passage:`Hospitals, factories, transport networks and emergency services all depend on people who work while the rest of the population sleeps. Around one in five employees in industrialised countries works outside standard daytime hours, at least occasionally. Research over several decades has linked long-term night work with a range of health problems, including disturbed sleep, digestive disorders and a higher risk of conditions such as diabetes. The underlying cause appears to be the conflict between working hours and the body's internal clock, which is regulated largely by daylight. Even workers who have done night shifts for years rarely adapt completely, partly because on their days off they tend to return to a normal routine in order to spend time with family and friends. Employers cannot eliminate night work, but they can reduce its effects. Sleep specialists recommend schedules in which shifts rotate forwards, from mornings to afternoons to nights, rather than backwards, since the body finds it easier to delay sleep than to advance it. Bright lighting during the shift and dark, quiet conditions for daytime sleep can also help. Perhaps most importantly, experts stress that workers should be consulted when rotas are designed, as schedules imposed without discussion tend to be followed less carefully.`,statements:[{text:`The text says that most employees in industrialised countries work at night.`,answer:!1},{text:`People who have worked night shifts for many years usually adapt fully.`,answer:!1},{text:`Experts suggest that shifts should move from mornings to afternoons to nights.`,answer:!0},{text:`Workers should be involved in planning their schedules.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-070`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`Benefits for younger children are 'small or difficult to detect'; homework may favour children whose parents can help; evaluation of abolition is still incomplete.`,passage:`Few aspects of primary education provoke as much disagreement among parents as homework. Some see it as essential preparation for secondary school, a way of developing discipline and reinforcing what has been learned in class. Others regard it as a source of nightly conflict that eats into time better spent playing, reading for pleasure or simply being with the family. Research offers only partial support to either side. Studies generally find that homework has a clear positive effect on achievement among older students, but for younger children the benefits are small or difficult to detect. Much seems to depend on the type of task set. Brief activities that involve reading together or practising a skill already taught in class appear more useful than lengthy worksheets, which younger pupils may be unable to complete without considerable help. That, in turn, raises a question of fairness: children whose parents have the time and confidence to assist them gain an advantage over those whose parents do not. In response, a number of primary schools have abolished compulsory homework, encouraging daily reading instead. Early reports suggest that standards have not fallen, though head teachers acknowledge that more systematic evaluation is still needed.`,statements:[{text:`Research strongly supports homework for young children.`,answer:!1},{text:`Short reading activities appear more useful than long worksheets for young pupils.`,answer:!0},{text:`Homework can benefit children whose parents are able to help them.`,answer:!0},{text:`Thorough studies have proved that abolishing homework has no negative effects.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-071`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`Gap years now include earning money and local volunteering; some students 'never take up their university place'; some volunteering programmes offer little genuine benefit.`,passage:`Taking a year off between school and university was once regarded in some countries as a privilege of the wealthy, an opportunity to travel the world before settling down to serious study. Over time, the gap year has broadened in scope. Today's students might spend the time earning money to pay for their education, gaining work experience, volunteering locally or learning a language abroad. Universities have generally become more positive about the practice. Admissions staff report that students who have taken a structured break often arrive more mature, more motivated and clearer about what they want to study, and some institutions actively encourage applicants to defer their place. However, the benefits depend heavily on how the year is spent. A gap year with no clear purpose can leave young people struggling to return to the discipline of study, and a minority never take up their university place at all. Concerns have also been raised about certain overseas volunteering programmes, which charge participants substantial fees while offering little genuine benefit to the communities they claim to help. Careers advisers therefore recommend that students plan the year carefully, set realistic goals and choose organisations whose work has been independently assessed.`,statements:[{text:`Gap years were once mainly associated with richer students.`,answer:!0},{text:`Universities have become more critical of gap years over time.`,answer:!1},{text:`A small number of students who take a gap year never start university.`,answer:!0},{text:`All overseas volunteering programmes provide real help to local communities.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-072`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`Studies have produced 'inconsistent findings'; branded items from a single supplier can make uniforms 'surprisingly expensive'; some governments limit branded items.`,passage:`School uniforms are a familiar sight in many countries and almost unknown in others, and the debate over their merits shows little sign of ending. Supporters argue that uniforms reduce visible differences between wealthier and poorer pupils, lessening pressure to wear expensive brands and limiting opportunities for bullying based on appearance. They also claim that a uniform fosters a sense of belonging and helps pupils distinguish between school and leisure, which may improve concentration. Opponents counter that uniforms suppress individuality and that the evidence for improved behaviour or academic results is weak. Indeed, studies comparing schools before and after introducing uniforms have produced inconsistent findings, with some showing modest improvements in attendance and others no measurable change at all. Cost is another point of contention. Although uniforms are often presented as a cheaper alternative to fashionable clothes, some schools require items with the school logo that can only be bought from a single supplier, which can make them surprisingly expensive. Several governments have introduced guidance limiting the number of branded items schools may insist on. Whatever one thinks of uniforms in principle, campaigners argue, families should not face financial hardship simply to comply with a dress code.`,statements:[{text:`Supporters believe uniforms can reduce bullying related to clothing.`,answer:!0},{text:`Studies consistently show that uniforms improve academic results.`,answer:!1},{text:`Uniforms are always cheaper than ordinary clothes.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some governments have restricted how many branded items schools can require.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-073`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`Children who learn instruments often come from richer families, which 'could explain much of the difference'; randomised studies found 'far smaller effects'.`,passage:`In many education systems, music has been squeezed out of the timetable by pressure to improve results in subjects such as mathematics and literacy. Advocates of music education argue that this is a false economy. Learning to play an instrument, they say, develops concentration, memory and perseverance, qualities that benefit pupils in every subject. Claims that music lessons make children more intelligent in general should be treated with caution, however. While some studies have found that children who learn instruments perform better academically, these children often come from families with higher incomes and more educated parents, which could explain much of the difference. Experiments that randomly assign children to music lessons have produced far smaller effects, and some have found none at all. Many music teachers argue that the debate misses the point. Music, in their view, deserves a place in schools for its own sake, just as literature and art do, rather than as a tool for improving test scores. They also point to evidence that playing in ensembles develops teamwork and gives pupils a sense of achievement. The greatest concern is unequal access: where schools cannot provide lessons, only families who can afford private tuition are able to give their children the opportunity.`,statements:[{text:`Music has lost time in some school timetables to other subjects.`,answer:!0},{text:`Randomised experiments have confirmed large intelligence gains from music lessons.`,answer:!1},{text:`Many music teachers think music should be taught mainly to raise test scores.`,answer:!1},{text:`Without school lessons, learning an instrument may depend on family income.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-074`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The approach originated in Scandinavia; research 'is still limited' and often based on teachers' observations; supporters say serious accidents are rare.`,passage:`In forest schools, children spend a large part of their day outdoors, in woodland or other natural settings, regardless of the weather. Instead of sitting at desks, they build shelters, climb trees, light fires under supervision and explore streams and undergrowth. The approach originated in Scandinavia, where outdoor education has a long tradition, and has since spread to many other countries, either as a complete alternative to conventional early education or as a weekly session within an ordinary school. Practitioners argue that learning outdoors develops confidence, resilience and problem-solving skills, while also giving children a practical understanding of nature that cannot be gained from books. Allowing children to take carefully managed risks, they believe, teaches them to assess danger for themselves. Research into the long-term effects is still limited, and much of it relies on observations by teachers rather than controlled comparisons. Nonetheless, studies have reported improvements in physical coordination, communication and motivation, especially among children who struggle in a traditional classroom. Some parents remain uneasy, worried about injuries or about whether their children will be ready for formal reading and writing. Supporters respond that serious accidents are rare and that the skills acquired outdoors provide a strong foundation for later learning.`,statements:[{text:`Forest schools first developed in Scandinavia.`,answer:!0},{text:`Children in forest schools stay indoors when it rains.`,answer:!1},{text:`Most research on forest schools is based on controlled comparisons.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some parents worry about their children's readiness for reading and writing.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-075`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`Lower participation is due to costs 'but also' information, confidence and commitments; grants 'rarely meet the full cost'; shorter placements bring benefits, though less pronounced.`,passage:`Student exchange programmes, which allow young people to spend a term or a year studying in another country, have expanded enormously over the past few decades. Participants frequently describe the experience as life-changing. Living with a host family or sharing accommodation with international classmates forces them to cope with unfamiliar customs, communicate in a foreign language and manage everyday problems on their own. Employers increasingly value such experience, regarding it as evidence of adaptability and independence. Surveys of former exchange students suggest that many go on to work in international roles, and a notable proportion form long-term relationships with partners they met abroad. However, participation is far from equal. Students from less advantaged backgrounds are considerably less likely to take part, partly because of the costs involved, but also because they may lack information or confidence, or have commitments such as part-time jobs. Grants exist to cover some of the expenses, yet they rarely meet the full cost of living abroad. Universities have attempted to widen access by offering shorter placements, which are easier to combine with work and family responsibilities. Early evidence suggests that even a few weeks abroad can bring significant benefits, although the effects are less pronounced than those of a full year.`,statements:[{text:`Employers often see exchange experience as a sign of adaptability.`,answer:!0},{text:`Cost is the only reason why poorer students take part less often.`,answer:!1},{text:`Grants usually cover the full cost of studying abroad.`,answer:!1},{text:`Short placements abroad can still be beneficial.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-076`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`Pocket parks fit into existing neighbourhoods; even small green areas can lower temperatures; they cost more per square metre to maintain.`,passage:`When people think of urban parks, they usually picture large green spaces with lakes, sports fields and long tree-lined paths. Yet in densely built cities, where land is scarce and expensive, planners are increasingly turning to much smaller spaces. Pocket parks, sometimes no bigger than a single building plot, are created on vacant lots, at awkward street corners or in spaces left over after construction. A few benches, some planting and perhaps a small fountain can transform a neglected site into a place where office workers eat lunch and elderly residents rest during a walk. Their modest size is precisely what makes them valuable. Because they can be fitted into existing neighbourhoods, they bring greenery within a few minutes' walk of people who would otherwise have none nearby. Studies suggest that even small green areas can lower local temperatures on hot days and absorb rainwater that would otherwise overwhelm drains. Maintenance, however, is often a challenge. Small parks scattered across a city are more costly to look after per square metre than a single large one, and some have fallen into disrepair once initial funding ran out. Many cities now involve local businesses or residents' associations in their upkeep.`,statements:[{text:`Pocket parks are often built on land that was previously unused.`,answer:!0},{text:`The text suggests small parks have no effect on local temperatures.`,answer:!1},{text:`Maintaining many small parks costs less per square metre than one large park.`,answer:!1},{text:`Residents' groups sometimes help to look after pocket parks.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-077`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`Early free bikes were stolen within weeks; dockless bikes created new problems; many trips replace walking or public transport rather than car journeys.`,passage:`Public bike-sharing schemes, in which bicycles can be picked up at one docking station and returned at another, now operate in hundreds of cities worldwide. Early attempts were often unsuccessful: in one famous case, bicycles left free for anyone to use were stolen or damaged within weeks. The modern versions rely on technology to prevent such problems, requiring users to register with a payment card and unlock bikes through an app. Their popularity has encouraged a second wave of schemes in which bicycles have no fixed stations and can be left anywhere. While this flexibility is convenient for users, it has created new difficulties, with bikes abandoned on pavements, in rivers or blocking entrances. Several cities have responded by limiting the number of operators or designating specific parking zones. Evaluations suggest that bike sharing works best when it is part of a broader transport strategy. Without safe cycle lanes, many potential users, especially women and older people, are reluctant to ride in traffic. Moreover, studies indicate that many trips replace journeys that would otherwise have been made on foot or by public transport rather than by car, which means the environmental benefits are smaller than is often claimed.`,statements:[{text:`Some early bike-sharing schemes failed because of theft and damage.`,answer:!0},{text:`Schemes without fixed stations have caused no new problems for cities.`,answer:!1},{text:`A lack of safe cycle lanes can discourage some people from using shared bikes.`,answer:!0},{text:`Most shared-bike trips replace journeys that would have been made by car.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-078`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`Opponents also mention practical costs; some cities add explanatory plaques instead of renaming; researchers value the discussion process itself.`,passage:`Street names are rarely given much thought by the people who use them every day, yet they can become the focus of fierce public debate. Many cities contain streets named after figures whose reputations have changed dramatically since the names were chosen, such as politicians, military leaders or merchants whose wealth came from activities now regarded as deeply unjust. Campaigners argue that continuing to honour such individuals sends a message about whose history matters, and that renaming streets is a way of acknowledging past wrongs. Opponents respond that names are part of a city's history and that removing them risks erasing uncomfortable truths rather than confronting them. Some also object to the practical costs, since residents and businesses must update addresses, documents and signs. Several cities have tried to find a middle way. Rather than renaming streets, they have added explanatory plaques that describe who the person was and why their legacy is controversial. Others have reserved new names for newly built streets, using them to commemorate people who were previously overlooked, such as women, immigrants and local workers. Researchers who study these disputes note that the process of discussion itself, if open and inclusive, can be as valuable as the final decision.`,statements:[{text:`Some streets are named after people whose reputations have worsened over time.`,answer:!0},{text:`Opponents of renaming are concerned only about history, not practical issues.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some cities have added information plaques instead of changing names.`,answer:!0},{text:`Researchers believe only the final decision matters in these debates.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-079`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The night economy includes theatres, shops and night workers; the first night mayor was elected by industry representatives; critics fear residents may lose out.`,passage:`Cities have traditionally been managed as if life stopped at sunset. Planning departments, transport authorities and licensing officials tended to focus on daytime activity, regarding the hours after dark mainly as a source of noise complaints and crime. That attitude has begun to change. A growing number of cities have appointed a night mayor or night-time adviser, an official whose job is to represent the interests of the evening and night economy, which includes not only bars and clubs but also theatres, restaurants, late-opening shops and the many workers who keep services running overnight. The role was pioneered in a European capital where the first night mayor was elected by representatives of the nightlife industry. Supporters argue that the night economy generates substantial employment and tax revenue, and that it deserves the same attention as other sectors. Night mayors typically act as mediators between businesses, residents and authorities, seeking solutions such as better late-night public transport, soundproofing grants and safer streets. Critics worry that the position may simply promote commercial interests at the expense of residents who want to sleep. Successful night mayors, observers say, are those who are seen as fair by both sides rather than as lobbyists for one.`,statements:[{text:`City authorities used to think of the night mainly in terms of problems.`,answer:!0},{text:`According to the text, the night economy consists only of bars and clubs.`,answer:!1},{text:`The first night mayor was chosen by people from the nightlife industry.`,answer:!0},{text:`Critics believe night mayors always protect residents' interests.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-080`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`Above a certain height more space goes to lifts and supports, 'leaving less space to rent'; record towers have sometimes coincided with the peak of a boom.`,passage:`The race to build ever taller towers shows no sign of slowing down. Each decade brings new records, and a skyscraper that ranked among the world's tallest twenty years ago may now be outside the top fifty. Developers and city leaders often justify such projects by pointing to the prestige they bring, arguing that an iconic tower can put a city on the map, attract investment and draw tourists. Architects add that tall buildings make efficient use of scarce land, allowing more people to live and work close to transport links. Yet the economics of super-tall buildings are far from straightforward. Beyond a certain height, an increasing share of each floor must be devoted to lifts, structural supports and services, leaving less space to rent. Construction costs rise steeply, and maintenance is expensive. Economists have even noted a curious historical pattern: announcements of record-breaking towers have sometimes coincided with the peak of a financial boom, shortly before a downturn. Environmental concerns are also growing. Although dense development can reduce car use, very tall glass towers consume large amounts of energy for heating, cooling and lifts. Some planners argue that medium-rise buildings offer a better balance between density and sustainability.`,statements:[{text:`Super-tall buildings lose a larger share of floor space to lifts and supports.`,answer:!0},{text:`Record-breaking towers have always been followed by economic growth.`,answer:!1},{text:`Very tall glass towers can use a great deal of energy.`,answer:!0},{text:`All planners agree that the tallest buildings are the most sustainable.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-081`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`Facilities closed because of cost; some older people avoid going out; campaigners consider existing schemes 'welcome but insufficient'.`,passage:`Public toilets might seem an unglamorous subject for urban policy, but their availability has a significant effect on who is able to use a city comfortably. In many places, the number of public facilities has declined over recent decades as local authorities, under financial pressure, closed those that were costly to clean and maintain. The consequences fall unequally. Older people, pregnant women, parents with young children, people with certain medical conditions and outdoor workers such as delivery drivers are particularly affected. Surveys indicate that some older residents limit how far they travel from home, or avoid going out altogether, because they are unsure whether they will find a toilet. Businesses such as cafés and department stores often fill the gap, but access usually depends on making a purchase, and facilities are unavailable once the premises close. Some cities have tried innovative solutions, including schemes that pay businesses a small fee to let the public use their toilets free of charge, and smartphone maps showing the nearest available facility. Campaigners argue that such measures are welcome but insufficient. In their view, public toilets should be treated as essential infrastructure, like pavements and street lighting, rather than as an optional extra to be cut whenever budgets are tight.`,statements:[{text:`The number of public toilets has fallen in many areas.`,answer:!0},{text:`The lack of toilets affects all residents equally.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some older residents stay at home because they fear not finding a toilet.`,answer:!0},{text:`Campaigners think business schemes and smartphone maps have solved the problem.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-082`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`Vinyl now earns more than CDs in some markets; the warmer sound claim 'is disputed'; some buyers do not own a record player; pressing plants cannot meet demand.`,passage:`When digital downloads and streaming services transformed the music industry, few people expected the vinyl record to survive, let alone thrive. Sales had collapsed decades earlier with the arrival of the compact disc, and by the early twenty-first century vinyl had become a niche product for collectors and DJs. Yet in recent years, sales of records have risen steadily, and in some markets they now earn more money than CDs. The reasons for the revival are partly emotional. Many buyers say they enjoy the ritual of placing a record on a turntable, studying the large cover artwork and listening to an album from beginning to end, rather than skipping between tracks. Some insist that vinyl sounds warmer than digital formats, although this claim is disputed by audio engineers. Interestingly, surveys suggest that a considerable number of buyers do not own a record player at all, purchasing albums as collectible objects or as a way of supporting their favourite artists. The boom has created practical challenges: the small number of factories still able to press records struggle to keep up with demand, which has led to long delays for musicians releasing new music.`,statements:[{text:`In some markets, vinyl records now generate more revenue than CDs.`,answer:!0},{text:`Audio engineers agree that vinyl sounds warmer than digital music.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some people buy records even though they cannot play them.`,answer:!0},{text:`Record factories can easily produce enough records to meet demand.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-083`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`For narrative texts comprehension is 'broadly similar'; readers benefit with complex material; teachers see audiobooks as support, not replacement.`,passage:`Audiobooks were once regarded as a resource mainly for people with visual impairments or for long car journeys. In the past decade, however, they have become one of the fastest-growing segments of the publishing industry. Smartphones have made it effortless to listen while commuting, exercising or doing housework, and many listeners say audiobooks have allowed them to enjoy books they would never otherwise have found time to read. Publishers have responded by investing heavily in production, hiring well-known actors as narrators and occasionally releasing works written specifically for audio. A recurring question is whether listening to a book counts as reading. Some critics suggest that listeners absorb less than readers, particularly when their attention is divided. Research on the subject is less conclusive than either side tends to assume. For straightforward narrative texts, studies have found comprehension levels to be broadly similar, while for complex material, readers seem to benefit from being able to slow down, reread difficult passages and control their own pace. Many teachers now see audiobooks as a valuable support for struggling readers, especially when combined with the printed text, rather than as a replacement for traditional reading.`,statements:[{text:`Audiobooks used to be associated with a limited group of users.`,answer:!0},{text:`Some audiobooks are now written specifically to be listened to.`,answer:!0},{text:`Studies show listeners always understand less than readers.`,answer:!1},{text:`Teachers generally want audiobooks to replace printed books.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-084`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`Late openings attract younger adults who rarely visit by day; directors want to change perceptions, 'not merely' increase ticket sales; some galleries stay closed.`,passage:`Museums have traditionally kept office hours, opening in the morning and closing in the late afternoon, just as many potential visitors finish work. In an effort to attract new audiences, a growing number of institutions now stay open late on selected evenings, often transforming their galleries with music, talks, workshops and bars. These events have proved particularly popular with younger adults, many of whom say they would rarely visit a museum during the day. For museum directors, the attraction is not merely the additional ticket sales. Late openings offer a chance to change perceptions of museums as quiet, formal places, and visitors who first come for a social evening may later return for a more conventional visit. Nonetheless, the trend has its critics. Some curators worry that loud music and crowds holding drinks distract from the works on display and may even put fragile objects at risk. Staffing is another issue, since evening events require security personnel and attendants to work longer hours, which can be expensive. Most museums have tried to strike a balance by limiting the number of late openings each month and keeping certain sensitive galleries closed during these events.`,statements:[{text:`Younger adults are among the main audiences for late openings.`,answer:!0},{text:`Directors value late openings only because they increase ticket sales.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some curators fear that late events may endanger delicate objects.`,answer:!0},{text:`Most museums open every gallery during evening events.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-085`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`Sales have 'grown considerably'; many new games come from small studios funded by crowdfunding; customers stay long but spend little.`,passage:`At a time when so much entertainment takes place on screens, it might seem surprising that board games are enjoying a revival. Yet sales of tabletop games have grown considerably over the past fifteen years, and cafés where customers pay a small fee to play from a library of hundreds of games have opened in cities around the world. The games themselves have changed. Alongside traditional favourites, there is now a vast range of titles involving complex strategy, cooperation between players or elaborate storytelling, many of them designed by small independent studios and funded through online crowdfunding campaigns. Enthusiasts explain the appeal largely in social terms. Sitting around a table with friends, they say, offers a kind of face-to-face interaction that online games cannot replicate, and the absence of screens is itself part of the attraction. Board game cafés also provide a relaxed setting for people who find bars or clubs uncomfortable. Running such a café is not easy, however. Customers often stay for hours while buying relatively little, so owners must find ways to make each table profitable, for example by charging by the hour or offering food. Staff need an encyclopaedic knowledge of rules in order to explain games to newcomers.`,statements:[{text:`Sales of board games have fallen as screen entertainment has grown.`,answer:!1},{text:`Many modern board games are produced by small independent companies.`,answer:!0},{text:`Enthusiasts say the lack of screens is part of the appeal.`,answer:!0},{text:`Board game cafés make money easily because customers spend a lot.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-086`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`Some cities require licences; codes of conduct reduce complaints if 'supported by occasional enforcement'; some buskers report higher earnings since accepting card payments.`,passage:`Street performers have entertained passers-by for centuries, but their relationship with city authorities has often been uneasy. Some cities allow busking freely, regarding it as part of a lively urban atmosphere, while others require performers to obtain a licence, audition for a place or perform only at designated spots and times. Supporters of regulation argue that it is necessary to balance the interests of performers with those of residents and shopkeepers, who may have to listen to the same songs repeated for hours, often through loudspeakers. Musicians, for their part, complain that strict rules can be applied unpredictably and that licensing fees may exclude those who perform precisely because they need the money. Several cities have attempted to find a compromise by introducing voluntary codes of conduct, drawn up together with performers, which set limits on volume and on the length of time spent in one location. Evidence suggests these codes can reduce complaints considerably, as long as they are supported by occasional enforcement. Busking has also been affected by changing technology. As fewer people carry cash, many performers now accept payments by card or smartphone, and some report that their earnings have actually risen as a result.`,statements:[{text:`All cities treat street performance in the same way.`,answer:!1},{text:`Residents may be disturbed by songs repeated for long periods.`,answer:!0},{text:`Voluntary codes work best with no enforcement at all.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some buskers say they earn more since accepting electronic payments.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-087`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`Workplace attitudes 'have changed more slowly'; some recruiters still view tattoos negatively; laser removal is expensive and may need many sessions.`,passage:`Only a few generations ago, tattoos in many Western societies were associated mainly with sailors, soldiers and people on the margins of society. Today they are worn by a substantial proportion of young adults, including doctors, teachers and lawyers, and tattoo studios can be found on ordinary high streets. Sociologists attribute this shift to several factors, including the influence of celebrities and athletes, improvements in hygiene and technique, and a growing emphasis on self-expression. For many wearers, a tattoo marks a significant life event, commemorates a loved one or expresses an identity they wish to make visible. Attitudes in the workplace have changed more slowly. Although many employers now accept visible tattoos, surveys suggest that some recruiters, particularly in customer-facing or traditional professions, still view them negatively. As a result, a number of people choose designs that can easily be covered by clothing. The popularity of tattoos has also fuelled demand for their removal. Laser treatment has become more effective, but it remains expensive, often painful and may require many sessions over a period of months. Dermatologists advise those considering a tattoo to think carefully about its location and design, since tastes that seem permanent at twenty may look very different at fifty.`,statements:[{text:`Tattoos were once linked to a limited range of social groups.`,answer:!0},{text:`Workplace attitudes towards tattoos have changed faster than social attitudes.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some people choose tattoos that can be hidden at work.`,answer:!0},{text:`Laser removal is now quick, cheap and painless.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-088`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`Chess is recognised by the IOC but has never been included in the Games; studies recorded elevated heart rates; a television drama boosted sales of sets.`,passage:`Is chess a sport? The question may sound frivolous, but it has practical consequences, since recognition as a sport can bring access to public funding, inclusion in multi-sport events and tax advantages for clubs. The International Olympic Committee recognised chess as a sport many years ago, although it has never been included in the Olympic Games themselves. Supporters of the classification emphasise the demands of top-level competition. Elite players prepare physically as well as mentally, since games can last for several hours and require intense concentration throughout. Studies have even recorded elevated heart rates and substantial energy use among players during important tournaments. Sceptics respond that physical activity is surely central to the definition of a sport, and that if chess qualifies, so too might card games or crossword competitions. Meanwhile, the game itself has experienced a remarkable surge in popularity. Online platforms allow millions of people to play at any hour against opponents around the world, and a popular television drama about a fictional chess prodigy prompted a sharp increase in sales of chess sets. Live broadcasts of top players, accompanied by expert commentary, now attract audiences comparable to those of some traditional sports.`,statements:[{text:`Chess has been played at the Olympic Games.`,answer:!1},{text:`Top chess players may prepare physically for competitions.`,answer:!0},{text:`Sceptics argue that sport should involve physical activity.`,answer:!0},{text:`Online chess broadcasts attract only very small audiences.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-089`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The events are free and run by volunteers; they attract many previously inactive people; some people only volunteer and gain social benefits.`,passage:`Every Saturday morning, in parks across many countries, thousands of people gather to run, jog or walk a five-kilometre course. The events are free, organised entirely by volunteers and open to anyone, regardless of age or ability. Participants register once, receive a personal barcode and can then take part as often as they like, receiving their time by message later the same day. What began as a small gathering of friends in a single park has grown into a global movement involving millions of registered participants. Its appeal lies partly in its inclusiveness. Unlike organised races, which can be expensive and intimidating for beginners, the weekly runs place no emphasis on winning, and many events deliberately include a volunteer who walks at the back to ensure that nobody finishes last alone. Health researchers have taken a keen interest, since the events appear to attract many people who previously did little exercise. Surveys of participants report improvements not only in fitness but also in mental well-being and feelings of belonging to a community. Interestingly, some people take part exclusively as volunteers, marshalling the course or recording times, and say they gain similar social benefits without running at all.`,statements:[{text:`Participants must pay a fee for each event.`,answer:!1},{text:`The events started on a small scale.`,answer:!0},{text:`A volunteer often walks at the back so that no one finishes alone.`,answer:!0},{text:`Only those who run receive social benefits from the events.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-090`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`Women were banned from members' grounds for half a century; many players outside top leagues have other jobs; much sports science has been based on male athletes.`,passage:`Women's football has a longer history than many people realise. In the early twentieth century, some women's matches in Britain attracted crowds of tens of thousands, until the national football association banned women from playing on its members' grounds, a restriction that remained in place for half a century. In recent years, however, the women's game has grown at an extraordinary pace. International tournaments now fill large stadiums, broadcast audiences have reached record levels and sponsors who once showed little interest are competing for deals. The increase in visibility has had a noticeable effect on participation, with many more girls joining clubs and school teams. Yet considerable inequalities remain. Outside the top leagues, many professional players earn modest salaries and combine football with other jobs, and facilities, medical support and coaching are often inferior to those available to men. Researchers have also pointed out that much sports science, including the design of boots and approaches to injury prevention, has been based on male athletes, which may help to explain why certain injuries appear more common among women. Campaigners argue that sustained investment, rather than occasional enthusiasm around major tournaments, is essential if the recent progress is to last.`,statements:[{text:`Women's football was popular with large crowds in the early twentieth century.`,answer:!0},{text:`The ban on women playing at members' grounds was lifted after a few months.`,answer:!1},{text:`Many players outside the top leagues need other jobs.`,answer:!0},{text:`Sports equipment has usually been designed with female athletes in mind.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-091`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`Travel and familiarity explain 'only a small proportion'; in empty stadiums the advantage shrank but results 'were not uniform', so crowds are 'not the only' factor.`,passage:`Sports fans have always believed that playing at home brings an advantage, and statistics broadly confirm their belief. Across many sports, home teams win more often than visiting teams, although the size of the effect varies considerably between sports and has declined in some over recent decades. Explaining why the advantage exists has proved more complicated. Travel fatigue and familiarity with local conditions play a part, but researchers have found that these factors account for only a small proportion of the effect. A more surprising explanation concerns referees. Several studies have suggested that officials, influenced perhaps unconsciously by the noise of a home crowd, tend to make slightly more decisions in favour of the home side. A remarkable opportunity to test this theory arose when many professional matches were played in empty stadiums during the pandemic. Analyses of those games found that the home advantage shrank noticeably in several leagues, and that the difference in the number of penalties and cards given to home and away teams largely disappeared. However, the results were not uniform: in some competitions, home teams continued to win more often, suggesting that crowds are an important factor but not the only one.`,statements:[{text:`The home advantage is the same in every sport.`,answer:!1},{text:`Travel fatigue explains most of the home advantage.`,answer:!1},{text:`Matches without spectators provided a way to test ideas about referees.`,answer:!0},{text:`The text suggests that crowds are not the only reason for home advantage.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-092`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`Bouldering is done without ropes, above mats; climbing first appeared at the Olympics in 2021; falls and overuse can still cause injuries.`,passage:`Climbing was once regarded as a dangerous pursuit for a small group of adventurers willing to travel to remote cliffs and mountains. The opening of indoor climbing centres in towns and cities has changed that image completely. Bouldering, a form of climbing on short walls without ropes, above thick protective mats, has proved especially popular, since it requires little equipment and can be learned quickly. Beginners can try a few routes on a first visit and see visible progress within weeks. Many centres have become social hubs, with cafés, yoga classes and evening sessions where people meet new friends. Climbing made its first appearance at the Olympic Games in 2021, which further boosted interest among young people. Physiotherapists note that the activity develops strength, flexibility and balance, and that solving the physical puzzles presented by each route also provides mental stimulation. Nevertheless, indoor climbing is not free from risk. Falls onto mats can still cause injuries, particularly to ankles and wrists, and overuse injuries to fingers are common among those who increase their training too quickly. Instructors recommend that newcomers learn how to fall safely and warm up properly before attempting difficult routes.`,statements:[{text:`Bouldering involves climbing with ropes on very high walls.`,answer:!1},{text:`Beginners can see improvement fairly quickly.`,answer:!0},{text:`Climbing has been part of the Olympic Games for many decades.`,answer:!1},{text:`Increasing training too fast can lead to finger injuries.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-093`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`Many new referees quit within two years, mainly because of abuse; body cameras appear to reduce abuse; officials say lasting change needs a cultural shift.`,passage:`Every weekend, thousands of amateur football matches take place in parks and on school fields, but in many countries there are no longer enough referees to officiate them all. Local associations report that large numbers of new referees give up within their first two years, and some matches have had to be cancelled or supervised by volunteers with no formal qualifications. When asked why they quit, referees most frequently mention abuse from players, coaches and spectators, which ranges from insults shouted from the touchline to, in rare cases, physical assault. Young referees, some of them only teenagers, are particularly vulnerable. The low payments for officiating grassroots matches do little to compensate for such experiences. Associations have introduced a variety of measures in response. Some require teams to nominate a respect officer responsible for the behaviour of their supporters, while others have experimented with body cameras worn by referees, which appear to make abusive behaviour less likely. Mentoring schemes pairing new referees with experienced colleagues have also helped to reduce the number who drop out. Yet officials insist that lasting change depends on a cultural shift, in which players and spectators come to regard referees as essential participants rather than as obstacles to victory.`,statements:[{text:`Some amateur matches have been cancelled because of a lack of referees.`,answer:!0},{text:`Referees mainly quit because they find the rules too complicated.`,answer:!1},{text:`Body cameras seem to reduce abuse towards referees.`,answer:!0},{text:`Officials believe new technology alone can solve the problem.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-094`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`Insects need less land, water and feed than cattle; the market grew 'more slowly than many investors predicted'; researchers think information alone won't change habits.`,passage:`Insects are a regular part of the diet for an estimated two billion people, particularly in parts of Asia, Africa and Latin America, yet in many Western countries the idea of eating them still provokes disgust. Advocates argue that this reluctance needs to be overcome. Compared with cattle, insects such as crickets and mealworms require far less land, water and feed to produce the same amount of protein, and they generate considerably lower greenhouse gas emissions. Some species can even be raised on food waste. Insects are also nutritious, containing high-quality protein, healthy fats and minerals such as iron and zinc. To make them more acceptable to cautious consumers, producers increasingly grind insects into flour, which can then be added to pasta, bread, energy bars and snacks without being visible. Despite these efforts, the market has grown more slowly than many investors predicted, and several start-ups have closed. Prices remain relatively high because production has not yet reached a large scale. Researchers who study food choices argue that information about environmental benefits is unlikely to change habits on its own. In their view, familiarity is crucial, and people are most likely to accept new foods when they are tasty, convenient and served by others they trust.`,statements:[{text:`Insects require less land and water than cattle to produce protein.`,answer:!0},{text:`Insect flour is used to hide insects in foods such as pasta.`,answer:!0},{text:`The insect food market has grown faster than investors expected.`,answer:!1},{text:`Researchers believe environmental information is enough to change eating habits.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-095`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`Simple labels are more effective than complex ones; the effect on overall diet is modest; some companies reformulated recipes; many schemes remain voluntary.`,passage:`Walk down the aisle of any supermarket and you will find packaging covered in information: ingredients, nutritional values, health claims and, increasingly, colour-coded labels indicating whether a product is high or low in fat, sugar and salt. The aim of such labels is to help shoppers make healthier choices quickly, without needing to study detailed tables of figures. Evidence suggests that simple front-of-pack labels are more effective than complex ones, since most shoppers spend only a few seconds deciding what to buy. Some research has found that clear labelling leads consumers to choose slightly healthier products, although the effect on overall diet appears modest. Interestingly, labels may have a greater impact on manufacturers than on shoppers. Faced with the prospect of displaying a red warning on their best-selling products, a number of companies have quietly reformulated recipes to reduce sugar or salt. Food producers have not always welcomed these schemes, arguing that labels based on a standard portion can be misleading, for instance by classing olive oil or cheese alongside sweets and fizzy drinks. As a result, labelling systems differ from country to country, and in many places they remain voluntary rather than compulsory.`,statements:[{text:`Complex labels with detailed figures are the most effective.`,answer:!1},{text:`Clear labelling has transformed people's diets dramatically.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some manufacturers have changed recipes to avoid warning labels.`,answer:!0},{text:`In many countries, colour-coded labels are not compulsory.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-096`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`Sales levelled off or declined; prices stay higher than meat; some products contain much salt; experts expect long-term growth.`,passage:`Plant-based alternatives to meat have existed for decades, but early versions were often bland products aimed mainly at vegetarians. A new generation of burgers, sausages and minced products, developed with considerable scientific effort and investment, aims at a much larger market: people who enjoy meat but would like to eat less of it for environmental, health or ethical reasons. Some of these products imitate the taste, texture and even the appearance of beef remarkably closely. After an initial period of rapid growth, during which plant-based burgers appeared on the menus of major fast-food chains, sales in several countries began to level off or even decline. Analysts have suggested several explanations. Prices have generally remained higher than those of conventional meat, which makes the products less attractive when household budgets are tight. Some consumers have also expressed concern about the long lists of ingredients, regarding the products as highly processed. Nutritionists point out that being made from plants does not automatically make a product healthy, since some alternatives contain high levels of salt. Nevertheless, most experts expect the market to keep growing in the long term, particularly if improved technology allows producers to reduce costs.`,statements:[{text:`New plant-based products target people who also eat meat.`,answer:!0},{text:`Sales of plant-based meat have risen continuously in every country.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some consumers worry that plant-based products are heavily processed.`,answer:!0},{text:`Nutritionists say all plant-based products are healthy.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-097`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`High-priced products are 'particularly attractive targets'; harm is mostly financial but 'occasionally' dangerous; testing every product is impossible, so experts emphasise prevention.`,passage:`Food fraud, the deliberate misrepresentation of food for financial gain, is as old as trade itself, but globalised supply chains have created new opportunities for it. Products that command high prices are particularly attractive targets. Extra virgin olive oil may be diluted with cheaper oils, honey can be mixed with sugar syrup, and expensive fish species are sometimes replaced with less valuable ones. Spices such as saffron, which is sold in tiny quantities at very high prices, have been bulked up with dyed plant material. In most cases, the main victims are consumers' wallets rather than their health, but fraud can occasionally be dangerous, as when undeclared ingredients cause allergic reactions. Detecting fraud has become a scientific discipline in its own right. Laboratories use techniques such as chemical fingerprinting and DNA analysis to establish a product's origin and composition, comparing samples with databases of authentic foods. Yet testing every product is impossible, and fraudsters continually adapt their methods to avoid detection. Experts therefore emphasise prevention, encouraging companies to know their suppliers, shorten supply chains where possible and be suspicious of prices that seem too good to be true. Consumers, too, can help by buying from trusted sources and reporting anything suspicious.`,statements:[{text:`Expensive foods are more likely to be targeted by fraudsters.`,answer:!0},{text:`Food fraud usually causes serious harm to consumers' health.`,answer:!1},{text:`Laboratories can use DNA analysis to check where food comes from.`,answer:!0},{text:`Experts believe testing all food products is the best solution.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-098`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`Small claims are designed for use without lawyers; hearings are informal; many disputes settle at mediation; many people are unaware of the system.`,passage:`Going to court is often imagined as an expensive and intimidating process involving lawyers in formal dress and lengthy proceedings. For many everyday disputes, however, a simpler route exists. Small claims procedures allow individuals and businesses to resolve disagreements involving relatively modest sums, such as a faulty product, an unpaid invoice or a deposit that a landlord refuses to return, without the costs of a full trial. The procedures are designed to be used without legal representation. Forms can usually be completed online, hearings are typically informal and held in an ordinary room rather than a traditional courtroom, and judges are expected to help both sides explain their case. In many systems, the party that loses is required to pay only limited costs, which reduces the financial risk of bringing a claim. Before a hearing takes place, both sides are frequently encouraged to try mediation, and a substantial proportion of disputes are settled at this stage. Despite these advantages, research suggests that many people with valid claims never use the system, either because they are unaware of it or because they assume that legal action is beyond their means. Consumer organisations argue that wider publicity could encourage more people to enforce their rights.`,statements:[{text:`Small claims procedures are intended for disputes involving large sums of money.`,answer:!1},{text:`People can use the small claims process without hiring a lawyer.`,answer:!0},{text:`Many disputes are resolved before reaching a hearing.`,answer:!0},{text:`Almost everyone with a valid claim makes use of the system.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-099`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The number of units depends on seriousness, their value on income; the system needs reliable income data; wealth from assets may allow manipulation.`,passage:`In most legal systems, a fine for a particular offence is a fixed sum, the same for a wealthy executive as for a student or a pensioner. Critics argue that this approach is fundamentally unfair, since a penalty that represents a trivial expense for one person may cause genuine hardship for another. Several countries, particularly in northern Europe, have adopted a different model known as day fines. Under this system, the seriousness of an offence determines a number of units, while the value of each unit depends on the offender's daily income. As a result, two people who commit the same offence may pay vastly different amounts, and newspapers occasionally report speeding fines running into tens of thousands of euros for high earners. Supporters claim that day fines make punishment equally felt across society and that they deter wealthy offenders more effectively. Implementing the system, however, requires reliable information about people's incomes, which is easier in countries where tax records are accessible to the authorities. Some argue that it is also open to manipulation by those whose wealth comes from assets rather than salaries. Even so, interest in income-based fines has grown, with several cities and states examining whether they could reduce the burden of penalties on low-income households.`,statements:[{text:`Under day fines, everyone pays the same amount for the same offence.`,answer:!1},{text:`The value of a unit depends on how much the offender earns.`,answer:!0},{text:`Day fines are easier to apply where authorities can access tax information.`,answer:!0},{text:`Everyone agrees that the system cannot be manipulated.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-100`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`Without a will, fixed rules apply; unmarried partners may have no automatic right in some systems; an outdated will can cause 'almost as many problems as having none'.`,passage:`Surveys repeatedly show that a large proportion of adults have not made a will, even though most agree that it would be sensible to do so. Some people assume they do not own enough to make it worthwhile; others simply find it uncomfortable to think about their own death. Yet dying without a will can create considerable difficulties for those left behind. In such cases, property is distributed according to fixed legal rules, which may not reflect the deceased person's wishes. Unmarried partners, for example, may have no automatic right to inherit in some legal systems, however long the couple lived together, while relatives the deceased rarely saw may receive a share. Disputes between family members can also become more likely, and resolving them may take months or even years. Lawyers recommend reviewing a will after major life events such as marriage, divorce or the birth of a child, since an outdated document can cause almost as many problems as having none at all. Increasingly, people must also consider their digital assets, from online bank accounts to photographs stored in the cloud. Without clear instructions and access details, relatives may find it difficult, or even impossible, to retrieve them.`,statements:[{text:`Most adults who have no will believe making one is a bad idea.`,answer:!1},{text:`In some systems, an unmarried partner may not automatically inherit.`,answer:!0},{text:`Lawyers say an old will is always better than no will at all.`,answer:!1},{text:`Relatives may have trouble accessing a dead person's online accounts.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-101`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says pay "becomes less important once a basic level of comfort has been reached", dissatisfaction is a source of resentment "when employees believe that colleagues... are better rewarded", and transparency "reveal[s] inequalities".`,passage:`The relationship between salary and job satisfaction is more complicated than many employers assume. Surveys consistently show that pay becomes less important once a basic level of comfort has been reached; factors such as autonomy, recognition and relationships with colleagues have a stronger influence on motivation. Nevertheless, dissatisfaction with pay is a powerful source of resentment, particularly when employees believe that colleagues doing similar work are better rewarded. Transparent pay structures, some firms have found, reduce this resentment, even if they reveal inequalities, because they replace rumour with clear criteria.`,statements:[{text:`Pay is always the most important factor in job satisfaction.`,answer:!1},{text:`Dissatisfaction with pay can cause resentment when others seem better rewarded.`,answer:!0},{text:`Transparent pay structures hide inequalities.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-102`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says it was "the most numerous bird", scientists believe it was vulnerable "not only to hunting but also to the loss of the forests", and "a few researchers have even proposed using genetic techniques".`,passage:`The extinction of the passenger pigeon, once the most numerous bird in North America, is often cited as a cautionary tale. Flocks so large that they were said to darken the sky for days were hunted on an industrial scale during the nineteenth century, their meat sold cheaply in city markets. Within a few decades, the population collapsed; the last known individual died in a zoo in 1914. What is striking is how quickly the decline occurred and how few contemporaries understood that it was happening. Some scientists now believe that the species was vulnerable not only to hunting but also to the loss of the forests on which it depended for food. A few researchers have even proposed using genetic techniques to bring the bird back, though others argue that the resources would be better spent protecting living species.`,statements:[{text:`The passenger pigeon was once extremely numerous.`,answer:!0},{text:`Hunting was the only cause of its extinction according to all scientists.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some researchers have proposed bringing the species back.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-103`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says employees are promoted "until they reach a level at which they are no longer competent", the idea was "originally offered as a satire", and companies created "parallel career paths".`,passage:`A well-known phenomenon in organisations is the "Peter Principle", which holds that employees are promoted on the basis of their performance in their current job until they reach a level at which they are no longer competent. At that point they stop being promoted and remain in a position for which they are ill-suited. Although originally offered as a satire, the idea has received some empirical support: a study of sales staff found that the best salespeople were often promoted to management, where their skills were less relevant, and that their teams subsequently performed worse. Companies have responded by creating parallel career paths, allowing talented specialists to advance without managing others.`,statements:[{text:`The Peter Principle suggests that people are promoted until they reach a level of incompetence.`,answer:!0},{text:`The principle was first presented as a serious scientific theory.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some companies have created career paths that do not require managing others.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-104`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says airports "look remarkably alike", some architects "designed terminals that reflect local landscapes", and whether features enrich the experience "is open to debate".`,passage:`Air travel has transformed our perception of distance, but it has also created a peculiar kind of placelessness. Airports around the world look remarkably alike, with the same duty-free shops, the same signs and the same smell of coffee, so that arriving in a foreign country can feel like walking from one lounge into another. Some architects have tried to resist this by designing terminals that reflect local landscapes and materials, and certain airports have become destinations in their own right, with gardens, museums and even waterfalls. Whether such features enrich the experience or simply add to the spectacle is open to debate. What seems clear is that passengers increasingly regard the journey itself, rather than merely the destination, as part of the holiday.`,statements:[{text:`Airports around the world tend to look similar.`,answer:!0},{text:`No airport has been designed to reflect its local setting.`,answer:!1},{text:`The text states that extra features in airports definitely enrich passengers' experience.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-105`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says people "were more worried about losing their independence", "most believed that attitudes... were improving", and happiness "ris[es] again in later years".`,passage:`A recent survey of people's attitudes to ageing found that many were more worried about losing their independence than about dying. Respondents said they feared becoming a burden on their families and being forced to leave their homes. At the same time, most believed that attitudes to older people were improving, and many looked forward to having more time for hobbies and travel. Gerontologists note that happiness tends to follow a U-shaped curve through life, dipping in middle age and rising again in later years.`,statements:[{text:`Many respondents feared losing independence.`,answer:!0},{text:`Most believed attitudes to older people were worsening.`,answer:!1},{text:`Happiness may rise again in later years.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-106`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says letter writing "has almost vanished", enthusiasts value "waiting days for a reply", and the spread beyond a minority "is doubtful".`,passage:`The art of letter writing, once central to personal communication, has almost vanished, replaced by messages that are dashed off in seconds and forgotten as quickly. A growing number of people, however, are rediscovering the pleasure of handwritten correspondence. They describe the deliberateness of choosing paper, composing sentences that cannot be deleted and waiting days for a reply. A letter, they argue, has a physical presence that a message lacks: it carries the writer's handwriting, perhaps a faint scent, the evidence of a moment of time taken. Recipients often keep letters for decades. Whether such enthusiasm will spread beyond a committed minority is doubtful, but its existence suggests that convenience is not the only value people seek in communication.`,statements:[{text:`Letter writing has almost disappeared.`,answer:!0},{text:`Letter enthusiasts value the speed of handwritten correspondence.`,answer:!1},{text:`The writer is confident that the revival will become widespread.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-107`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says it "has transformed the way many people plan", operators "have been quick to respond", and critics argue it "encourages a shallow, competitive attitude".`,passage:`The concept of the "bucket list" has transformed the way many people plan their travels. Rather than choosing a destination for rest or pleasure, travellers draw up a list of experiences to be ticked off before they die: seeing the northern lights, walking the Inca Trail, sailing round the Greek islands. Tour operators have been quick to respond, offering packages that promise to deliver a series of such highlights. Critics argue that this approach encourages a shallow, competitive attitude to travel, in which photographs matter more than encounters. Others reply that a list can provide a spur to people who might otherwise never leave home, and that the motives for travel have always been mixed.`,statements:[{text:`The "bucket list" idea has changed how some people plan travel.`,answer:!0},{text:`Tour operators have ignored the trend.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some critics think bucket-list travel can be shallow.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-108`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says park walkers "showed lower levels of stress hormones", benefits come "not only from exercise", and doctors "prescribe time outdoors".`,passage:`A walk in the countryside is often recommended as a cure for stress, and there is growing evidence to support the advice. In experiments, people who spent half an hour walking in a park showed lower levels of stress hormones than those who walked along a busy street. The benefits seem to come not only from exercise but from the sights and sounds of nature, which allow the mind to rest from the constant demands of city life. Planners are therefore urged to include green spaces in new developments, and doctors in some places now prescribe time outdoors for patients with anxiety.`,statements:[{text:`Walking in a park lowered stress hormones more than walking on a busy street.`,answer:!0},{text:`The benefits come only from exercise.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some doctors prescribe time outdoors.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-109`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says "bones lose density", crews "exercise for about two hours a day", and astronauts' changes are "accelerated versions of changes that occur with ageing".`,passage:`When astronauts spend long periods in space, their bodies change. Without gravity, muscles weaken and bones lose density at a rate comparable to that of an elderly person with osteoporosis. Fluids shift towards the head, causing puffy faces and sometimes problems with vision. To counteract these effects, crews exercise for about two hours a day on specially designed machines. Even so, some changes take months to reverse after a return to Earth. Medical researchers regard astronauts as a valuable group to study, since the processes they experience are accelerated versions of changes that occur with ageing and prolonged inactivity.`,statements:[{text:`Bones lose density in space.`,answer:!0},{text:`Astronauts avoid exercise in space.`,answer:!1},{text:`Researchers study astronauts to understand ageing.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-110`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says resistance occurs "when bacteria evolve to survive", "few new antibiotics are being developed", and patients should "complete the course".`,passage:`Antibiotic resistance occurs when bacteria evolve to survive the drugs designed to kill them. It is accelerated by the overuse of antibiotics in medicine and agriculture, and by patients who stop taking a course of treatment as soon as they feel better. The danger is that infections that were once easily cured may become untreatable. Few new antibiotics are being developed, since pharmaceutical companies find them less profitable than drugs for chronic conditions that patients take for life. Public health officials therefore urge doctors to prescribe antibiotics only when necessary and patients to complete the course they are given.`,statements:[{text:`Resistance arises when bacteria evolve to survive drugs.`,answer:!0},{text:`Many new antibiotics are being developed.`,answer:!1},{text:`Patients are urged to finish the course of antibiotics.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-111`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says patients "may have to travel for hours", telemedicine "requires reliable internet access", and "some conditions cannot be diagnosed without a physical examination".`,passage:`In rural areas of many countries, access to healthcare is limited by distance. A woman who needs a specialist may have to travel for hours by bus, and for those without a car the journey can be prohibitive. Telemedicine, in which doctors consult patients by video link, offers a partial solution, and its use expanded dramatically during the pandemic. Patients appreciate the convenience, and trials suggest that for many conditions the quality of care is comparable. Yet the technology is no panacea: it requires reliable internet access, which is often poorest in precisely the areas most in need, and some conditions cannot be diagnosed without a physical examination.`,statements:[{text:`Rural patients may have to travel for hours to see a specialist.`,answer:!0},{text:`Telemedicine requires no internet access.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some conditions cannot be diagnosed without a physical examination.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-112`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says "each year represents a smaller fraction of our total life", "adult life is dominated by routine, which leaves fewer distinct memories", and "neither explanation has been conclusively proved".`,passage:`Everyone has a favourite explanation for why time seems to pass more quickly as we grow older. One theory holds that each year represents a smaller fraction of our total life, so a year seems shorter to a fifty-year-old than to a five-year-old. Another suggests that novelty slows our perception of time: in childhood, almost everything is new and memorable, whereas adult life is dominated by routine, which leaves fewer distinct memories. If so, there may be a remedy: seeking out new experiences, such as travelling to unfamiliar places or learning new skills, could make time feel richer and slower. Neither explanation has been conclusively proved.`,statements:[{text:`One theory is that each year is a smaller fraction of a longer life.`,answer:!0},{text:`According to the second theory, routine makes time seem to pass slowly.`,answer:!1},{text:`Both explanations have been conclusively proved.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-113`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says misunderstandings arise from "attitudes to hierarchy, time and directness", the training "does not aim to impose a single model", and firms "report fewer conflicts".`,passage:`Companies that operate internationally face the challenge of managing staff from different cultures. Misunderstandings can arise from differences in attitudes to hierarchy, time and directness. In some cultures, disagreement with a superior is considered disrespectful; in others it is a sign of engagement. Firms that provide training in cultural awareness report fewer conflicts and better cooperation. The training does not aim to impose a single model but to encourage curiosity and flexibility, recognising that no culture has a monopoly on good management.`,statements:[{text:`Cultures differ in their attitudes to hierarchy and time.`,answer:!0},{text:`Cultural training aims to impose a single model.`,answer:!1},{text:`Firms providing training report fewer conflicts.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-114`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says "sorry" is "a social lubricant rather than a genuine expression of regret", visitors "find the practice puzzling", and commentators argue "its overuse devalues it".`,passage:`The habit of apologising has become something of a cultural stereotype in Britain, where people are said to say sorry when someone else treads on their foot. Linguists, however, suggest that the word often serves as a social lubricant rather than a genuine expression of regret. It softens requests, signals politeness and avoids conflict, and listeners generally understand this without confusion. Visitors from other cultures sometimes find the practice puzzling, even insincere, since they associate an apology with admission of fault. In recent years, some commentators have urged that the word be kept for occasions when it is really meant, arguing that its overuse devalues it. Others reply that language is not obliged to be literal.`,statements:[{text:`Linguists say the British "sorry" often functions as a social courtesy.`,answer:!0},{text:`Foreign visitors always find the habit natural.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some commentators think overuse reduces the value of the word.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-115`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says it "dates only from the 1840s", the Duchess "is said to have asked", and "many people still enjoy it".`,passage:`The tradition of afternoon tea is often assumed to be centuries old, but it dates only from the 1840s, when the Duchess of Bedford is said to have asked for tea and cakes to be brought to her in the late afternoon to stave off hunger before a late dinner. She began inviting friends to join her, and the habit spread through fashionable society. By the end of the century, afternoon tea had become a social institution, with hotels offering elaborate versions on tiered stands. Today, it is as much a tourist attraction as a custom, though many people still enjoy it on special occasions.`,statements:[{text:`Afternoon tea is a very old tradition.`,answer:!1},{text:`The Duchess of Bedford is said to have started the habit.`,answer:!0},{text:`Afternoon tea is no longer enjoyed by anyone.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-116`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says "pilots cannot steer in the usual sense", flights are "at dawn", and the crew "must follow the balloon by road".`,passage:`Hot-air ballooning offers a view of the landscape that no other form of transport can match. Balloons travel with the wind, so pilots cannot steer in the usual sense; they rise or descend to find a current moving in the desired direction. Flights usually take place at dawn, when the air is calm. The experience is silent, apart from the occasional roar of the burner, and passengers often describe a sense of peacefulness. Landing, however, can be bumpy, and the crew must follow the balloon by road to collect it and its passengers, who are traditionally greeted with a glass of sparkling wine.`,statements:[{text:`Pilots steer balloons in the usual way.`,answer:!1},{text:`Flights usually take place at dawn.`,answer:!0},{text:`The crew follow the balloon by road.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-117`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says "millions of visitors", "only a handful of unexplained recoveries", and volunteers "help to bathe and transport the pilgrims".`,passage:`The pilgrimage town of Lourdes in southern France receives millions of visitors each year, many of them sick or disabled and hoping for a cure. Volunteers, often young people, help to bathe and transport the pilgrims, and the atmosphere is one of quiet devotion. Medical authorities have confirmed only a handful of unexplained recoveries, but believers say that the greatest healing is spiritual. The town depends heavily on the pilgrims, with shops selling candles and bottled water from the spring. Visitors who do not share the faith often report that they find the experience moving.`,statements:[{text:`Lourdes receives millions of visitors each year.`,answer:!0},{text:`Medical authorities have confirmed hundreds of unexplained recoveries.`,answer:!1},{text:`Volunteers help to transport pilgrims.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-118`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says active people "reported greater satisfaction", they "often reported being tired afterwards", and watching television "is not harmful in moderation".`,passage:`A study of how people use their free time found that those who spent it actively, for example by pursuing a hobby or meeting friends, reported greater satisfaction than those who spent it passively in front of a screen. This held true even though the active group often reported being tired afterwards. The researchers suggest that the effort involved in an activity contributes to a sense of achievement. They caution, however, that rest is also necessary, and that watching television is not harmful in moderation. The key, they say, is to be deliberate about how time is spent rather than allowing it to drift.`,statements:[{text:`Active leisure was linked with greater satisfaction.`,answer:!0},{text:`The active group never felt tired.`,answer:!1},{text:`The researchers say television is always harmful.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-119`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says it lets a firm "concentrate on its core activities", some firms "brought functions back in-house", and analysts advise to "retain control of anything central to the brand".`,passage:`Outsourcing, the practice of paying another company to carry out tasks such as payroll or customer service, can reduce costs and allow a firm to concentrate on its core activities. Yet the arrangement carries risks. Quality may suffer if the contractor does not share the company's standards, and sensitive information may be exposed. Some firms that outsourced enthusiastically have later brought functions back in-house after discovering that savings were smaller than expected and that customers disliked dealing with remote call centres. The lesson, analysts suggest, is to outsource selectively and to retain control of anything central to the brand.`,statements:[{text:`Outsourcing can allow a firm to focus on core activities.`,answer:!0},{text:`All firms that outsourced have been satisfied.`,answer:!1},{text:`Analysts recommend keeping control of central functions.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-120`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says visitors are "concentrated on a few beaches in the summer months", the authorities "introduced limits on the number of tourist beds", and campaigners say the restrictions "do not go far enough".`,passage:`The Mediterranean island of Mallorca receives millions of visitors each year, most of them concentrated on a few beaches in the summer months. Residents have long complained of overcrowding, water shortages and the erosion of their traditional way of life. Recently, the local authorities have introduced limits on the number of tourist beds, banned the sale of alcohol at certain hours in the busiest resorts and encouraged visitors to explore the quieter interior in spring and autumn. The measures have met with mixed reactions: hoteliers fear for their livelihoods, while campaigners say that the restrictions do not go far enough. Everyone agrees that the existing model of mass tourism cannot continue indefinitely.`,statements:[{text:`Most visitors to Mallorca concentrate on a few beaches in summer.`,answer:!0},{text:`The authorities have introduced limits on tourist beds.`,answer:!0},{text:`Everyone agrees that the restrictions are sufficient.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-121`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the far side "receives just as much sunlight", it "was unseen until... 1959", and scientists "are still debating" the difference.`,passage:`Most people believe that the moon has a dark side that is never lit by the sun. In fact, the far side of the moon receives just as much sunlight as the near side. The confusion arises because the moon rotates at the same rate as it orbits the Earth, so that the same face always points towards us. The far side, which was unseen until a spacecraft photographed it in 1959, looks different from the near side, with more craters and fewer dark plains. Scientists are still debating why the two hemispheres differ so greatly.`,statements:[{text:`The far side of the moon receives sunlight.`,answer:!0},{text:`The far side was first photographed before 1950.`,answer:!1},{text:`Scientists fully understand why the two sides differ.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-122`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says ideas "rarely emerge from solitary reflection", the likelihood of collaboration "declined sharply with the distance between their desks", and the findings "do not necessarily support a return to full-time office work".`,passage:`A recent study of engineers suggests that the most creative ideas rarely emerge from solitary reflection. Instead, they arise when people from different disciplines encounter one another by chance and exchange half-formed thoughts. The researchers, who tracked communication patterns in a large technology firm, found that the likelihood of two colleagues collaborating declined sharply with the distance between their desks. Some companies have taken the lesson to heart, designing offices with shared kitchens and winding corridors intended to promote unplanned meetings. Yet the findings do not necessarily support a return to full-time office work. Remote teams can replicate some benefits by scheduling informal virtual gatherings, though participants often say that such events feel forced and lack the spontaneity of a chance encounter in the corridor.`,statements:[{text:`The study found that creative ideas usually come from working alone.`,answer:!1},{text:`Distance between desks reduced the likelihood of collaboration.`,answer:!0},{text:`The writer concludes that offices must always be preferred to remote work.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-123`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the proverb means "collaboration produces poor results", groups "perform well on tasks that can be divided", and "beyond about seven members, communication becomes cumbersome".`,passage:`The proverb that "too many cooks spoil the broth" is generally taken to mean that collaboration produces poor results. Research on group work, however, paints a more nuanced picture. Groups perform well on tasks that can be divided into separate parts, and on those requiring a variety of skills. They perform badly when members compete for status, when one person dominates, or when the task calls for a single creative vision. Group size matters too: beyond about seven members, communication becomes cumbersome and individuals tend to put in less effort. The best advice, perhaps, is not to avoid teamwork but to match the structure of the group to the nature of the task.`,statements:[{text:`The proverb is taken to mean that collaboration produces poor results.`,answer:!0},{text:`Groups always perform badly on divisible tasks.`,answer:!1},{text:`Groups larger than about seven members tend to find communication cumbersome.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-124`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says forced cheerfulness is "associated with burnout", teams that admit mistakes "learn more quickly", and honesty "can easily degenerate into blame" without skilled leadership.`,passage:`The notion that the workplace should be a place of unremitting positivity has come under scrutiny. Psychologists who study organisations argue that forced cheerfulness can be exhausting, since employees must constantly suppress genuine feelings, a process known as emotional labour. Over time, it is associated with burnout and reduced job satisfaction. By contrast, teams in which members feel safe to voice doubts and admit mistakes tend to learn more quickly and make fewer serious errors. Some companies have therefore begun to encourage constructive disagreement and to treat failures as opportunities for analysis. Sceptics point out that such cultures require skilled leadership; without it, honesty can easily degenerate into blame. The ideal, according to one researcher, is not constant happiness but a climate of trust in which difficult conversations are possible.`,statements:[{text:`Forced cheerfulness at work is linked to burnout.`,answer:!0},{text:`Teams that admit mistakes tend to learn more slowly.`,answer:!1},{text:`The text argues that honesty always improves workplace relations.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-125`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says retailers "now report that pedestrians spend more time, and often more money", critics mention "the elderly and disabled", and planners "acknowledge these concerns".`,passage:`The debate over whether cities should be designed around cars or people is not new, but it has acquired fresh urgency as urban populations swell and air quality deteriorates. Proponents of car-free zones point to cities such as Oslo and Ghent, where restricting traffic in the centre has led to cleaner air, quieter streets and a surge in footfall for local shops. Retailers, who had initially feared bankruptcy, now report that pedestrians spend more time, and often more money, than drivers rushing to find a parking space. Critics counter that such policies disadvantage the elderly and disabled, who may struggle to reach the centre without a vehicle, and that the benefits are concentrated among the affluent residents who can afford to live nearby. Planners acknowledge these concerns and increasingly pair traffic restrictions with improved public transport, subsidised taxis and accessible shuttle services. The evidence suggests that policies succeed when they are introduced gradually and accompanied by genuine consultation, rather than imposed overnight.`,statements:[{text:`Retailers in Oslo and Ghent suffered financially after traffic was restricted.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some critics believe car-free zones may disadvantage the elderly and disabled.`,answer:!0},{text:`Planners have ignored the objections raised by critics.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-126`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says corals "die" unless conditions "return to normal quickly", tourism "brings billions of dollars", and restoration "can only buy time".`,passage:`The Great Barrier Reef, the world's largest coral system, has suffered repeated episodes of bleaching caused by rising sea temperatures. Corals expel the algae that give them their colour and nourishment when water becomes too warm, and unless conditions return to normal quickly, they die. Scientists have documented large-scale mortality in several successive years. Tourism on the reef, which brings billions of dollars to the Australian economy, depends on healthy coral, and operators have begun to participate in restoration projects, transplanting fragments grown in nurseries. Experts agree that such efforts, however well-intentioned, can only buy time; the long-term survival of the reef depends on limiting global warming.`,statements:[{text:`Corals die if bleaching conditions persist.`,answer:!0},{text:`Tourism on the reef is of little economic importance.`,answer:!1},{text:`Experts believe restoration projects alone can save the reef.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-127`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says lanes attract "women, children and older people", studies "often show" cycling customers "spend more in total", and success depends on "consistent, long-term commitment".`,passage:`Cycling has experienced a revival in many cities, helped by investment in protected lanes and the spread of electric bikes. Cities that have built continuous networks of lanes report striking increases in the number of people cycling, including women, children and older people who had previously felt unsafe. The health benefits are considerable, and so are the savings from reduced congestion and pollution. Opponents complain that road space is being taken from drivers and that businesses suffer when parking is lost, though studies of shopping streets often show that customers who arrive by bicycle or on foot spend more in total over time. The success of such schemes depends on consistent, long-term commitment.`,statements:[{text:`Protected cycle lanes have encouraged more diverse groups to cycle.`,answer:!0},{text:`Studies always show that businesses lose money when parking is removed.`,answer:!1},{text:`The success of cycling schemes depends on short-term commitment.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-128`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says "over a week", the views "for long stretches, can be monotonous", and attendants are "famously formidable".`,passage:`The Trans-Siberian railway has become a pilgrimage for lovers of slow travel. Over a week, the train crosses seven time zones, taking in forests, steppe and the shores of Lake Baikal. Passengers share compartments with strangers, drinking tea from glasses with metal holders and exchanging fragments of conversation in a handful of languages. Provodnitsas, the carriage attendants, are famously formidable, ruling their domain with an iron hand. The train is not luxurious and the views, for long stretches, can be monotonous. But travellers speak of a mood of suspended time, in which the ordinary obligations of life are replaced by the simple business of the journey.`,statements:[{text:`The Trans-Siberian journey takes about a week.`,answer:!0},{text:`The views are always varied and spectacular.`,answer:!1},{text:`The carriage attendants are described as formidable.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-129`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says "traffic fell by around a fifth", retailers "reported little long-term effect", and road pricing "raises concerns about surveillance and privacy".`,passage:`Several cities have introduced congestion charges, requiring drivers to pay to enter the central zone at peak times. In London, traffic fell by around a fifth in the first year, and bus services improved as streets became less crowded. Opponents predicted that businesses would suffer, but retailers have generally reported little long-term effect. A weakness of the scheme is that its burden falls most heavily on lower-income drivers who cannot easily switch to public transport, which is why some economists propose that revenues should be spent on improving bus and train services. Others argue that road pricing based on distance would be fairer and more precise, though it raises concerns about surveillance and privacy.`,statements:[{text:`Traffic in London fell after the introduction of the charge.`,answer:!0},{text:`Retailers suffered severely in the long term.`,answer:!1},{text:`Distance-based pricing raises privacy concerns.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-130`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says adults hear "what one cannot yet produce", adults "often make slower progress than children in the early stages", and the best "accept the awkward early months".`,passage:`Anyone who has tried to learn the violin as an adult will know the particular frustration of hearing in one's head what one cannot yet produce with one's hands. Teachers say that adults often make slower progress than children in the early stages, since they are less willing to tolerate sounding bad. Yet adults have advantages: they understand how to practise, they bring discipline and they can appreciate the music they are learning. The best adult learners, teachers observe, are those who accept the awkward early months as part of the process and who take pleasure in small improvements.`,statements:[{text:`Adults hear in their heads what they cannot yet produce.`,answer:!0},{text:`Adults always progress faster than children.`,answer:!1},{text:`Teachers say good adult learners accept the awkward early months.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-131`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the temperature "is high enough to melt lead", it "may once have had oceans", and it is "similar in size to Earth".`,passage:`The weather on Venus is among the most hostile in the solar system. Its thick atmosphere of carbon dioxide traps heat so effectively that the surface temperature is high enough to melt lead, and the pressure is ninety times that on Earth. Clouds of sulphuric acid hang in the sky. Despite this, the planet is of great interest to scientists, since it is similar in size to Earth and may once have had oceans. Understanding how it became so inhospitable could help us to predict the future of our own climate.`,statements:[{text:`The surface of Venus is hot enough to melt lead.`,answer:!0},{text:`Venus may once have had oceans.`,answer:!0},{text:`Venus is very different in size from Earth.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-132`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the tablets were preserved "in waterlogged soil", they reveal "requests for warm socks, complaints about the weather", and writing was "far more widespread... than previously supposed".`,passage:`Archaeologists excavating a Roman fort in northern England have uncovered a collection of wooden writing tablets, preserved in waterlogged soil for nearly two thousand years. The thin leaves of wood, covered with ink, contain letters, accounts and even a birthday invitation addressed to the wife of a commander. The texts offer a rare glimpse into the everyday lives of soldiers and their families, revealing concerns that seem strikingly modern: requests for warm socks, complaints about the weather and anxiety about late payment. Because the ink has faded, scholars have had to use infrared photography to read many of the tablets. The find has transformed understanding of literacy on the frontier, suggesting that writing was far more widespread among ordinary people than previously supposed.`,statements:[{text:`The tablets were found in dry conditions.`,answer:!1},{text:`The texts include personal and practical concerns.`,answer:!0},{text:`The discovery suggests that writing was less common than scholars had thought.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-133`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the network is "famed for its punctuality", fares "are high by international standards", and connections "are carefully synchronised".`,passage:`The Swiss railway network is famed for its punctuality, and visitors often remark that trains arrive and depart to the minute. The system is based on a regular-interval timetable in which services run at the same minutes past each hour, making journeys easy to remember. Connections between trains and buses are carefully synchronised, so that passengers rarely wait long. The system is expensive to maintain, and fares are high by international standards, but the Swiss use it heavily, and the quality of the service is a source of national pride.`,statements:[{text:`Swiss trains are known for punctuality.`,answer:!0},{text:`Fares are described as low by international standards.`,answer:!1},{text:`Connections are carefully synchronised.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-134`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says "the authors do not foresee mass unemployment", they "recommend that governments invest in retraining", and unions say the report "understates the difficulty".`,passage:`A recent report on the future of work predicts that by the end of the decade many routine administrative tasks will be performed by software. The authors do not foresee mass unemployment, since new roles will emerge, but they warn that the transition will be painful for workers whose skills are made redundant. They recommend that governments invest in retraining and that employers take responsibility for helping staff to adapt. Trade unions have welcomed the report's emphasis on retraining but argue that it understates the difficulty of finding secure employment for people in middle age, whose chances of reinventing themselves are limited.`,statements:[{text:`The report predicts mass unemployment.`,answer:!1},{text:`The authors recommend investment in retraining.`,answer:!0},{text:`Unions think the report overestimates the difficulty for middle-aged workers.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-135`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says it "cross[es] the Alps into Italy", "neither was designed for tourists", and travellers "book months ahead".`,passage:`Some of the most spectacular railway journeys in the world cross mountain ranges. The Bernina Express in Switzerland climbs through glaciers to cross the Alps into Italy, passing over viaducts that seem to hang in the air. In India, a narrow-gauge line winds up through tea plantations to the hill station of Darjeeling, its small steam locomotives puffing at walking pace. Neither line is fast, and neither was designed for tourists, yet both are now protected as heritage sites, and travellers book months ahead to secure window seats.`,statements:[{text:`The Bernina Express crosses the Alps.`,answer:!0},{text:`Both lines were designed for tourists.`,answer:!1},{text:`Travellers book months ahead.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-136`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says supporters believe criminals "feel observed", evidence "is modest", and police welcome them "provided that residents do not take the law into their own hands".`,passage:`Neighbourhood watch schemes encourage residents to look out for suspicious activity and to report it to the police. Supporters believe that they reduce crime by making criminals feel observed and by strengthening ties among neighbours. Evidence for the claim is modest, however: some studies find a small reduction in burglary, while others find no effect. Critics worry that the schemes may encourage suspicion of strangers and misuse of information. Police officers generally welcome them as a source of local knowledge, provided that residents do not take the law into their own hands.`,statements:[{text:`Supporters think the schemes make criminals feel observed.`,answer:!0},{text:`Evidence for a large reduction in crime is overwhelming.`,answer:!1},{text:`Police welcome the schemes provided residents do not take the law into their own hands.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-137`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says employers are "not obliged to offer any minimum", critics say "the flexibility is one-sided", and the review said they "should not be banned".`,passage:`Zero-hours contracts, under which employers are not obliged to offer any minimum number of hours, have become widespread in some sectors. Supporters claim that they offer flexibility to both sides and suit people who want occasional work. Critics argue that in practice the flexibility is one-sided: workers who turn down shifts may find that the offers dry up, and they cannot plan their finances when their income varies from week to week. A government review concluded that the contracts should not be banned but that workers should be given the right to request fixed hours after a period of service.`,statements:[{text:`Zero-hours contracts guarantee a minimum number of hours.`,answer:!1},{text:`Critics say the flexibility is one-sided.`,answer:!0},{text:`The review recommended banning the contracts.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-138`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says operators "leave days free for independent exploration", they are "often more expensive", and customers say "the difference is worth paying for the saved effort".`,passage:`Many travellers prefer to avoid package holidays but find that organising everything themselves is time-consuming. A middle course is offered by specialist operators who arrange transport and accommodation but leave days free for independent exploration. Customers appreciate the security of knowing that the basics are in place, together with the freedom to follow their own interests. These holidays are often more expensive than booking separately, but customers say the difference is worth paying for the saved effort. Some operators also provide local contacts who can help if difficulties arise.`,statements:[{text:`Specialist operators leave days free for independent exploration.`,answer:!0},{text:`These holidays are always cheaper than booking separately.`,answer:!1},{text:`Customers say the extra cost is worth paying.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-139`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says it "absorbs about a quarter of the carbon dioxide", water "is becoming more acidic", and "as it warms, it may become less efficient".`,passage:`The ocean's role in regulating the climate is often overlooked. It absorbs about a quarter of the carbon dioxide released by human activity and captures much of the excess heat. This service has come at a price: the water is becoming more acidic, threatening organisms with shells, such as corals and shellfish, and warmer seas are altering the movement of fish and the strength of storms. Scientists warn that the ocean's capacity to absorb carbon is not unlimited, and that as it warms, it may become less efficient. Protecting marine ecosystems is therefore not only a matter of conservation but of climate policy.`,statements:[{text:`The ocean absorbs about a quarter of the carbon dioxide released by human activity.`,answer:!0},{text:`The ocean is becoming less acidic.`,answer:!1},{text:`The ocean's capacity to absorb carbon may decline as it warms.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-140`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says early writing was "lists: records of grain, livestock and debts", the romantic view "is challenged", and literacy "was confined to a small elite" for most of history.`,passage:`The oldest known examples of writing, found in ancient Mesopotamia, were not poems or histories but lists: records of grain, livestock and debts. It seems that writing was invented to meet the practical needs of administration. Only later did scribes begin to record stories, laws and prayers. This origin challenges the romantic notion that writing arose from a desire for self-expression. It also suggests that literacy has always been bound up with power, since those who controlled records controlled resources. Historians note that for most of history reading and writing were confined to a small elite, and that mass literacy is a very recent phenomenon.`,statements:[{text:`The earliest writing recorded grain, livestock and debts.`,answer:!0},{text:`Writing was invented mainly for self-expression.`,answer:!1},{text:`For most of history only a small elite could read and write.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-141`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says they were celebrated "not only for their speed", critics complained of "a blur", and others welcomed that "working people" could reach "the seaside for the first time".`,passage:`The great railway journeys of the nineteenth century were celebrated not only for their speed but for the new way of seeing that they offered. Passengers could watch landscapes unfold from a comfortable seat, and writers compared the experience to looking at a moving panorama. Some critics at the time deplored what they saw as a loss of the intimate knowledge of place that came from travelling on foot or by coach, complaining that the railway reduced the countryside to a blur. Others welcomed the democratisation of travel, which allowed working people to reach the seaside for the first time. Both reactions have echoes today in debates about low-cost flights and the question of whether easier travel enriches or cheapens the experience of other places.`,statements:[{text:`Nineteenth-century railways were praised only for their speed.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some critics thought railways reduced the countryside to a blur.`,answer:!0},{text:`Railways allowed working people to reach the seaside.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-142`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says deaths followed doctors "straight from dissecting corpses", colleagues "rejected his ideas", and "it was only after his death that the germ theory... vindicated him".`,passage:`Hand washing is one of the simplest and most effective ways to prevent the spread of disease. The nineteenth-century physician Ignaz Semmelweis noticed that women whose babies were delivered by doctors who had come straight from dissecting corpses died far more often of fever than those attended by midwives. When he insisted that doctors wash their hands in disinfectant, deaths fell sharply. His colleagues, however, rejected his ideas, finding it offensive to suggest that they had been the cause. It was only after his death that the germ theory of disease vindicated him.`,statements:[{text:`Semmelweis linked deaths to doctors who had handled corpses.`,answer:!0},{text:`His colleagues welcomed his ideas immediately.`,answer:!1},{text:`He was proved right after his death.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-143`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says hikers "speak of a state of mind", therapists "incorporate walking in nature", and sceptics say benefits "may owe as much to fresh air and exercise as to any special mental state".`,passage:`Hikers on long-distance trails often speak of a state of mind that they find hard to describe. After several days of walking, the pressures of everyday life recede, the mind slows and attention focuses on simple things: the next step, the weather, the sound of one's own breathing. Some psychologists compare it to meditation. Others suggest that the rhythm of walking itself promotes reflection, noting that many writers and philosophers have been enthusiastic walkers. For this reason, a number of therapists now incorporate walking in nature into their treatment of anxiety and depression. Sceptics caution that the benefits may owe as much to fresh air and exercise as to any special mental state.`,statements:[{text:`Long-distance hikers report a distinctive state of mind.`,answer:!0},{text:`Some therapists use walking in nature to treat anxiety.`,answer:!0},{text:`Sceptics are sure that the mental state is the only source of benefit.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-144`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says "support fell sharply" after accidents, opinion "has shifted again" because of climate change, and risk perceptions are "shaped less by statistics than by dramatic events".`,passage:`Public attitudes to nuclear power have fluctuated with events. After accidents such as Chernobyl and Fukushima, support fell sharply in many countries and some governments decided to phase out their reactors. As concern about climate change has grown, however, opinion has shifted again, with a growing number of people prepared to accept nuclear energy as a low-carbon alternative to fossil fuels. Opponents continue to stress the unresolved problem of long-term waste storage, while supporters point to the safety record of modern designs. The debate shows how perceptions of risk are shaped less by statistics than by dramatic events and by what people fear most.`,statements:[{text:`Support for nuclear power fell after major accidents.`,answer:!0},{text:`Concern about climate change has made no difference to opinion.`,answer:!1},{text:`Perceptions of risk are shaped mainly by statistics.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-145`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the city "has always been vulnerable", the barriers followed "years of delay and controversy", and critics "worry that it may not cope".`,passage:`Venice, built on a lagoon, has always been vulnerable to flooding, but the problem has worsened in recent decades. A combination of rising sea levels and the gradual sinking of the city has made high tides more frequent, and the flooding of St Mark's Square has become almost routine in autumn. A system of mobile barriers, designed to close the lagoon during exceptional tides, was completed after years of delay and controversy. It has worked on several occasions, but critics worry that it may not cope with future rises in sea level, and some residents fear that it will damage the lagoon's ecology.`,statements:[{text:`Venice has only recently begun to experience flooding.`,answer:!1},{text:`The barrier system was built quickly and without controversy.`,answer:!1},{text:`Critics fear the barriers may not cope with future sea level rises.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-146`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the island was evacuated "after a volcanic eruption", "most of them voted to return", and "some scholars warn against romanticising isolated communities".`,passage:`When the village of Tristan da Cunha was evacuated after a volcanic eruption in 1961, the islanders were taken to England, where they encountered electricity, central heating and consumer goods for the first time. Within two years, however, most of them voted to return to their remote island in the South Atlantic, despite the absence of a hospital, an airport or regular communications. Anthropologists who studied the community noted that the islanders had missed not material comforts but the strong sense of mutual obligation that had governed their daily lives. In England they felt anonymous and, paradoxically, more vulnerable. The story is often cited in discussions of how modernity can erode social bonds, though some scholars warn against romanticising isolated communities, whose members may also face limited opportunities and stifling conformity.`,statements:[{text:`The islanders were evacuated because of a volcanic eruption.`,answer:!0},{text:`Most of the islanders chose to stay in England permanently.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some scholars caution against romanticising isolated communities.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-147`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says guests were asked "to bring a photograph and a story", the writer "had asked her" little, and "some are reluctant".`,passage:`When my grandmother turned ninety, the family organised a party and asked guests to bring a photograph and a story. The collection that resulted was extraordinary: pictures of her as a girl on a farm, as a young nurse in wartime, as a bride in borrowed shoes. Hearing the stories, I realised how little I had asked her over the years. She had always been present in my life as a fixture of Sunday lunches, rarely as a person with a past of her own. Since then I have tried to ask older relatives about their lives before it is too late. Some are reluctant and change the subject, but others talk for hours, delighted that anybody should be interested.`,statements:[{text:`Guests were asked to bring a photograph and a story.`,answer:!0},{text:`The writer knew a great deal about the grandmother's past before the party.`,answer:!1},{text:`All the older relatives are eager to talk about their lives.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-148`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says containers reduced "time and... cost", "ports that did not [adapt] declined", and "the number of dockers needed fell dramatically".`,passage:`The introduction of the standard shipping container in the 1950s revolutionised international trade. Before then, cargo was loaded by hand, and ships spent days in port while dockers carried goods on board. Containers could be transferred directly between ship, train and lorry, reducing both the time and the cost. Ports that adapted prospered, while those that did not declined. The change also had a human cost, as the number of dockers needed fell dramatically and communities that had depended on port work lost their livelihoods. Today, most manufactured goods spend part of their journey in a metal box.`,statements:[{text:`Containers made loading faster and cheaper.`,answer:!0},{text:`All ports benefited equally.`,answer:!1},{text:`Many dockers lost their jobs.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-149`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says it "was met... with considerable scepticism", "some scholars question whether he really visited China", and "his book inspired generations of explorers".`,passage:`Marco Polo's account of his journeys through Asia, written in the thirteenth century, was met at the time with considerable scepticism, and has remained controversial. Some scholars question whether he really visited China, pointing out that he failed to mention features such as the Great Wall and the habit of drinking tea. Others reply that his descriptions of Chinese cities, paper money and the postal system are too detailed to have been invented. The truth may lie between the two: he may have travelled widely but supplemented his own observations with stories from other merchants. Whatever the verdict, his book inspired generations of explorers, including Columbus.`,statements:[{text:`Marco Polo's account was widely accepted without doubt.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some scholars question whether he visited China.`,answer:!0},{text:`His book influenced later explorers.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-150`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says apprenticeships were "the poor relation... in some countries", "around half of school-leavers" in Germany enter them, and attempts elsewhere "have met with limited success".`,passage:`Apprenticeships, long regarded as the poor relation of university education in some countries, have been experiencing a revival. Employers complain that graduates often lack practical skills, while young people increasingly resent the debt that a degree entails. In Germany, where the apprenticeship system has long enjoyed high prestige, around half of school-leavers enter such programmes, combining paid work with part-time study. The model is credited with keeping youth unemployment low. Attempts to transplant it elsewhere have met with limited success, however. Employers must be willing to invest in training, parents must regard vocational routes as respectable, and qualifications must be recognised across industries. Without these conditions, apprenticeships risk becoming a source of cheap labour rather than genuine education.`,statements:[{text:`Apprenticeships have always been prestigious in every country.`,answer:!1},{text:`In Germany about half of school-leavers enter apprenticeship programmes.`,answer:!0},{text:`Transplanting the German model has been very successful elsewhere.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-151`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says a degree "almost guaranteed a good job", employers value skills "as much as academic results", and others say a degree should develop "knowledge and critical thinking".`,passage:`In the past, a university degree almost guaranteed a good job. Today, with so many graduates entering the market, the advantage has diminished, and many young people find themselves in positions that do not require their qualifications. Employers say they value skills such as communication, teamwork and adaptability as much as academic results, and some large firms have dropped degree requirements for certain roles. Education specialists argue that universities should pay more attention to these skills, while others insist that the purpose of a degree is to develop knowledge and critical thinking rather than to train for specific jobs.`,statements:[{text:`A degree used to almost guarantee a good job.`,answer:!0},{text:`Employers value only academic results.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some argue a degree should develop knowledge and critical thinking.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-152`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says customs "vary widely", some birthdays "are regarded as milestones", and "some individuals... prefer to ignore the day".`,passage:`Birthday customs vary widely. In some countries, the person celebrating is expected to treat friends and colleagues, bringing cakes to the office or paying for a meal. In others, the guest of honour receives gifts and is not allowed to pay for anything. Certain birthdays, such as the eighteenth or the sixtieth, are regarded as milestones and marked with special ceremonies. Anthropologists observe that although the details differ, the underlying purpose is similar: to affirm a person's place in a community. Some individuals, however, prefer to ignore the day, seeing it as an unwelcome reminder of passing time.`,statements:[{text:`Birthday customs are identical everywhere.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some birthdays are marked as milestones.`,answer:!0},{text:`Everyone enjoys celebrating their birthday.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-153`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the model used "X-ray images produced by Rosalind Franklin", she "died in 1958, before the Nobel Prize", and "her careful experimental work was essential".`,passage:`The discovery of DNA's double-helix structure in 1953 is one of the best-known episodes in science, but the story is more complicated than the popular version suggests. James Watson and Francis Crick built their model using X-ray images produced by Rosalind Franklin, whose contribution was not fully acknowledged at the time. She died in 1958, before the Nobel Prize was awarded in 1962 to Watson, Crick and Maurice Wilkins. Historians continue to debate how much credit she deserves, but there is general agreement that her careful experimental work was essential to the discovery.`,statements:[{text:`Watson and Crick used images produced by Rosalind Franklin.`,answer:!0},{text:`Franklin received a Nobel Prize in 1962.`,answer:!1},{text:`Historians agree her work was essential.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-154`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says they were "responsible mainly for answering telephones and greeting visitors", visitors "still value a friendly person", and receptionists are "ambassadors for the company".`,passage:`The role of the receptionist has changed with technology. Once responsible mainly for answering telephones and greeting visitors, many now manage appointments, handle online enquiries and coordinate with security. Automated check-in systems have removed some of the routine tasks, but companies that have introduced them often find that visitors still value a friendly person who can solve problems. Receptionists, who have the first contact with clients, are increasingly regarded as ambassadors for the company. Training in customer service and in dealing with difficult situations is becoming more common.`,statements:[{text:`Receptionists used to be responsible mainly for telephones and visitors.`,answer:!0},{text:`Automated systems have completely replaced receptionists.`,answer:!1},{text:`Receptionists are increasingly seen as ambassadors for the company.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-155`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says insects "form the base of food chains", their decline "is hard to measure", and "there is wide agreement that the trend is worrying".`,passage:`The mass extinction of insects has received less attention than that of larger animals, but its consequences may be just as serious. Insects pollinate crops, decompose waste and form the base of food chains for birds and fish. Studies from several countries report sharp falls in numbers, which scientists attribute to habitat loss, pesticides and climate change. Because insects are small and numerous, their decline is hard to measure, and some experts caution that the data are incomplete. Nonetheless, there is wide agreement that the trend is worrying and that action to protect habitats is needed.`,statements:[{text:`Insects form the base of food chains for birds and fish.`,answer:!0},{text:`The decline of insects is easy to measure.`,answer:!1},{text:`There is wide agreement that the trend is worrying.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-156`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says some stations have "never recorded rain", the landscape is "anything but monotonous", and visitors should spend "a couple of days acclimatising".`,passage:`The Atacama Desert in northern Chile is among the driest places on earth, with some weather stations having never recorded rain. Yet the landscape is anything but monotonous: salt flats shimmer under a pale sky, geysers erupt at dawn and flamingos feed in shallow lagoons. Because the air is exceptionally dry and clear, and because there is almost no light pollution, the region hosts some of the world's most powerful telescopes, and a growing number of tourists travel there to gaze at the stars. Local guides provide telescopes and explain the constellations of the southern hemisphere. The altitude can cause headaches and breathlessness, so visitors are advised to spend a couple of days acclimatising before undertaking any strenuous excursions.`,statements:[{text:`Some weather stations in the Atacama have never recorded rain.`,answer:!0},{text:`The desert is visually monotonous.`,answer:!1},{text:`Visitors are advised to acclimatise before strenuous excursions.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-157`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says retraining "is increasingly common", "those who already have good qualifications are the most likely to continue learning", and successful programmes "begin with practical, work-related tasks".`,passage:`The pace of technological change has made it increasingly common for people to retrain several times during their working lives. Governments and employers are experimenting with schemes to support this, from tax incentives for adult education to short courses delivered online. Yet participation is uneven: those who already have good qualifications are the most likely to continue learning, while those who left school early and most need new skills tend to be the least engaged. Barriers include cost, lack of time and, perhaps most significant, a lack of confidence among adults who associate education with failure. Programmes that have succeeded often begin with practical, work-related tasks and offer one-to-one guidance in place of traditional classroom teaching.`,statements:[{text:`Retraining several times is becoming more common.`,answer:!0},{text:`Those with the poorest qualifications are most likely to keep learning.`,answer:!1},{text:`Successful programmes often start with practical tasks.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-158`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says nudges alter decisions "without restricting their freedom", governments "set up dedicated units", and effects "often shrink" when extended.`,passage:`The word "nudge" has entered public policy in recent years, thanks to behavioural economists who argue that small changes in the way choices are presented can significantly alter people's decisions without restricting their freedom. Placing healthy food at eye level in a canteen, or making organ donation the default option, are typical examples. Governments in several countries have set up dedicated units to apply such insights. Supporters praise the approach for being cheap and respectful of individual liberty, while critics argue that it is a subtle form of manipulation, and that it distracts attention from larger structural problems such as poverty and poor housing. A further difficulty is that effects measured in small trials often shrink when the interventions are extended to entire populations.`,statements:[{text:`Nudges restrict people's freedom of choice.`,answer:!1},{text:`Governments have created units to use behavioural insights.`,answer:!0},{text:`The effects of nudges always grow when applied to whole populations.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-159`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says "the loss of a regular salary", "she would not return to employment", and "she advised others to build savings".`,passage:`Self-employment brings freedom but also uncertainty. A surveyor interviewed about her decision to leave a large firm said that the greatest change was the loss of a regular salary: some months she earned very little, while in others she was overwhelmed with work. She had to learn to manage accounts, find clients and plan for periods without income. Nevertheless, she said she would not return to employment, valuing the control she now had over her time and the variety of her work. She advised others to build savings before taking the plunge.`,statements:[{text:`The surveyor lost a regular salary when she became self-employed.`,answer:!0},{text:`She regrets leaving the large firm.`,answer:!1},{text:`She advised building savings first.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-160`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says they sell "donated" goods, are "staffed largely by volunteers", and "occupy premises that would otherwise stand empty".`,passage:`Charity shops are a familiar sight on British high streets, and their number has grown steadily. They raise money by selling donated clothes, books and household goods, often staffed largely by volunteers. Customers range from bargain hunters to people who prefer to buy second-hand for environmental reasons, and the shops have gained a reputation as a source of vintage items. Some commentators worry that they contribute to the decline of the traditional high street by displacing independent traders. Charities respond that they occupy premises that would otherwise stand empty and that the funds they raise support vital work.`,statements:[{text:`Charity shops sell goods bought from wholesalers.`,answer:!1},{text:`Charity shops are often staffed largely by volunteers.`,answer:!0},{text:`Charities say they occupy premises that would otherwise be empty.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-161`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says technology "often creates additional demands", organisations have "impos[ed] quiet periods", and experts suggest "managers... set an example by sending fewer messages themselves".`,passage:`One of the paradoxes of modern work is that technology designed to save time often creates additional demands. Email, for instance, was meant to speed up communication, yet many employees now feel burdened by a constant stream of messages that must be answered. The expectation of immediate replies blurs the boundary between working and nonworking hours. Some organisations have attempted to address the problem by discouraging internal email, imposing quiet periods or setting up shared platforms where discussions can be tracked. The results have been mixed, since the underlying causes, such as a culture of anxiety and an unwillingness to delegate, are not technological. Experts suggest that the most effective remedy is for managers to set an example by sending fewer messages themselves.`,statements:[{text:`Email has fully achieved its aim of saving time for workers.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some organisations impose quiet periods.`,answer:!0},{text:`Experts think managers should send fewer messages themselves.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-162`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says "much of the water lies beyond the jurisdiction of any single country", the treaty is "a landmark achievement", and monitoring is "extremely difficult".`,passage:`Efforts to protect the world's oceans face a peculiar difficulty: much of the water lies beyond the jurisdiction of any single country. Fish stocks have collapsed in areas where fleets from many nations compete, each reluctant to restrain itself while others continue. After years of negotiation, governments have agreed a treaty to create protected areas on the high seas, a landmark achievement that campaigners compare to the Paris climate agreement. Its success will depend on enforcement, however. Monitoring vast stretches of open water is extremely difficult, and critics point out that previous agreements have been undermined by vessels that switch off their tracking devices. Satellite technology and cooperation among coastal states may help close the gap.`,statements:[{text:`Much of the ocean lies beyond the jurisdiction of any one country.`,answer:!0},{text:`The new treaty is described as a minor achievement.`,answer:!1},{text:`Enforcement on the high seas is considered easy.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-163`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says psychologists "take it seriously", "a few become the foundation of public museums", and the distinction "lies in whether the collector remains in control".`,passage:`The practice of collecting, whether stamps, coins or antique clocks, is often dismissed as a harmless eccentricity. Psychologists, however, take it seriously. Collecting appears to satisfy deep human needs for order, mastery and a sense of identity, and many collectors report that the hunt gives them as much pleasure as the possession. Some collections acquire great value, and a few become the foundation of public museums. At the other extreme, the habit can become compulsive, consuming money and space and straining relationships. The distinction, therapists say, lies in whether the collector remains in control, deriving pleasure from the activity, or is driven by anxiety to acquire more.`,statements:[{text:`Psychologists dismiss collecting as meaningless.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some collections have become the basis of public museums.`,answer:!0},{text:`Collecting is always compulsive and harmful.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-164`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says "most of them paying clients", queues "have contributed to fatalities", and the authorities "have proposed stricter requirements" but limiting numbers drastically is "politically difficult".`,passage:`Mount Everest, once the preserve of a few elite mountaineers, now attracts hundreds of climbers in a good season, most of them paying clients of commercial expeditions. Sherpas fix ropes and ladders, carry oxygen and cook meals, enabling people with limited experience to attempt the summit. The consequence has been congestion at the most dangerous stages of the climb, where queues of climbers waiting in thin air have contributed to fatalities. Critics argue that the mountain has become a commercial spectacle and that Sherpas bear a disproportionate share of the risk. The authorities of Nepal have proposed stricter requirements for climbers, though the economic importance of the industry makes it politically difficult to limit the numbers drastically.`,statements:[{text:`Most climbers on Everest today are paying clients.`,answer:!0},{text:`Queues at dangerous stages have contributed to deaths.`,answer:!0},{text:`Nepal has imposed a strict limit on climber numbers.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-165`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says one motive was "to cut costs", employees "converse less face to face", and architects argue that "well-designed open spaces can work".`,passage:`The introduction of the open-plan office was driven by two motives: to cut costs by squeezing more people into less space, and to encourage collaboration by removing walls. While the first aim has certainly been achieved, evidence for the second is thin. Studies indicate that employees in open-plan offices converse less face to face, retreating behind headphones and communicating by message instead. They also report more distraction and greater exposure to illness, as germs spread more easily. Architects point out that well-designed open spaces can work, provided they include quiet rooms for concentration and areas for informal meetings. The problem, they argue, is not openness itself but the failure of many organisations to match the design to the needs of the people who actually use it.`,statements:[{text:`Open-plan offices were introduced partly to save money.`,answer:!0},{text:`Employees in open-plan offices talk more face to face.`,answer:!1},{text:`Architects believe open-plan offices can never work.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-166`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says proponents call the tests "more objective than interviews", sceptics note "candidates quickly learn what answers are expected", and psychologists advise "one source of information among several, never as the sole basis".`,passage:`Large companies increasingly use psychometric tests when selecting staff, hoping to identify personality traits that predict success. Proponents say that the tests are more objective than interviews and can screen large numbers of applicants cheaply. Sceptics point out that candidates quickly learn what answers are expected and tailor their responses accordingly, which undermines the results. There is also evidence that the tests may favour certain cultural backgrounds, raising questions of fairness. Psychologists who design the tests acknowledge these limitations and recommend that results be used as one source of information among several, never as the sole basis for a decision. Even so, the temptation to treat a number as a verdict is strong, particularly for busy recruiters.`,statements:[{text:`Psychometric tests are described as more objective than interviews by proponents.`,answer:!0},{text:`Sceptics think candidates cannot adjust their answers.`,answer:!1},{text:`Psychologists recommend using test results as the only basis for decisions.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-167`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says dependent destinations "faced severe economic hardship", canals "ran clear" and wildlife returned, and the experience "prompted a debate about whether tourism can be rebuilt in a more sustainable form".`,passage:`The tourist industry is notoriously vulnerable to events beyond its control, from volcanic eruptions to pandemics. When international travel ground to a halt, destinations that had depended heavily on visitors faced severe economic hardship. In a few places, however, the pause had unexpected benefits. Canals in a famous Italian city ran clear for the first time in decades, and wildlife returned to beaches that were normally crowded with sunbathers. Some local residents who had long resented the crowds found that they missed the income, while others wished that the quiet could be preserved. The experience has prompted a debate about whether tourism can be rebuilt in a more sustainable form, with fewer visitors who stay longer and spend more.`,statements:[{text:`Destinations dependent on visitors suffered when travel stopped.`,answer:!0},{text:`The pause in tourism had no environmental effects.`,answer:!1},{text:`The experience encouraged discussion about a more sustainable form of tourism.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-168`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the press "changed the nature of knowledge itself", errors "could now be identified and corrected", and "some historians argue that the scientific revolution would have been impossible without it".`,passage:`The development of the printing press in the fifteenth century did more than speed up the copying of books; it changed the nature of knowledge itself. Because identical copies could be produced in large numbers, scholars in different places could refer to the same text and build on one another's work. Errors, once copied and multiplied by hand, could now be identified and corrected in later editions. The new technology also encouraged the standardisation of languages and spellings, as printers sought to reach the widest possible audience. Some historians argue that the scientific revolution would have been impossible without it.`,statements:[{text:`The printing press only increased the speed of copying.`,answer:!1},{text:`Printing made it easier to correct errors in later editions.`,answer:!0},{text:`All historians agree that printing was irrelevant to science.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-169`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says it is "warming roughly twice as fast", companies are interested in "savings in time and fuel", and communities "fear disruption".`,passage:`The Arctic is warming roughly twice as fast as the global average, and the loss of sea ice is opening new shipping routes between Europe and Asia. Shipping companies are interested in the savings in time and fuel, while environmentalists warn of the risk of oil spills in waters where clean-up would be extremely difficult. Indigenous communities, whose way of life depends on the ice, fear disruption to hunting and fishing. Governments of the surrounding countries have begun to assert their claims to the resources of the seabed, raising questions about how this fragile region should be governed.`,statements:[{text:`The Arctic is warming more slowly than the global average.`,answer:!1},{text:`Shipping companies are attracted by savings in time and fuel.`,answer:!0},{text:`Indigenous communities welcome the loss of ice.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-170`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says some "have abandoned their offices entirely", firms "face challenges in maintaining a sense of shared purpose", and "results, not hours spent online, should be the measure".`,passage:`Technology has made it possible for many workers to do their jobs from anywhere, and some companies have abandoned their offices entirely. Employees save the cost and time of commuting, and employers save on property. Yet fully remote firms face challenges in maintaining a sense of shared purpose. They often hold regular gatherings, invest in communication tools and encourage informal contact. Managers report that trust becomes even more important, since they cannot observe staff directly, and that results, not hours spent online, should be the measure of performance.`,statements:[{text:`Some companies have abandoned offices entirely.`,answer:!0},{text:`Fully remote firms find it easy to maintain shared purpose.`,answer:!1},{text:`Managers say results should measure performance.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-171`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says hours were reduced "without cutting pay", most firms "recorded stable or even improved revenue", and sceptics note employers were "self-selected".`,passage:`The idea of a four-day working week has moved from the fringes of political debate into serious experiments in several countries. In a large trial in Britain, dozens of companies reduced their working hours without cutting pay, and the great majority chose to continue the arrangement afterwards. Employees reported lower stress, better sleep and fewer sick days, while most firms recorded stable or even improved revenue. Sceptics, however, caution that the participating employers were self-selected and therefore likely to be well-suited to the scheme. Jobs that require constant presence, such as nursing or retail, may find it much harder to compress their hours, and there is a danger that the benefits of shorter weeks will accrue mainly to office workers who already enjoy privileges. Supporters respond that the experiment is a starting point rather than a blueprint.`,statements:[{text:`Participating firms in the British trial cut their employees' pay.`,answer:!1},{text:`Most firms recorded stable or better revenue.`,answer:!0},{text:`Sceptics point out that the trial participants chose to take part.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-172`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says decorators "were more productive", the finding "surprised managers", and shared-desk firms "tend to ban personal items".`,passage:`A well-known study of workplace behaviour found that employees who were allowed to decorate their own desks were more productive than those in bare, uniform offices. The finding surprised managers, who had assumed that tidiness and conformity encouraged efficiency. The researchers suggested that the freedom to personalise a space gives people a sense of control and identity, which in turn increases commitment. Not all employers have drawn the lesson, however. Companies that operate shared desks, where staff sit in a different place each day, tend to ban personal items altogether, and some workers report feeling like temporary visitors in their own office.`,statements:[{text:`Employees with decorated desks were more productive.`,answer:!0},{text:`The finding confirmed managers' expectations.`,answer:!1},{text:`Shared-desk companies tend to allow personal items.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-173`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says it is "microscopic", metabolism "almost stops" in hostile conditions, and the proteins "might one day help to preserve organs or vaccines".`,passage:`The tardigrade, a microscopic animal often called the water bear, is among the toughest creatures known. It can survive temperatures close to absolute zero and far above boiling point, withstand pressures greater than those at the bottom of the ocean, and endure doses of radiation that would kill a human instantly. When conditions become hostile, it curls into a dry, dormant state in which its metabolism almost stops. Scientists have revived specimens after decades in this condition. Research into how tardigrades protect their cells is of particular interest to medicine, since the proteins involved might one day help to preserve organs or vaccines without refrigeration.`,statements:[{text:`Tardigrades are visible only with a microscope.`,answer:!0},{text:`A tardigrade's metabolism increases in hostile conditions.`,answer:!1},{text:`Research on them might help preserve vaccines.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-174`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says it was "a shifting network of trade routes", the term "was coined only in the nineteenth century", and "few merchants completed the whole journey".`,passage:`For centuries, the Silk Road has captured the imagination of travellers and historians alike, though the term itself was coined only in the nineteenth century by a German geographer. In reality there was no single road but a shifting network of trade routes linking China with the Mediterranean, along which not only silk but also spices, religions, technologies and diseases travelled. Few merchants completed the whole journey; goods passed from hand to hand through a chain of intermediaries, each adding a profit. Today, tourists retrace parts of the route, visiting oasis towns and ruined caravanserais. Archaeologists warn that the increase in visitors, together with rapid development, threatens fragile sites, and several governments have introduced limits on numbers at the most vulnerable locations.`,statements:[{text:`The Silk Road was a single, well-defined road.`,answer:!1},{text:`The term "Silk Road" was invented in the nineteenth century.`,answer:!0},{text:`Few merchants travelled the entire length of the route.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-175`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the presence of data "does not guarantee better decisions", some firms appoint people "to challenge assumptions", and such practices "are uncomfortable".`,passage:`Many organisations pride themselves on being "data-driven", yet research suggests that the presence of data does not guarantee better decisions. Managers often select figures that support a conclusion they have already reached, a tendency known as confirmation bias, and ignore evidence that contradicts it. Moreover, statistics can be misleading if the underlying measurements are flawed or if correlations are mistaken for causes. Some firms have responded by appointing people whose task is to challenge assumptions, regardless of how senior their colleagues may be. Others require teams to state in advance what evidence would persuade them to change course. Such practices are uncomfortable, but advocates insist that they are among the most effective safeguards against self-deception in complex organisations.`,statements:[{text:`Having data guarantees better decisions.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some firms appoint people to challenge assumptions.`,answer:!0},{text:`Requiring teams to state in advance what would change their minds is described as comfortable.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-176`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the Earth "appeared small, fragile", astronauts describe "a change in their own outlook", and researchers study "whether similar feelings can be produced by virtual reality".`,passage:`The first photographs of the Earth taken from space had a profound effect on public attitudes. Seen from the Moon, the planet appeared small, fragile and without borders, a blue sphere in the darkness. Many people later credited the images with strengthening the environmental movement. Astronauts have described a change in their own outlook, sometimes called the overview effect, in which they feel a deep concern for the planet and its inhabitants. Researchers are now studying whether similar feelings can be produced by virtual reality, offering ordinary people a taste of the experience.`,statements:[{text:`The images showed the Earth as small and fragile.`,answer:!0},{text:`Astronauts never change their outlook.`,answer:!1},{text:`Researchers study virtual reality as a way to reproduce the feeling.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-177`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says each person "has an internal clock" that varies, teenagers "tend to be night owls", and later starts show "improved results among adolescents".`,passage:`Many people claim to be morning or evening types, and research suggests that there is a biological basis for the difference. Each person has an internal clock that determines when they feel most alert, and this varies with age: teenagers tend to be night owls, while older adults wake earlier. Schools that start later in the morning have reported better attendance and improved results among adolescents. Employers, however, rarely adapt working hours to individual rhythms. Sleep scientists argue that allowing flexibility would benefit both workers and organisations, since people perform best when working in harmony with their own clocks.`,statements:[{text:`Everyone has the same internal clock.`,answer:!1},{text:`Teenagers tend to be night owls.`,answer:!0},{text:`Later school start times have improved results among adolescents.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-178`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says supporters argue "parents have the right", opponents fear "selection for desirable traits", and "defining 'severe' is far from straightforward".`,passage:`The question of whether to allow genetic screening of embryos for serious inherited diseases divides opinion. Supporters argue that parents have the right to avoid passing on conditions that cause great suffering, and that the technique has already prevented many families from experiencing tragedy. Opponents fear a slippery slope, in which screening for disease leads to selection for desirable traits such as height or intelligence, and in which disabled people are made to feel that their lives are less valuable. Regulators in several countries have tried to draw a line, permitting screening only for severe conditions that threaten life or health. Yet defining "severe" is far from straightforward, and the boundary is likely to remain contested as the technology advances.`,statements:[{text:`Supporters say parents have the right to avoid passing on serious conditions.`,answer:!0},{text:`Opponents fear that screening could lead to selection for desirable traits.`,answer:!0},{text:`Regulators have found it easy to define "severe" conditions.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-179`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says tourists "exceed the population... several times over", landscapes "have been trampled", and Icelanders "worry that... solitude and wilderness may be lost".`,passage:`Iceland has become one of Europe's fastest-growing tourist destinations, drawing visitors with its glaciers, geysers and the northern lights. The number of tourists now exceeds the population of the country several times over, which brings both prosperity and strain. Fragile landscapes have been trampled, and small communities find it difficult to cope with the demands on roads, accommodation and emergency services. The government has begun to consider limiting numbers or introducing charges at popular sites. Many Icelanders welcome the income but worry that the qualities that attract visitors, such as solitude and wilderness, may be lost.`,statements:[{text:`Tourists in Iceland outnumber the population several times over.`,answer:!0},{text:`The landscape has not been affected by visitors.`,answer:!1},{text:`Many Icelanders worry about losing solitude and wilderness.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-180`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says tours were "the preserve of the mass market", operators have offered "smaller groups", and a good guide provides "context that a guidebook cannot".`,passage:`Package tours were once the preserve of the mass market, but the pandemic has encouraged a revival of interest among older and more affluent travellers. They value the security of having flights, hotels and excursions arranged in advance, and the reassurance of a guide in case of difficulty. Operators have responded with smaller groups, more flexible itineraries and a focus on distinctive experiences, such as private visits to museums after closing time. Independent travellers sometimes disparage the idea of following a guide with a flag, but defenders point out that a good guide can transform a visit by providing context that a guidebook cannot.`,statements:[{text:`Package tours are described as having been aimed at the mass market.`,answer:!0},{text:`Operators have made groups larger.`,answer:!1},{text:`Defenders say a good guide gives useful context.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-181`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says children "may never achieve full command of grammar", the evidence "does not suggest that adults cannot learn", and adults "often progress more quickly in the early stages".`,passage:`Language learning in childhood appears to follow a sensitive period, after which acquiring a language becomes more difficult. Children who are deprived of language during early years, for example, may never achieve full command of grammar. Yet the evidence does not suggest that adults cannot learn. Adults often progress more quickly in the early stages, thanks to their ability to analyse rules and to use study strategies, although they rarely attain a native-like accent. Researchers therefore suggest that age affects the way in which languages are learned rather than determining whether learning is possible.`,statements:[{text:`Children deprived of early language may never fully master grammar.`,answer:!0},{text:`Adults cannot learn languages at all.`,answer:!1},{text:`Adults often progress quickly in the early stages.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-182`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the diet is "rich in vegetables, olive oil and fish", "fast food and processed products have become common", and "the diet is part of a way of life".`,passage:`The Mediterranean diet, rich in vegetables, olive oil and fish, is often recommended as a model of healthy eating. Travellers to southern Europe, however, may find the reality more varied. In recent decades, fast food and processed products have become common, and in some regions young people eat less traditionally than their grandparents did. Nutritionists point out that the diet is part of a way of life that includes eating slowly, sharing meals and walking regularly. Copying the ingredients without the habits, they suggest, may not produce the same benefits.`,statements:[{text:`The Mediterranean diet includes vegetables, olive oil and fish.`,answer:!0},{text:`Fast food is unknown in southern Europe.`,answer:!1},{text:`Nutritionists say the diet is part of a way of life.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-183`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says moving is "among the most stressful events", the stress "is not only logistical", and those who see "a fresh start... often adapt more quickly".`,passage:`Moving house is consistently ranked among the most stressful events in life, alongside bereavement and divorce. The practical burden is considerable: packing, arranging transport, changing addresses and coping with the uncertainty of a new neighbourhood. Yet psychologists note that the stress is not only logistical. A home carries emotional associations, and leaving it can feel like a loss even when the move is desired. People who plan carefully, delegate tasks and allow themselves time to settle generally report that the experience becomes more manageable. Those who treat the move as a fresh start, rather than a disruption, often adapt more quickly.`,statements:[{text:`Moving house is ranked among the most stressful life events.`,answer:!0},{text:`The stress of moving is purely practical.`,answer:!1},{text:`People who treat a move as a fresh start often adapt more quickly.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-184`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says trains are "enjoying a modest revival", tickets are "difficult to book across national borders", and campaigners argue governments should "tax aviation fuel".`,passage:`Long-distance trains in Europe, once threatened by cheap flights, are enjoying a modest revival. Concerns about the climate have made many travellers reluctant to fly, and new night services link cities that had lost their connections decades ago. Passengers value the opportunity to sleep while travelling and to arrive in the city centre rather than at a distant airport. The drawbacks are familiar: tickets can be expensive and difficult to book across national borders, and journeys that take a day by rail take two hours by air. Campaigners argue that governments should simplify ticketing and tax aviation fuel, so that the most environmentally friendly option is not also the most expensive.`,statements:[{text:`Long-distance trains are experiencing a revival in Europe.`,answer:!0},{text:`Booking train tickets across borders is described as simple.`,answer:!1},{text:`Campaigners want aviation fuel to be taxed.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-185`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says "immersion alone does not guarantee fluency", rapid progress comes from those who "seek out opportunities to interact with local people", and adults "are less able than children to absorb grammar unconsciously".`,passage:`Language learners are often advised to immerse themselves in the culture of the country where the language is spoken, but immersion alone does not guarantee fluency. Studies of expatriates show that many live for years in a foreign country while speaking mainly their own language with compatriots. Those who make rapid progress tend to be the ones who deliberately seek out opportunities to interact with local people, tolerate making mistakes and take an interest in aspects of the culture beyond the tourist attractions. Teachers add that adults need structured study as well as exposure, since they are less able than children to absorb grammar unconsciously. A combination of formal instruction and real communication produces the best results, they say.`,statements:[{text:`Living abroad automatically makes people fluent.`,answer:!1},{text:`Rapid progress is linked to seeking out interaction with local people.`,answer:!0},{text:`Teachers say that adults can absorb grammar unconsciously as easily as children.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-186`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says life satisfaction "has barely risen", the "hedonic treadmill" explains adaptation, and critics "doubt whether such indicators can be defined with sufficient precision".`,passage:`In the late twentieth century, many economists believed that growth in national income would inevitably lead to greater happiness. Yet surveys conducted in a number of wealthy countries have found that average reported life satisfaction has barely risen over several decades, despite substantial increases in income. One explanation, known as the "hedonic treadmill", holds that people adapt quickly to improvements in their circumstances and soon return to their previous level of contentment. Another stresses that happiness depends on relative rather than absolute income: what matters is how we compare with those around us. These findings have prompted some governments to measure wellbeing alongside economic output, although critics doubt whether such indicators can be defined with sufficient precision to guide policy.`,statements:[{text:`Surveys show a large rise in life satisfaction as incomes grew.`,answer:!1},{text:`One explanation is that people adapt quickly to better circumstances.`,answer:!0},{text:`All critics accept that wellbeing indicators are precise.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-187`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says satellites and forecasts "made long voyages more accessible", "the sea can be unforgiving", and "thorough preparation... is more important than courage".`,passage:`Sailing around the world used to be an adventure reserved for the most experienced seafarers. Today, navigation by satellite, reliable weather forecasts and improved equipment have made long voyages more accessible. Even so, the sea can be unforgiving. Sailors speak of the isolation, the fatigue of constant watchkeeping and the anxiety that comes with storms. Those who complete a circumnavigation often describe a sense of achievement and a changed perspective on what matters. Safety experts emphasise that thorough preparation, including training and a well-maintained boat, is more important than courage.`,statements:[{text:`Technology has made long voyages more accessible.`,answer:!0},{text:`The sea has become entirely safe.`,answer:!1},{text:`Safety experts say preparation matters more than courage.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-188`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says "the opposite seems to have happened", campuses have "free meals, gyms and sleeping pods", and the pandemic showed "how much casual conversation and mentoring are lost".`,passage:`In the early days of the internet, many commentators predicted that cheap communication would make physical offices obsolete. Two decades later, the opposite seems to have happened in some sectors: technology companies have built vast campuses with free meals, gyms and sleeping pods, designed to keep employees on site for as long as possible. Critics describe this as a subtle blurring of the boundary between work and private life, noting that long hours spent at the office make it harder to maintain relationships outside it. Defenders reply that the facilities foster creativity and that no employee is forced to use them. The pandemic complicated the picture still further, demonstrating that many tasks can be performed remotely while also showing how much casual conversation and mentoring are lost when colleagues never meet.`,statements:[{text:`Commentators correctly predicted that offices would disappear.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some technology companies provide facilities that keep staff on site.`,answer:!0},{text:`The pandemic proved that remote work has no disadvantages.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-189`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says incentives "may actually reduce performance" on complex work, targets "can also encourage cheating", and recognition and purpose "motivate more durably than money alone".`,passage:`The practice of paying employees bonuses for meeting targets is widespread, but evidence of its effectiveness is mixed. Bonuses can motivate effort on simple, measurable tasks. For complex work requiring creativity or teamwork, however, studies have found that financial incentives may actually reduce performance, as employees focus on the reward rather than the task. Targets can also encourage cheating, as some staff manipulate figures to reach them. Many organisations now supplement bonuses with recognition, development opportunities and a sense of purpose, which research suggests motivate more durably than money alone.`,statements:[{text:`Bonuses always improve performance on creative work.`,answer:!1},{text:`Targets can encourage cheating.`,answer:!0},{text:`Recognition and purpose may motivate more durably than money alone.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-190`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says late sleeping "can disrupt the body clock", extra sleep "does not fully repair" the effects, and the remedy is "regular hours throughout the week".`,passage:`For many people, the weekend is a time for catching up on sleep, but sleep scientists warn that this strategy has limits. Sleeping late on Saturday and Sunday can disrupt the body clock, making it harder to fall asleep on Sunday night and to wake on Monday morning, a phenomenon sometimes described as social jet lag. Extra sleep at weekends may relieve tiredness temporarily, but studies suggest that it does not fully repair the effects of a week of short nights. The best remedy, scientists advise, is to maintain regular hours throughout the week.`,statements:[{text:`Sleeping late on weekends can disrupt the body clock.`,answer:!0},{text:`Extra weekend sleep fully repairs a week of short nights.`,answer:!1},{text:`Scientists advise regular hours throughout the week.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-191`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says paint-making dates to "nearly a hundred thousand years ago", "it is not known what the paint was used for", and the mixture "indicat[es] a deliberate recipe".`,passage:`Archaeologists working in a cave in southern Africa have uncovered evidence that early humans were making paint nearly a hundred thousand years ago. The discovery was a toolkit containing shells used as containers, along with grinding stones and ochre, a reddish mineral. The mixture appears to have been blended with bone and charcoal, indicating a deliberate recipe. Researchers say the finding suggests that early humans planned ahead and possessed a degree of abstract thought earlier than previously believed. It is not known what the paint was used for, though body decoration is one possibility.`,statements:[{text:`The discovery dates from nearly a hundred thousand years ago.`,answer:!0},{text:`The researchers know exactly what the paint was used for.`,answer:!1},{text:`The mixture appears to follow a deliberate recipe.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-192`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says "most polls are reasonably accurate", "close contests are difficult to call", and the media "report single results with spurious precision".`,passage:`Opinion polls have a reputation for inaccuracy since a few high-profile failures to predict election results. In fact, most polls are reasonably accurate, but the margin of error means that close contests are difficult to call. Problems arise when pollsters fail to reach a representative sample, for example because certain groups are less willing to respond or because telephone surveys miss people without landlines. Experts suggest that the public should treat polls as snapshots rather than forecasts and pay attention to trends across several surveys. The media, however, tend to report single results with spurious precision, giving the impression of certainty where none exists.`,statements:[{text:`Most polls are described as reasonably accurate.`,answer:!0},{text:`Polls can easily call very close contests.`,answer:!1},{text:`The media tend to report single results with false precision.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-193`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says waste "breaks into tiny fragments", cleaning up "is extremely difficult", and "the most effective approach is to prevent plastic from entering the water".`,passage:`Plastic pollution in the oceans has become a focus of international concern. Each year millions of tonnes of waste enter the sea, where it breaks into tiny fragments that are eaten by fish and seabirds and may enter the human food chain. Cleaning up is extremely difficult, because the particles are spread over vast areas and often sink. Experts therefore stress that the most effective approach is to prevent plastic from entering the water in the first place, through better waste management, reduced packaging and the redesign of products so that they can be recycled.`,statements:[{text:`Plastic in the sea breaks into tiny fragments.`,answer:!0},{text:`Cleaning the oceans is easy.`,answer:!1},{text:`Experts say prevention is the most effective approach.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-194`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says women "remain under-represented", supporters call targets "necessary to overcome entrenched habits", and "the quality of appointees has not suffered".`,passage:`Women remain under-represented in senior positions in many professions, despite decades of equal opportunity legislation. Studies suggest several causes: unconscious bias in recruitment, the difficulty of combining demanding careers with childcare, and a shortage of role models. Some companies have introduced targets for the proportion of women in senior posts, a policy that critics call unfair to men and supporters call necessary to overcome entrenched habits. Evidence from countries that have introduced quotas suggests that the change has been accepted more readily than opponents predicted, and that the quality of appointees has not suffered.`,statements:[{text:`Equal opportunity laws have eliminated the under-representation of women.`,answer:!1},{text:`Supporters say targets are needed to overcome entrenched habits.`,answer:!0},{text:`Evidence shows that quotas lowered the quality of appointees.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-195`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says people "notice price rises on frequently purchased items... leading them to overestimate", economists "disagree about which better explains recent episodes", and rates "can curb prices but also slow growth".`,passage:`Few phenomena are as poorly understood by the public as inflation. Surveys repeatedly show that people tend to notice price rises on frequently purchased items, such as petrol and bread, while overlooking declines in the price of durable goods, which leads them to overestimate the overall rate. Economists distinguish between inflation caused by excessive demand and that driven by rising costs, such as energy prices, and they disagree about which better explains recent episodes. Central banks typically respond by raising interest rates, a blunt instrument that can curb prices but also slow growth and increase unemployment. The most damaging effect may be on expectations: if people come to believe that prices will keep rising, they demand higher wages, which in turn pushes prices up further.`,statements:[{text:`People tend to overestimate inflation because they notice some prices more than others.`,answer:!0},{text:`Economists agree on the main cause of recent inflation.`,answer:!1},{text:`Raising interest rates has no effect on economic growth.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-196`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says "the brain consolidates what has been learned", all-night revision "is... a poor strategy", and educators "advise spacing study over several days".`,passage:`Many people find that they think more clearly after a good night's sleep, and scientists have identified a reason. During sleep, the brain consolidates what has been learned the previous day, transferring information from short-term to long-term memory. Students who study and then sleep perform better on tests than those who study and stay awake, even if the latter spend more time reviewing. Staying up all night to revise before an examination is therefore a poor strategy. Educators advise spacing study over several days and ensuring that students get enough sleep, especially in the nights before important assessments.`,statements:[{text:`The brain consolidates learning during sleep.`,answer:!0},{text:`Staying awake all night to revise is recommended.`,answer:!1},{text:`Educators advise spacing study over several days.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-197`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says bleaching occurs when corals "expel the algae", "rising sea temperatures are the main cause", and breeding "can only buy time unless... emissions are reduced".`,passage:`The Great Barrier Reef, the world's largest coral system, has suffered several episodes of mass bleaching in recent years. Bleaching occurs when stressed corals expel the algae that live in their tissues and give them colour and nourishment; if conditions do not improve quickly, the coral may die. Rising sea temperatures are the main cause. Scientists have found that some corals recover in cooler seasons, and a few areas appear more resilient than others. Researchers are experimenting with breeding heat-tolerant corals, but they stress that such measures can only buy time unless greenhouse gas emissions are reduced.`,statements:[{text:`Bleaching happens when corals expel the algae in their tissues.`,answer:!0},{text:`Rising sea temperatures are the main cause.`,answer:!0},{text:`Researchers say breeding heat-tolerant corals alone will solve the problem.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-198`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says "the type of use matters as much as the quantity", "children observe how adults behave", and rules work best "when they are agreed with children in discussion".`,passage:`Many parents worry about the amount of time their children spend on screens. Researchers advise that the type of use matters as much as the quantity: creative activities, such as making videos or learning to code, are different from passively scrolling through feeds. Setting boundaries, such as device-free meals and a bedtime without screens, is widely recommended. Experts also suggest that parents set an example, since children observe how adults behave. Rules are most effective, they add, when they are agreed with children in discussion and not simply imposed.`,statements:[{text:`The type of screen use matters as well as the amount.`,answer:!0},{text:`Experts say children do not notice adults' behaviour.`,answer:!1},{text:`Rules are most effective when agreed with children.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-199`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says writing "has long been dominated by voices from wealthy countries", new writers are "turning the gaze back on Europe", and "some critics suspect that the enthusiasm is a passing fashion".`,passage:`Travel writing has long been dominated by voices from wealthy countries, describing the supposedly exotic customs of others. In recent years, a new generation of writers from formerly colonised regions has challenged this tradition, turning the gaze back on Europe and North America and questioning the assumptions of earlier authors. Their accounts often reveal how unfamiliar and strange the habits of visitors appear to the people they meet. Publishers have responded with growing interest, although some critics suspect that the enthusiasm is a passing fashion. What seems beyond dispute is that the genre is being enriched, since a journey looks very different depending on who is looking and from where.`,statements:[{text:`Travel writing has traditionally been dominated by writers from wealthy countries.`,answer:!0},{text:`The new writers avoid commenting on Europe and North America.`,answer:!1},{text:`All critics believe the interest in the new writers is permanent.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-200`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says smallpox "was eradicated in 1980", "diseases that had been controlled have returned", and authorities emphasise "listening to the concerns of parents".`,passage:`The development of vaccines against infectious diseases is one of the greatest achievements of modern medicine. Smallpox, which once killed millions, was eradicated in 1980 after a worldwide campaign of vaccination, and polio has been reduced by more than ninety-nine per cent. Yet progress depends on public confidence. Where fear of side effects, often spread by false information, leads to lower vaccination rates, diseases that had been controlled have returned. Health authorities therefore emphasise the importance of clear communication and of listening to the concerns of parents rather than dismissing them.`,statements:[{text:`Smallpox was eradicated in 1980.`,answer:!0},{text:`Diseases cannot return once controlled.`,answer:!1},{text:`Authorities stress listening to parents' concerns.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-201`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says pressure is "more than a thousand times" sea level, explorers "found signs of life", and "plastic waste" was discovered.`,passage:`The deepest part of the ocean, the Mariana Trench, lies nearly eleven kilometres below the surface. The pressure there is more than a thousand times that at sea level, and no sunlight reaches it. Only a handful of people have visited the bottom in submersibles. Yet when explorers arrived, they found signs of life: small shrimp-like creatures and microbes thriving in the darkness. Scientists were also dismayed to discover plastic waste at the bottom, showing that human pollution has reached even the most remote place on Earth.`,statements:[{text:`The pressure in the trench is far greater than at sea level.`,answer:!0},{text:`Explorers found no life at the bottom.`,answer:!1},{text:`Plastic waste was found at the bottom.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-202`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says quality "depends less on what is said than on how well people listen", "listening... is not the same as agreement", and employees rate such managers "as more trustworthy".`,passage:`The quality of a conversation often depends less on what is said than on how well people listen. A good listener maintains eye contact, asks questions that show interest and resists the urge to offer advice before it is requested. Surveys of employees show that they rate managers who listen well as more trustworthy, regardless of how often the managers agree with them. Listening, however, is not the same as agreement: one can listen carefully and still disagree. The skill lies in making the other person feel understood, which often defuses conflict before it begins.`,statements:[{text:`The quality of a conversation depends only on what is said.`,answer:!1},{text:`Listening well is the same as agreeing.`,answer:!1},{text:`Employees rate managers who listen well as more trustworthy.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-203`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says apps let visitors "find their way... without ever consulting a map", some writers "lament" the loss of challenges, and supporters say "nobody is obliged to use it".`,passage:`Not all travellers regard the independence offered by modern technology as an advantage. Navigation apps allow visitors to find their way in unfamiliar cities without ever consulting a map or asking for directions, and translation tools remove many language difficulties. Yet some travel writers lament that these conveniences eliminate the small challenges and chance encounters that once made journeys memorable. Getting lost, they argue, forces travellers to look around, to talk to strangers and to discover places they would never have planned to visit. Supporters of the technology reply that nobody is obliged to use it, and that a traveller who wishes to be lost can always switch off the phone.`,statements:[{text:`Navigation apps help travellers without maps.`,answer:!0},{text:`All travel writers welcome these technologies.`,answer:!1},{text:`Supporters argue that travellers can choose not to use the technology.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-204`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says "the timing varies from year to year", locals "have mixed feelings", and visitors "are advised to travel on weekdays".`,passage:`Autumn in New England is famous for its colours, and thousands of visitors travel to the region to see the leaves turn red, orange and gold. The timing varies from year to year, depending on temperature and rainfall, so forecasts are closely followed. Roadside stalls sell pumpkins and cider, and small inns fill up months in advance. Local people have mixed feelings: they welcome the income but complain of traffic on narrow roads. Leaf-watchers are advised to travel on weekdays and to explore side roads, where the display is often just as beautiful and far less crowded.`,statements:[{text:`The timing of the colours varies from year to year.`,answer:!0},{text:`Local people are entirely happy with the visitors.`,answer:!1},{text:`Leaf-watchers are advised to travel on weekdays.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-205`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says sailors "could determine their latitude... but not their longitude", he "struggled to win recognition", and "he was rewarded only after the king intervened".`,passage:`The development of the first clocks that could keep accurate time at sea saved countless lives. Before their invention, sailors could determine their latitude from the sun or stars but not their longitude, and many ships were wrecked after straying off course. The solution, a precision timepiece that remained accurate despite the motion of the ship and changes in temperature, was built by a self-taught clockmaker after decades of work. He struggled to win recognition, as the official board preferred a method based on astronomy, and he was rewarded only after the king intervened.`,statements:[{text:`Sailors could not determine longitude before accurate clocks.`,answer:!0},{text:`The clockmaker received instant recognition.`,answer:!1},{text:`The king intervened to help him be rewarded.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-206`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says "all regions of the brain are active at some time", damage "has some effect", and the myth is attractive because "it suggests that we all have hidden potential".`,passage:`The idea that we use only ten per cent of our brains is one of the most persistent myths in popular psychology. Brain imaging shows that all regions of the brain are active at some time, and that even during sleep many areas continue to function. Damage to almost any part of the brain has some effect on behaviour. The myth probably survives because it is attractive: it suggests that we all have hidden potential waiting to be unlocked. Neuroscientists point out that the real challenge is to understand how the many parts of the brain work together.`,statements:[{text:`All regions of the brain are active at some time.`,answer:!0},{text:`Damage to most parts of the brain has no effect.`,answer:!1},{text:`The myth survives because it suggests hidden potential.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-207`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says chatbots answer "at any hour", customers "become frustrated", and experts predict "combining the two".`,passage:`Customer service has been transformed by the arrival of chatbots, which can answer simple questions at any hour. Companies value the savings, as a single program can handle thousands of enquiries at once. Customers, however, are less enthusiastic. Surveys suggest that many become frustrated when a chatbot fails to understand their problem, and prefer to speak to a person. The best systems therefore pass difficult cases quickly to human staff. Experts predict that the future lies in combining the two, with automation handling routine matters and people dealing with complexity and emotion.`,statements:[{text:`Chatbots can answer questions at any hour.`,answer:!0},{text:`Customers are generally delighted with chatbots.`,answer:!1},{text:`Experts predict a combination of automation and human staff.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-208`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the number "surprised many scientists, who had expected a far larger number", "a single gene can produce several different proteins", and "most common conditions involve many genes".`,passage:`The discovery that the human genome contains only around twenty thousand genes surprised many scientists, who had expected a far larger number given the complexity of human beings. The explanation, it emerged, lies less in the quantity of genes than in the ways they are regulated and combined. A single gene can produce several different proteins, and vast stretches of DNA once dismissed as "junk" turn out to control when and where genes are switched on. This realisation has transformed the study of heredity, shifting attention from individual genes to complex networks. It has also tempered early hopes that diseases could be cured by correcting single defective genes; most common conditions involve many genes interacting with the environment.`,statements:[{text:`Scientists had expected humans to have fewer genes than they do.`,answer:!1},{text:`A single gene can produce more than one protein.`,answer:!0},{text:`The new understanding suggests most common diseases involve a single gene.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-209`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says guests sleep "on futons laid over tatami mats", "prices are high", and a stay is a "contrast to the bustle of Japanese cities".`,passage:`The tradition of staying in a ryokan, a Japanese inn, offers travellers a glimpse of an older way of life. Guests sleep on futons laid over tatami mats, wear cotton robes and eat meals of many small dishes served in their rooms. Many ryokan have hot spring baths, which guests are expected to wash before entering. Prices are high, and the pace is slow, but visitors value the attentive service and calm atmosphere. Staying in one is often recommended as a contrast to the bustle of Japanese cities.`,statements:[{text:`Guests sleep on futons over tatami mats.`,answer:!0},{text:`Ryokan are described as cheap.`,answer:!1},{text:`Staying in a ryokan is recommended as a contrast to city bustle.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-210`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says those with wide circles "were healthier and happier", work friendships "often fade with retirement", and researchers recommend starting "well before retirement".`,passage:`A survey of retired people found that those who had maintained a wide circle of friends were healthier and happier than those whose social lives had narrowed. Friendship seems to provide emotional support, practical help and a sense of purpose. Yet it is also fragile: friendships made at work often fade with retirement, and moving house can sever ties of a lifetime. Researchers recommend that people begin to cultivate new connections well before retirement, through clubs, volunteering or shared interests, rather than waiting until loneliness sets in.`,statements:[{text:`Retired people with wide circles of friends were healthier and happier.`,answer:!0},{text:`Friendships made at work always last after retirement.`,answer:!1},{text:`Researchers advise cultivating new connections before retirement.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-211`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the dome is "unreinforced", lime fragments "give it the ability to heal its own cracks", and the finding "could lead to" better concrete.`,passage:`Roman concrete has endured for two thousand years, outlasting many modern structures. The Pantheon in Rome, built in the second century, still has the largest unreinforced concrete dome in the world. For a long time, scientists assumed that the secret lay in the use of volcanic ash. More recent analysis, however, has revealed small white fragments of lime in the material, which seem to give it the ability to heal its own cracks: when water seeps in, it reacts with the lime to form new crystals that fill the gaps. If this finding is confirmed, it could lead to more durable modern concrete, reducing the enormous emissions associated with cement production.`,statements:[{text:`The Pantheon's dome is made of reinforced concrete.`,answer:!1},{text:`The lime fragments may allow the concrete to heal cracks.`,answer:!0},{text:`The finding has already been used in all modern concrete.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-212`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says off-season has "lower prices", hotels and restaurants are "closing for the winter", and businesses "are... grateful for off-season trade".`,passage:`Many popular holiday destinations are seasonal, with hotels and restaurants closing for the winter. Visitors who arrive in the off-season find the places very different: quiet beaches, lower prices and local people with time to talk. Some travellers deliberately choose such times, seeking a more authentic experience. The disadvantages are the weather, which may be cold and wet, and the limited choice of activities. Local businesses are, however, grateful for off-season trade, which provides income at a time when it is most needed.`,statements:[{text:`Off-season visitors find lower prices.`,answer:!0},{text:`Hotels and restaurants always stay open in winter.`,answer:!1},{text:`Local businesses welcome off-season trade.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-213`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says "queues of climbers form near the summit", delay "can be dangerous", and authorities "tightened rules on permits".`,passage:`Mount Everest has become a victim of its own popularity. In peak season, queues of climbers form near the summit, waiting in freezing conditions for their turn to pass narrow sections of the route. The delay can be dangerous, since time at extreme altitude exhausts oxygen supplies and increases the risk of frostbite. Critics argue that inexperienced climbers, attracted by guided expeditions, are putting themselves and the Sherpas who support them at risk. The Nepalese authorities have tightened rules on permits, but some mountaineers believe that numbers should be limited more strictly.`,statements:[{text:`Climbers wait in queues near the summit.`,answer:!0},{text:`The delays are described as harmless.`,answer:!1},{text:`Nepalese authorities have tightened rules on permits.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-214`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says it is "the world's most visited country", sites are "overcrowded" in summer, and the authorities encourage travel "outside the peak season".`,passage:`Many travellers are surprised to learn that the world's most visited country is not one of the famous long-haul destinations but France, which receives tens of millions of visitors a year. The attractions are varied: Paris and its museums, the beaches of the Riviera, the vineyards of Bordeaux and the castles of the Loire. Yet the country's popularity is not without problems. In summer, famous sites are overcrowded, and some residents in tourist areas complain that local services have been squeezed. The authorities are therefore encouraging visitors to explore less well-known regions and to travel outside the peak season.`,statements:[{text:`France is described as the world's most visited country.`,answer:!0},{text:`Famous sites are empty in summer.`,answer:!1},{text:`The authorities encourage travel outside the peak season.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-215`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says "many offices have relaxed dress codes", supporters say comfortable clothes "improve creativity", and "younger workers expect flexibility".`,passage:`The practice of dressing formally for work is changing. Many offices have relaxed dress codes, and in some companies employees may wear jeans and trainers every day. Supporters argue that comfortable clothes make people feel more at ease, which improves creativity and wellbeing. Critics warn that casual dress can blur the boundary between work and leisure and may lower standards of behaviour. Some firms have found a compromise by dressing formally only when meeting clients. Surveys suggest that younger workers expect flexibility, while older employees are more likely to prefer clear rules about what is appropriate.`,statements:[{text:`Many offices have relaxed their dress codes.`,answer:!0},{text:`Supporters think comfortable clothes harm creativity.`,answer:!1},{text:`Younger workers expect flexibility.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-216`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says it "is rarely a priority", young adults "may fall into debt", and supporters argue it "should begin early".`,passage:`Few skills are as useful as the ability to manage one's money, yet financial education is rarely a priority in schools. Surveys show that many young adults leave education without understanding basic concepts such as interest, budgeting or the cost of borrowing. As a result, they may fall into debt through credit cards or loans with high charges. Educators who support teaching personal finance argue that it should begin early and use practical examples, such as planning the cost of a school trip. Opponents say that the curriculum is already crowded and that parents should take responsibility.`,statements:[{text:`Financial education is a priority in most schools.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some young adults fall into debt through lack of understanding.`,answer:!0},{text:`Supporters think personal finance should begin early.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-217`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the interview "is a poor predictor", "work-sample tests" produce better results, and managers "rarely accept that their instincts may be unreliable".`,passage:`A growing body of research suggests that the traditional job interview is a poor predictor of future performance. Interviewers tend to favour candidates who resemble themselves or who are articulate and self-assured, qualities that do not always correlate with competence. Structured interviews, in which every candidate answers the same questions and responses are scored against agreed criteria, produce markedly better results. So do work-sample tests, in which applicants perform a task similar to the one they would do in the job. Despite this evidence, many employers continue to rely on unstructured conversations, partly from habit and partly because they trust their own judgement. Consultants who advocate reform admit that changing practice is difficult, since managers rarely accept that their instincts may be unreliable.`,statements:[{text:`Unstructured interviews are the most reliable way to predict performance.`,answer:!1},{text:`Work-sample tests are mentioned as a better method.`,answer:!0},{text:`Managers readily accept that their instincts may be unreliable.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-218`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says a person can travel "three or four times as far", "fear of traffic deters people", and numbers rise "including among groups who had not previously considered riding".`,passage:`The humble bicycle has been called one of the most efficient machines ever invented. It converts a larger proportion of the rider's energy into motion than a walker does, and allows a person to travel three or four times as far with the same effort. Cycling to work reduces traffic, pollution and the cost of commuting, and brings health benefits. Yet in many places, fear of traffic deters people from cycling. Surveys show that where protected cycle lanes are built, the numbers of cyclists rise, including among groups who had not previously considered riding, such as older people and parents with children.`,statements:[{text:`A cyclist travels as far as a walker with the same effort.`,answer:!1},{text:`Fear of traffic stops many people from cycling.`,answer:!0},{text:`Protected lanes attract new groups of cyclists.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-219`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says styles "return in updated forms", cheap clothes "are produced in weeks", and the shift "will have little impact unless the industry changes".`,passage:`Fashion tends to repeat itself, with styles from earlier decades returning in updated forms. Designers draw on archives for inspiration, and consumers who remember a style from youth may feel nostalgic when it reappears. At the same time, the speed of change has increased. Cheap clothes that follow trends are produced in weeks and discarded after a few wearings, creating waste. A growing number of people are therefore buying second-hand or choosing fewer, better-made garments. Environmental campaigners welcome this shift but argue that it will have little impact unless the industry changes the way it produces clothing.`,statements:[{text:`Fashion styles often return in updated forms.`,answer:!0},{text:`Trend-following clothes are produced slowly.`,answer:!1},{text:`Campaigners say consumers alone cannot change the industry's impact.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-220`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says "mortality from waterborne disease fell dramatically", "success is marked by the absence of illness", and "millions still lack access to safe water".`,passage:`During the nineteenth century, chlorination of drinking water transformed public health in industrial cities. Before then, epidemics of cholera and typhoid were common, spread through contaminated supplies. Once treatment became widespread, mortality from waterborne disease fell dramatically, and life expectancy rose. The achievement is easy to overlook, because success is marked by the absence of illness. Yet in many parts of the world, millions still lack access to safe water, and the diseases that cause the most deaths among children are precisely those that clean water prevents. Public health experts therefore consider investment in water and sanitation among the most cost-effective ways of saving lives.`,statements:[{text:`Chlorination reduced deaths from waterborne disease.`,answer:!0},{text:`The benefits of clean water are always highly visible.`,answer:!1},{text:`Millions of people still lack access to safe water.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-221`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the assumption "sits uneasily with the experience of many modern employees", the right "prohibits employers from contacting staff outside working hours", and "some workers say they would rather keep the flexibility".`,passage:`The notion of "work-life balance" presupposes that work and life are separate domains, an assumption that sits uneasily with the experience of many modern employees. For those who answer emails at the dinner table or take calls on holiday, the boundary has dissolved. Some commentators suggest that the phrase should be replaced by "work-life integration", acknowledging that the two spheres inevitably overlap. Critics reply that this euphemism legitimises the expectation of constant availability and shifts the burden onto individuals to protect their own time. Several countries have introduced a "right to disconnect", which prohibits employers from contacting staff outside working hours. Evidence about its effectiveness is mixed, since enforcement is difficult, and some workers say they would rather keep the flexibility to organise their own schedules.`,statements:[{text:`The writer suggests that work and life are clearly separate for most modern employees.`,answer:!1},{text:`A "right to disconnect" prevents employers from contacting staff outside working hours.`,answer:!0},{text:`All workers welcome the right to disconnect.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-222`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says writing by hand "engages regions of the brain associated with memory and language", handwriters "cannot transcribe every word", and "some schools... have reintroduced it".`,passage:`Few people today write by hand for any length of time, and many find that their handwriting has deteriorated. Yet neurologists suggest that the loss may matter. Writing by hand involves fine motor control and engages regions of the brain associated with memory and language in ways that typing apparently does not. Students who take notes by hand tend to process the material more actively, since they cannot transcribe every word and must decide what is important. Some schools in countries that had abandoned handwriting teaching have reintroduced it. Sceptics respond that typing is faster and that future generations will find different ways of thinking, as every new technology has demanded.`,statements:[{text:`Neurologists suggest that handwriting involves brain regions linked to memory and language.`,answer:!0},{text:`Students who handwrite notes transcribe every word.`,answer:!1},{text:`All schools have abandoned the teaching of handwriting.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-223`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says "we reconstruct it rather than replay it", estimates differed depending on "smashed" or "hit", and "confidence is a poor guide to accuracy".`,passage:`Memory is notoriously unreliable, as anyone who has compared recollections of a shared event with a sibling will know. Psychologists have shown that each time we recall an event, we reconstruct it rather than replay it, and that details can be altered by suggestion, mood and subsequent experiences. In a famous series of experiments, participants who watched a film of a car accident gave different estimates of the cars' speed depending on whether the question used the word "smashed" or "hit". Such findings have implications for the courtroom, where eyewitness testimony carries considerable weight. Yet people tend to be most confident about memories that are vivid and emotional, and confidence is a poor guide to accuracy.`,statements:[{text:`Recalling an event involves reconstructing it.`,answer:!0},{text:`The word used in a question did not affect speed estimates.`,answer:!1},{text:`People's confidence in a memory reliably shows that it is accurate.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-224`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says it "became the wealthiest nation in Europe", tolerance "was pragmatic rather than principled", and prosperity "depended in part on a brutal colonial empire".`,passage:`In the seventeenth century, the Dutch Republic became the wealthiest nation in Europe, and one explanation lies in its relative tolerance. Persecuted religious minorities, among them Jews and French Protestants, found refuge in its cities, bringing skills, capital and trading connections. Printing presses flourished because authors could publish works that would have been censored elsewhere. The government, a loose federation dominated by merchants, had little interest in enforcing orthodoxy at the expense of commerce. Historians caution, however, against idealising the Republic: tolerance was pragmatic rather than principled, Catholics were barred from public worship, and the nation's prosperity depended in part on a brutal colonial empire. The Dutch example shows that openness can be economically productive, but it was never as generous as later myths suggest.`,statements:[{text:`The Dutch Republic became the wealthiest nation in Europe in the seventeenth century.`,answer:!0},{text:`The Republic's tolerance was based on strong moral principles.`,answer:!1},{text:`The nation's wealth partly relied on a colonial empire.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-225`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says planners "separated pedestrians from traffic", Jacobs argued for "mixed uses, short blocks and... eyes on the street", and such neighbourhoods "are now unaffordable to many".`,passage:`When urban planners in the 1950s designed new housing estates, they often separated pedestrians from traffic, placing walkways above the streets and creating large open spaces between the buildings. The intention was to make life safer and more pleasant, but the results frequently disappointed. The open spaces were poorly supervised, the walkways became places where crime flourished, and residents felt isolated. Later reformers, notably the writer Jane Jacobs, argued that vibrant neighbourhoods depend on mixed uses, short blocks and the presence of "eyes on the street" provided by people going about their business. Her ideas have been widely adopted, although critics note that the neighbourhoods that most closely resemble her ideal have become so popular that they are now unaffordable to many of the people who once lived there.`,statements:[{text:`The 1950s estates separated pedestrians from traffic.`,answer:!0},{text:`Jane Jacobs supported isolated open spaces between buildings.`,answer:!1},{text:`Neighbourhoods resembling Jacobs's ideal have sometimes become unaffordable.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-226`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says "the number of Americans who bowled had increased, but the number who belonged to bowling leagues had fallen", Putnam linked it to "television, suburban sprawl and generational change", and critics note "new forms of association, many of them online".`,passage:`When the sociologist Robert Putnam published his influential study of American community life, its title, "Bowling Alone", captured a striking observation: the number of Americans who bowled had increased, but the number who belonged to bowling leagues had fallen. He argued that this reflected a broader decline in "social capital", the networks of trust and cooperation that make societies function. Putnam linked the trend to television, suburban sprawl and generational change, and warned of consequences ranging from weaker democracy to poorer health. Critics have questioned his evidence, noting that new forms of association, many of them online, have replaced older ones. Putnam himself has acknowledged that the picture is complicated, but he maintains that face-to-face contact remains crucial for building trust across social divides.`,statements:[{text:`More Americans were bowling but fewer belonged to leagues.`,answer:!0},{text:`Putnam blamed the internet for the decline in social capital.`,answer:!1},{text:`Critics claim that new forms of association have emerged.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-227`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the evidence "is mixed", it is "difficult to separate the effects of ageing from those of generation", and core attitudes "remain fairly stable" after early adulthood.`,passage:`There is a widespread belief that people become more conservative as they grow older, but the evidence is mixed. Surveys over decades show that attitudes to some issues, such as authority and tradition, tend to harden with age, while views on others, such as civil liberties, may actually become more liberal. Researchers point out that it is difficult to separate the effects of ageing from those of generation: today's elderly grew up in a different social climate from today's young, and their opinions may reflect the values of their youth rather than a change of heart. Studies that follow the same individuals over time suggest that most people's core political attitudes are formed in early adulthood and then remain fairly stable.`,statements:[{text:`Surveys show that all attitudes become more conservative with age.`,answer:!1},{text:`It is hard to separate the effects of ageing from the effects of generation.`,answer:!0},{text:`Studies suggest core political attitudes mostly change dramatically in old age.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-228`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says "the results were poor", "a neighbour who had gardened for fifty years advised me", and "I stopped regarding every lost lettuce as a personal defeat".`,passage:`The first time I tried to grow vegetables, I treated the garden as a problem to be solved by efficiency. I measured the soil, planned the planting in neat rows and bought a book of schedules. The results were poor. The following year, a neighbour who had gardened for fifty years advised me to watch the garden rather than to manage it. She taught me to read the weather, to notice which corners received the afternoon sun and to accept that some failures were inevitable. My second harvest was better, but the real change was in my attitude: I learned to enjoy the process and stopped regarding every lost lettuce as a personal defeat.`,statements:[{text:`The writer's first attempt at gardening was successful.`,answer:!1},{text:`A neighbour with long experience gave the writer advice.`,answer:!0},{text:`The writer's attitude to failure changed.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-229`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says "the system favours the wealthy", "several countries" (not all) require pay, and "paid internships lead to better job offers than unpaid ones".`,passage:`Interns have long performed unpaid work in the hope of gaining experience and, eventually, a job. Critics argue that the system favours the wealthy, since only those with financial support can afford to work for nothing, and that it allows employers to avoid paying for labour. Several countries have introduced legislation requiring that interns be paid at least the minimum wage when they perform real work. Employers' organisations respond that such rules may reduce the number of opportunities. Evidence is mixed: some studies indicate that paid internships lead to better job offers than unpaid ones.`,statements:[{text:`Critics say unpaid internships favour the wealthy.`,answer:!0},{text:`All countries require interns to be paid.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some studies suggest paid internships lead to better job offers.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-230`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says QWERTY was "adopted in the 1870s", it endured "because millions of people had already learnt it", and historians say the evidence for rival layouts "is less convincing than often claimed".`,passage:`The history of the typewriter keyboard illustrates how a design chosen for one reason can persist long after the reason has vanished. The QWERTY layout, adopted in the 1870s, is often said to have been devised to prevent mechanical arms from jamming by separating frequently used letters. Rival layouts designed for speed have been available for decades, and enthusiasts claim they reduce finger movement. Yet QWERTY has endured, largely because millions of people had already learnt it and no individual had an incentive to switch. Economists use the example to illustrate "path dependence", the way early decisions constrain later choices. Some historians, however, caution that the story has been oversimplified and that the evidence for a clear efficiency advantage in rival layouts is less convincing than often claimed.`,statements:[{text:`QWERTY was designed in the twentieth century.`,answer:!1},{text:`QWERTY has lasted because many people already knew it.`,answer:!0},{text:`Historians unanimously agree that rival layouts are clearly more efficient.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-231`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says it is "increasingly common", solo travel has "the higher cost of accommodation for one person", and most say "the experience increased their confidence".`,passage:`Travelling alone has become increasingly common, particularly among women. Solo travellers speak of freedom: they can follow their own interests, change plans at will and meet people more easily than when in a group. They also acknowledge disadvantages, such as loneliness, the higher cost of accommodation for one person and concern about safety. Guidebooks and websites offer advice, such as telling someone one's itinerary and avoiding isolated places after dark. Most solo travellers say that the experience increased their confidence and that they would do it again.`,statements:[{text:`Solo travelling is becoming more common.`,answer:!0},{text:`Accommodation costs are lower for solo travellers.`,answer:!1},{text:`Most solo travellers say the experience increased their confidence.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-232`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says results are "inconsistent", music "interferes with tasks requiring reading or writing since... lyrics", and "introverts tend to be more disturbed by music".`,passage:`Research into the effects of background music on work has produced inconsistent results. Some studies suggest that familiar, unobtrusive music can improve mood and concentration, particularly in repetitive tasks. Others find that it interferes with tasks requiring reading or writing, since the brain's language centres are engaged by lyrics. Individual differences matter: introverts tend to be more disturbed by music than extroverts, and people who are accustomed to studying with music may perform worse without it. Employers, meanwhile, are caught between those who want music for atmosphere and those who find it distracting, and some have resorted to providing headphones and quiet zones so that everyone can choose.`,statements:[{text:`Research results on background music have been consistent.`,answer:!1},{text:`Lyrics may interfere with reading and writing tasks.`,answer:!0},{text:`Introverts are generally less disturbed by music than extroverts.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-233`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says clever-labelled pupils "avoid challenges", effort-praised pupils "persist after failure", and critics warn interventions "can be oversimplified or applied superficially".`,passage:`Teachers often assume that praising children's intelligence will raise their confidence, but studies suggest the opposite. Pupils who are told that they are clever tend to avoid challenges that might reveal limitations, and they give up quickly when they encounter difficulty. In contrast, pupils who are praised for effort and strategy are more willing to attempt hard tasks and to persist after failure. Psychologists attribute the difference to the belief that ability is either fixed or capable of growth. Schools that have introduced programmes teaching pupils that the brain develops through practice report improvements in motivation, though critics warn that such interventions can be oversimplified or applied superficially.`,statements:[{text:`Praising intelligence tends to increase children's willingness to take on challenges.`,answer:!1},{text:`Pupils praised for effort tend to persist after failure.`,answer:!0},{text:`Critics think such programmes can be applied superficially.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-234`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says "few skills are as undervalued", listening "contributes to better decisions", and the most difficult part is "suppressing the impulse to offer advice".`,passage:`Few skills are as undervalued as the ability to listen. In most workplaces, the voluble are rewarded, while those who listen carefully are overlooked. Yet studies of high-performing teams show that effective listening contributes to better decisions, since information is shared more freely and misunderstandings are caught early. Training programmes teach techniques such as paraphrasing what the speaker has said, asking open questions and resisting the urge to interrupt. Participants frequently report that the most difficult part is suppressing the impulse to offer advice before the other person has finished explaining. Managers who have mastered the skill say it has transformed their relationships with their staff, who feel valued and are therefore more willing to raise problems early.`,statements:[{text:`Listening is highly valued in most workplaces.`,answer:!1},{text:`Effective listening is linked to better decisions.`,answer:!0},{text:`Participants find it easy to avoid giving advice too soon.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-235`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says earlier work followed "the rhythm of the seasons", workers wanted "not the abolition of the clock but control over it", and the struggle was "over the ownership of time".`,passage:`The introduction of the factory system in the eighteenth century changed not only how goods were made but also how time was understood. Before then, most people worked according to the rhythm of the seasons and the demands of the task. In factories, the clock became the master: workers were fined for lateness, and the pace was set by machinery. Resistance took the form of absenteeism and drinking, particularly on Mondays. Over time, workers began to demand not the abolition of the clock but control over it, in the form of shorter hours and a weekend. The struggle for the eight-hour day was thus a struggle over the ownership of time.`,statements:[{text:`Before factories, work was organised around the seasons and the task.`,answer:!0},{text:`Workers in factories wanted to abolish the clock.`,answer:!1},{text:`The struggle for shorter hours was a struggle over time.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-236`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the gap "averages only about two hundred milliseconds", listeners "begin preparing their reply while the other person is still speaking", and the pattern "holds across very different languages".`,passage:`The art of conversation, it is sometimes lamented, is dying. Yet linguists who record everyday talk find that people still manage a remarkable feat of coordination: the gap between one speaker finishing and the next beginning averages only about two hundred milliseconds, far shorter than the time needed to plan a sentence. This implies that listeners begin preparing their reply while the other person is still speaking. The pattern holds across very different languages, suggesting that it is a human universal rather than a cultural habit. Mobile phones, on the other hand, have introduced new complications, since video calls often involve a slight delay that disrupts this timing and leaves participants talking over one another.`,statements:[{text:`The gap between speakers' turns is very short.`,answer:!0},{text:`Listeners wait until the speaker has finished before planning a reply.`,answer:!1},{text:`The timing pattern appears only in a few languages.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-237`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the composition has been linked "to conditions ranging from obesity... to depression", evidence "comes from studies on mice", and the advice is to "eat a varied diet rich in fibre".`,passage:`Few scientific ideas have travelled as far from the laboratory into everyday conversation as that of the "gut microbiome", the community of trillions of microorganisms living in the human digestive tract. Researchers have linked its composition to conditions ranging from obesity and diabetes to depression, and the commercial world has responded with an array of probiotic yoghurts, supplements and personalised diets. Yet many scientists urge caution. Most of the evidence so far comes from studies on mice, whose guts differ significantly from our own, and even in human studies it is often unclear whether a particular microbial profile causes a disease or merely accompanies it. Some products have been marketed with claims that far outstrip the data. The most reliable advice, according to specialists, remains unglamorous: eat a varied diet rich in fibre and avoid unnecessary antibiotics.`,statements:[{text:`The gut microbiome has been linked to a range of health conditions.`,answer:!0},{text:`Most of the evidence comes from large human trials.`,answer:!1},{text:`Specialists recommend an expensive personalised diet.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-238`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the practical challenges "are manageable" and the harder task is a social life, friendships form through "repeated, unplanned contact", and a regular class is "more effective than attending occasional events".`,passage:`Moving to a new city as an adult is rarely as simple as it appears in the brochures. The practical challenges, such as finding accommodation and registering with doctors, are manageable. The harder task is building a social life from nothing. Newcomers often discover that people in established cities already have full lives and little spare time for strangers. Sociologists suggest that friendships tend to form through repeated, unplanned contact, which is why joining a regular class, club or team is more effective than attending occasional events. Patience is essential: studies suggest that it can take around two hundred hours of shared time to turn an acquaintance into a close friend.`,statements:[{text:`The practical challenges of moving are described as the hardest part.`,answer:!1},{text:`Friendships often form through repeated unplanned contact.`,answer:!0},{text:`Joining a regular club is more effective than attending occasional events.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-239`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says people "addressed one another formally, even in intimate correspondence", informality "has its advantages", and without rules "a message can be misunderstood simply because the writer chose the wrong tone".`,passage:`Anyone who has read old letters will have been struck by how formally people once addressed one another, even in intimate correspondence. Husbands and wives signed themselves with full names, and friends adopted elaborate courtesies that now seem stiff. Modern informality has its advantages: it is quicker and arguably more honest. But something may also have been lost. The conventions of formal address provided a framework in which people could express affection or disagreement without risking offence, a kind of shared code that guided the unsure. Today, the absence of such rules means that a message can be misunderstood simply because the writer chose the wrong tone.`,statements:[{text:`Old letters were often formal even between intimates.`,answer:!0},{text:`The writer believes informality has no advantages.`,answer:!1},{text:`The absence of formal rules can lead to misunderstanding.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-240`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says "twice in three years", moving is "primarily an emotional upheaval", and "it was several months before I stopped reaching for light switches".`,passage:`I used to think that moving house was a purely logistical matter, a question of boxes, vans and forwarding addresses. Having recently done it twice in three years, I now believe that it is primarily an emotional upheaval. Each object I packed seemed to carry a memory I had forgotten, and deciding what to keep felt like a verdict on my past self. In the new house, familiar items looked strangely unfamiliar, as though they had lost their meaning along with their location. It was several months before I stopped reaching for light switches that were no longer where my hand expected them to be. Gradually, however, the place acquired the layers of habit that turn a building into a home.`,statements:[{text:`The writer has moved house twice in recent years.`,answer:!0},{text:`The writer now believes moving is mainly a practical task.`,answer:!1},{text:`The writer took months to adjust to the new house.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-241`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says it was "popularised in the 1990s", critics say it is "loosely defined", and skilled people "can use that skill to manipulate others".`,passage:`The concept of "emotional intelligence", popularised in the 1990s, holds that the ability to recognise and manage emotions, one's own and others', is as important as intellectual ability. Employers have embraced the idea, and training courses in empathy and self-awareness are now common. Critics, however, point out that the concept is loosely defined and that measures of it overlap with personality traits. Moreover, some studies suggest that people who are skilled at reading emotions can use that skill to manipulate others as well as to help them. The idea remains valuable as a reminder that technical competence is not enough, but claims made on its behalf should be treated with caution.`,statements:[{text:`Emotional intelligence became popular in the 1990s.`,answer:!0},{text:`Critics say the concept is precisely and narrowly defined.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some people may use emotional skills to manipulate others.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-242`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says visitors "may land only at designated sites", animals "show little fear of humans", and "introduced species... remain the greatest threats".`,passage:`The Galápagos Islands, famous for inspiring Darwin's ideas about evolution, are strictly protected. Visitors may land only at designated sites, must be accompanied by a licensed guide and are forbidden to touch or feed the animals. The strict rules have helped to preserve the unique wildlife, including giant tortoises and marine iguanas that show little fear of humans. Tourism, however, is growing, and the permanent population has increased with it, bringing demands for water and energy. Conservationists warn that introduced species and the pressure of development remain the greatest threats.`,statements:[{text:`Visitors may land anywhere on the islands.`,answer:!1},{text:`The animals show little fear of humans.`,answer:!0},{text:`Conservationists see introduced species as a threat.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-243`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says audiences are "more sympathetic than speakers imagine", "a degree of adrenaline can sharpen performance", and "few claim that it vanishes entirely".`,passage:`Many people dread public speaking more than almost anything else, yet the fear is often out of proportion to the risk. Audiences are generally more sympathetic than speakers imagine; they want the speaker to succeed and rarely notice minor errors. Experienced presenters offer consistent advice: prepare thoroughly, practise aloud, and focus on the message rather than on oneself. Some also recommend arriving early to become familiar with the room. Nervousness itself need not be eliminated, since a degree of adrenaline can sharpen performance; the aim is to channel it. Those who give regular talks say that the fear diminishes with experience, though few claim that it vanishes entirely.`,statements:[{text:`Audiences are generally hostile to speakers.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some nervousness can sharpen a performance.`,answer:!0},{text:`Experienced speakers say the fear vanishes completely.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-244`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says "the shops have changed", "a vague feeling of being a visitor", and conversations "tended to circle around the past".`,passage:`Few experiences are as disorienting as returning to one's home town after a long absence. The streets are familiar, yet the shops have changed, the people look older, and the sense of belonging that once felt effortless has been replaced by a vague feeling of being a visitor. I recently spent a week in the town where I grew up and was struck by how much of my memory consisted of things that no longer existed: a cinema now converted into a supermarket, a café that had become a bank. Old friends welcomed me warmly, but our conversations tended to circle around the past, as if we were unsure what else we had in common.`,statements:[{text:`The writer found their home town unchanged.`,answer:!1},{text:`The writer felt less at home than they used to.`,answer:!0},{text:`The writer's conversations with old friends focused mainly on the present.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-245`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the canal "reduced the sea journey", it "was enlarged in 2016", and "thousands of workers died of disease".`,passage:`The Panama Canal, opened in 1914, reduced the sea journey between the Atlantic and Pacific oceans by thousands of kilometres. Ships are raised and lowered in huge locks, a process that takes about a day to complete the passage. Construction was costly in both money and lives, as thousands of workers died of disease before measures against mosquitoes were introduced. The canal was enlarged in 2016 to accommodate bigger vessels. Visitors can watch ships pass through the locks from viewing platforms, a spectacle that attracts many tourists to the region.`,statements:[{text:`The canal shortened the sea route between the two oceans.`,answer:!0},{text:`The canal was enlarged in 2016.`,answer:!0},{text:`Construction caused few deaths.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c1-tf-246`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the profession is "often thought to be on the verge of extinction" but the picture is "more nuanced", tools "stumble over humour, ambiguity and allusion", and "demand has grown for post-editors".`,passage:`The profession of translator is often thought to be on the verge of extinction, threatened by machines that can render text from one language into another in seconds. Yet the experience of many translators suggests a more nuanced picture. Automatic tools perform well on straightforward material, such as technical manuals, but they stumble over humour, ambiguity and allusion, and they cannot judge what is appropriate for a particular audience. As a result, demand has grown for post-editors, who revise machine output, and for specialists in sensitive fields such as law and medicine, where errors can have grave consequences. Translators also stress that their work involves cultural mediation, an art that depends on understanding not only words but the people who use them.`,statements:[{text:`Translators are generally considered to be in no danger from machines.`,answer:!1},{text:`Machines struggle with humour and ambiguity.`,answer:!0},{text:`Demand has grown for post-editors.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-247`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the writer's holidays had "a rigid itinerary", the Welsh week was "rainy" with nothing open, and "I now deliberately leave several days... unplanned".`,passage:`I have never been able to relax on holiday. My idea of a break used to involve a rigid itinerary: museums in the morning, a famous restaurant at lunchtime, and a guided tour in the afternoon, with little room for spontaneity. It took a rainy week in a small Welsh village, where nothing was open and the only available entertainment was a pile of second-hand novels, to teach me otherwise. By the third day I had stopped checking my watch. I read, walked in the drizzle and talked to the landlady about her sheep. When I returned home, colleagues commented that I looked more rested than after any of my meticulously planned trips. I now deliberately leave several days of every holiday unplanned.`,statements:[{text:`The writer used to plan holidays in great detail.`,answer:!0},{text:`The writer's week in Wales was sunny and busy.`,answer:!1},{text:`The writer now leaves some holiday days unplanned on purpose.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-248`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says whistle-blowers "pay a high price", "enforcement is uneven", and responding "is in the organisation's own interest".`,passage:`Whistle-blowers, employees who report wrongdoing within their organisations, often pay a high price for doing so. Many lose their jobs, find it difficult to obtain new ones and suffer stress. Laws in many countries now protect them from dismissal, but enforcement is uneven. Organisations that value honesty create safe channels for reporting problems and respond seriously to complaints. Experts argue that this is in the organisation's own interest, since problems that are ignored tend to grow, and public scandal damages reputation far more than an issue dealt with promptly.`,statements:[{text:`Whistle-blowers often pay a high price.`,answer:!0},{text:`Laws protecting them are enforced uniformly everywhere.`,answer:!1},{text:`Experts say responding to complaints is in the organisation's interest.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-249`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says diseases "have become rare", "simply presenting facts is rarely enough", and effective approaches include "enlisting trusted local figures".`,passage:`The success of vaccination campaigns has had an ironic consequence: because diseases such as measles have become rare, many parents have never seen their effects and tend to overestimate the risks of the vaccine while underestimating those of the illness. Misinformation spreads quickly, particularly on social media, where emotionally compelling stories travel further than statistical evidence. Public health authorities have found that simply presenting facts is rarely enough to change minds. More effective approaches involve listening to parents' concerns, enlisting trusted local figures and explaining how vaccination protects not only individual children but also vulnerable members of the community who cannot be immunised. Even so, the challenge remains considerable, since beliefs about health are bound up with identity and trust.`,statements:[{text:`Vaccination has made some diseases rare.`,answer:!0},{text:`Presenting facts alone is usually enough to change minds.`,answer:!1},{text:`Trusted local figures can help in persuading parents.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c1-tf-250`,type:`true-false`,level:`C1`,topic:`reading`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says flexibility is "genuinely valuable" for some, main-income workers have "no sick pay or pension contribution", and platforms "resist".`,passage:`The gig economy is often presented as a story of liberation: workers choose when and where to work, free from the constraints of a conventional employer. For some, notably students and those seeking supplementary income, this flexibility is genuinely valuable. But for the many who depend on platform work as their main source of income, the picture is bleaker. Earnings fluctuate unpredictably, there is no sick pay or pension contribution, and algorithms can deactivate accounts with little explanation. Regulators in several countries have begun to examine whether such workers should be classified as employees, a change that would entitle them to a minimum wage and holiday pay. Platforms resist, warning that higher costs would force them to reduce the number of jobs.`,statements:[{text:`Flexibility is of no value to anyone in the gig economy.`,answer:!1},{text:`Platform workers who rely on it for their main income often lack sick pay.`,answer:!0},{text:`Platforms welcome the reclassification of workers as employees.`,answer:!1}]}]};export{e as default};